window.HUB_SEARCH=[{"id": "pmm/getting-started/what-pmms-do", "text": "Ask a few people what a product marketer does and you'll hear \"launches,\" \"sales decks\" or \"messaging.\" None of them is wrong. Each is only one piece of the job. Product marketing is one of the harder tech jobs to pin down, because it shifts with the company and the product. That matters the moment you walk into an interview, since many open with some version of \"what does a PMM do?\" You want a clear, specific answer ready before anything else. The job in two sentences Product marketing decides who a product is for, how the market should understand it, and what it takes for those people to adopt it. The product manager, by contrast, decides what gets built. As the product marketer, you make sure the right people understand why it matters, and you drive adoption. That takes far more than managing a launch. Four jobs hiding in one title Those two sentences cover a lot of ground. Martina Lauchengco, author of LOVED (an SVPG book on tech marketing), breaks product marketing into four fundamentals: Job What it looks like day to day Ambassador Connects customer and market insight, and carries it back into the company Strategist Directs the product's go-to-market Storyteller Shapes how buyers and the market understand the product, through positioning and messaging Evangelist Enables others, such as press, pundits and superfans, to tell the story Look at who actually does the work in each row. Most of the four depend on teams the PMM doesn't run, which is why learning to win people over without authority is the job. If four roles feel abstract, try four activities instead. RocketBlocks' free guide to the role, by former Google and PayPal product marketer Anna Wiggins, groups the work into market and customer research, positioning and messaging, launch, and driving adoption after launch. What PMMs say they own Definitions tell you what the job should be. Surveys tell you what people actually get handed. The Product Marketing Alliance (PMA) surveys product marketers every year, and in its 2025 data, this is the share of PMMs who own each area: Area Share of PMMs who own it Positioning and messaging 91% Launches 80.9% Sales enablement 78.7% Customer and market research 69.7% Competitive intelligence 65.2% Website 65.2% Content 48.3% Onboarding 37.5% Roadmap input 28.1% Read these as shares of people, not hours. Roadmap input lands at the bottom because PMMs feed insight into the roadmap, but PMs decide it. The row to watch is onboarding. PMA reports that the share of PMMs who own it nearly doubled from the year before. Job postings point the same way: Notion's Lead PMM role includes driving adoption of key features after launch. Play video: What is product marketing? The role, strategy and impact explained What is product marketing? The role, strategy and impact explained Product Marketing Alliance, Jan 2024 · 10 min · YouTube PMM vs. PM vs. brand manager: three different questions The three titles sound alike. The fastest way to tell them apart is the question each one is trying to answer: Product manager Product marketing manager Brand manager Core question What should we build, and why? Who is it for, how do we explain it, and are they adopting it? What does this brand mean to people? Owns Strategy, roadmap, prioritized features Positioning, launches, sales enablement, market insight Brand identity and consistency Often measured on Speed to market, roadmap delivery, customer satisfaction Market growth, customer acquisition cost, lifetime value Awareness, perception, loyalty, brand equity Sources: Pragmatic Institute , SVPG , BU Career Development . In PMA's 2025 survey, 53.2% of product marketers named revenue as one of their KPIs. The line between PM and PMM blurs most at launch, and some companies have PMs with no PMMs at all. Pragmatic Institute draws the line this way. PMs focus on building the product, prioritizing features and managing the roadmap. PMMs face the market: they talk with customers and prospects, track trends and write the go-to-market plan, positioning and messaging. How closely the two work together depends on the size of the company. Be careful with the brand title if you interview outside tech. In consumer packaged goods, \"brand manager\" usually means something bigger: the person who runs a brand as a business, P&L included. Play video: Product manager vs. product marketing manager: which career is right for you? Product manager vs. product marketing manager: which career is right for you? Product Marketing Alliance, Jul 2024 · 5 min · YouTube The job in action: a waitlist for fitness studios An illustration, not a real company. The numbers are made up to show the work. You're the product marketer at a booking app used by about 2,000 independent fitness studios. The product team has just built a waitlist: when someone cancels a class, the next person on the list gets the spot and is charged automatically. Now watch the four activities play out in order. 1. Research the customers and the market. Start with your own data. Which studios have the most last-minute cancellations, and which classes sell out? The answer is boutique studios with small classes of 15 spots or fewer. Next, you interview eight of their owners. Today they handle cancellations by texting a list of regulars by hand, or by overbooking and hoping. Both cost them money, because an empty spot in a sold-out class is revenue they never get back. 2. Position and message. Your target is boutique studios with small, full classes. Their real alternative isn't another app. It's the hand-texted list. So the difference to stress is that the spot fills itself and the new client pays without the owner lifting a finger. The message: \"Every canceled spot, filled and paid for.\" The proof: a pilot studio that filled most of its late cancellations in the first month. 3. Launch. This is a feature for existing customers, so it gets a mid-size launch, not a big public one. You write an in-app announcement and an email to owners"}, {"id": "pmm/getting-started/b2b", "text": "A software deal has gone well for three months. The team on the free plan loves the product, and their manager wants to buy. Then the security team sends questions nobody prepared for, finance asks for the return math, and the deal sits still for weeks. Nothing was wrong with the product. It just had more people to convince than anyone planned for. Most tech PMM roles are B2B: the customer is a company, and the person who signs is rarely the only one deciding. A single deal can involve a group of people with different worries, take months, and pass through a website, a free plan and a salesperson before anyone pays. Your job is to make sure each of those people, at each of those steps, finds a reason to say yes. Three questions organize the work: who buys, how they buy, and what each of them needs to see. Who's actually buying? Start with company size, because a 20-person firm and a 20,000-person firm buy software in very different ways. There's no single cutoff. The US Small Business Administration sets \"small\" by industry, using either employee counts or revenue. Most manufacturers count as small at 500 employees or fewer, for example. The EU uses different numbers: small means under 50 staff, and medium-sized means under 250. Tech companies set their own tiers, usually some version of small business, mid-market and enterprise. So before you answer a case, ask what the company means by each. The buyer is a group. Gartner puts B2B buying groups at five to 16 people from as many as four functions. Forrester's 2026 survey counts 13 internal stakeholders in a typical decision, plus outside influencers. Don't picture a room full of spreadsheets, either. Research from Bain finds that personal and emotional factors weigh more and more in B2B buying. Here's who tends to sit in a software buying group, and what each one needs from you: Member What they worry about What the PMM gives them The executive who signs Return, risk, priorities A business case and proof from similar companies Daily users Whether it makes their work easier A demo, a trial, clear onboarding IT and security Integration, data protection, compliance Security and integration documentation Finance and procurement Cost and contract terms Clear pricing and packaging, return math The champion Whether they can sell it internally A short internal pitch they can forward How do they buy? B2B companies sell in one of three ways, and the way they sell decides where your words end up. Self-serve, or product-led. The product does the selling: it finds customers and turns them into paying ones, with a free or low-cost way in and little human sales contact. More and more buyers want exactly that. In Gartner's survey of 646 B2B buyers in 2025, 67% said they prefer a buying experience without a sales rep, and 45% had used AI during a recent purchase. With no seller in the room, the website, the pricing page, the free plan and the help docs carry your message. Sales-led. A sales team runs the deal, usually for larger contracts. Your work reaches buyers secondhand, through what the sellers say and send. Both. McKinsey found that most public B2B companies that started out purely product-led have since added their own sales teams. Sales turns trial and free users into larger deals, while marketing and product nurture those users together. That's why most B2B PMMs end up writing for both motions at once. What you build for each step Follow a deal from the first website visit to renewal. Every stop has its own audience, its own thing for you to build and its own sign that it's working: Touchpoint Who it's for What the PMM builds Signal it's working Website and pricing page Buyers researching on their own Positioning, plan comparison, proof Sign-ups, trial starts, demo requests Free plan or trial Daily users Onboarding emails, in-product tips Activation, trial-to-paid conversion Sales conversations The buying group Pitch deck, demo script, battlecards, objection handling Win rate, deal cycle length Security and procurement review IT, security, finance Security overview, pricing FAQ, return calculator Fewer deals stalling at review After the sale Admins and users Adoption campaigns, release notes, customer stories Expansion and renewal The sales row alone covers a lot. Pragmatic Institute lists case studies, presentation decks and one-pagers among the enablement materials PMMs create, along with information about target buyers and users and key messaging points for sales. A Salesforce posting for a sales enablement PMM goes further, adding battlecards, objection handling, quarterly sales plays, and onboarding and training programs. Late reviews are a common place for deals to slow down. Forrester predicts that in 2027, risk reviews will delay or block a third of enterprise purchases of AI software, as legal, security and compliance teams ask vendors to prove their governance. It's a forecast, not a measured number. Still, it explains why security documentation has landed on a PMM's list. Worked example: Atlassian starts low-touch and adds sales later Atlassian, the maker of Jira and Confluence, shows both motions inside one company. It grew on what its annual report calls a \"high-velocity, low-friction distribution model.\" Its website is the main sales channel, customers can buy online with a credit card, prices are public, there's a free tier, and it says it doesn't rely on a traditional sales force until customers reach a certain size. As customers grew, a second motion joined the first. A direct sales team now focuses on large existing customers, adding teams, products and higher editions, and channel partners drove more than half of its fiscal 2025 revenue. The filing also warns that enterprise sales cycles are longer and cost more. By June 30, 2026, 57,334 Atlassian customers paid more than $10,000 a year for its cloud products, up from 51,978 a year earlier, and channel partners still drove more than half of its revenue. If you were the PMM th"}, {"id": "pmm/getting-started/b2c", "text": "Someone on a bus is thumbing through an app store page. They glance at the screenshots, skim a review or two and decide whether to download before the next stop. Nobody from your company is there to explain anything. That's the world of consumer product marketing. Instead of a buying group that takes months, you're reaching millions of people who each decide alone, often in a few seconds on an app store page or an upgrade screen. There's usually no sales team to explain the product, so the product itself, its store page and the messages inside the app have to do that work. The thinking carries over from B2B. The tools and the pace don't. How one person decides Even a fast decision has steps. OpenStax's Principles of Marketing breaks the consumer decision into five stages: need recognition, information search, evaluation of alternatives, the purchase decision and post-purchase evaluation. What changes from business buying is the crowd and the clock. There are far more buyers, each of whom decides alone or with a household, and decisions take less time. In a consumer app, every stage happens somewhere specific, and that's where your work shows up: Stage Where it happens in a consumer app What the PMM shapes Need recognition A friend's post, an ad, a moment of frustration Campaigns with brand and social teams, and the story people share Information search The app store page, reviews, the website Store screenshots and copy, and what press and reviewers hear at launch Evaluation of alternatives A free tier or a trial Onboarding that gets people to a first win fast Purchase decision The upgrade screen Which plan to show, what it includes and how it's worded Post-purchase evaluation The first weeks as a paying user Lifecycle emails and in-app messages that keep people using what they paid for Don't skim past the last row. Many consumer subscriptions can be canceled any month, so your customer makes the purchase decision again every billing cycle, and annual plans move that decision to renewal time. What's on a consumer PMM's plate Read a real job description and the difference jumps out. Duolingo's posting for a Duolingo Math PMM is a good snapshot: plans for acquisition, engagement and retention, launch go-to-market, consumer research, and campaigns across in-app messages, app store pages and email. Notice what's missing: no pitch deck, no battlecard. When your audience is millions of individuals, you work through channels that reach them at scale, and your closest partners are the growth, lifecycle, brand and PR teams. Scale comes with a perk: testing gets cheap. Apple's product page optimization lets an app test up to three alternate versions of its App Store page against the original, changing the icon, screenshots or preview videos, for up to 90 days. It then reports which version converts better and how confident the result is. A B2B PMM rarely gets that clean a read on a message. A consumer PMM can get one in weeks. The numbers you'll be judged on Downloads and sign-ups feel like wins, but they only show the top of the funnel. A consumer PMM is judged on what happens after them, using standard consumer subscription metrics like these: Metric What it tells you Monthly active users (MAU) How many people use the product at all Daily active users (DAU) How many have made it a habit Conversion to paid What share of users decide it's worth paying for Churn How many paying users leave each month Retention by cohort Whether people who joined in a given month are still around later Worked example: Duolingo keeps the lessons free Duolingo gives its lessons away on purpose. Free learners see an ad after each lesson, and paid tiers remove the ads and add features. In 2025, paid subscribers were 9.2% of average monthly users, and the company says its growth comes mainly from word of mouth and brand buzz, with selective paid marketing. Its Q3 2026 shareholder letter brought a change to the lineup. Along with the numbers below, it said Duolingo would stop selling its AI tier, Max, and fold its Video Call feature into the Super plan. Max had launched in March 2023 as a tier above Super, built around AI features such as Explain My Answer and Roleplay, with Video Call added later. Q3 2026 Duolingo Monthly active users 153.1 million Daily active users 64.9 million Paid subscribers 13.1 million Divide DAU by MAU and you get a rough habit score: about 42% that quarter. If you were the PMM, three things would follow: The free tier is the acquisition engine. Messages to free learners aim at habit and sharing, because a learner who keeps a streak and tells a friend brings in more users than an ad does. The upgrade happens in a few moments. That's where the PMM has the most say: the upgrade screen, trial offers and the prompts that appear when a free learner wants something the free tier doesn't have. Tiers need clear jobs. An AI feature that once justified a premium tier is moving into the main paid plan. You can watch it turn into table stakes, right on the price list. B2B and consumer, side by side B2B, sales-led Consumer Who decides A buying group One person, or a household How long it takes Weeks to months Seconds to days What the message leans on Business value, risk and proof Everyday benefit, habit, what friends use Where the message lives Sales conversations, decks, demos, the website The store page, the product, social and email Main PMM outputs Enablement, persona messaging, customer proof Lifecycle and in-app messages, store pages, campaigns, tests Typical metrics Pipeline, win rate, deal size Active users, conversion to paid, churn Real companies are messier than any table. A consumer app that adds a team plan picks up B2B work, and a B2B tool with a free plan picks up consumer-style work. Common traps Assuming consumer PMM means advertising. Postings like Duolingo's lean on lifecycle, onboarding and in-app messages, often inside the product itself. Counting downloads as success. A download that nev"}, {"id": "pmm/getting-started/partners", "text": "Make a list of what a product marketer actually controls and it's short. The product belongs to product management, the pipeline to sales and most channels to other marketing teams. Almost everything you ship depends on someone else saying yes. So the job starts with knowing whose yes you need, and what each of those people needs from you. You sit at the hub of the wheel PMMs work between a lot of teams. In PMA's 2025 survey, 88.8% said they work closely with product and 81% with marketing. Bessemer's guide to building the function adds more spokes: customers, sales, engineering and analysts. It also says PMM lines up with demand generation, brand, field marketing and communications. Real postings read the same way. Duolingo's posting for a Product Marketing Lead names Product, Brand, Performance marketing, Comms, Business Development and Analytics as partners. Play video: Day in the life of a product marketing manager, with Holly Watson Day in the life of a product marketing manager, with Holly Watson RocketBlocks, Nov 2021 · 6 min · YouTube What each partner needs, and what you need back Every one of these relationships is a trade. You give each team something it can use, and you need something back: Partner What they need from you What you need from them Product (PM, design, engineering) Customer and market insight, positioning, the launch plan Roadmap visibility, early access, honest scope and dates Sales A pitch that works, battlecards, objection handling, training What's happening in deals: wins, losses, objections Demand generation and performance Messaging, audiences, offers Channel and campaign results Brand and creative Positioning and key messages A consistent look and voice Customer success and support Release notes, onboarding content, FAQs Adoption problems, churn reasons, feature requests Analytics The questions and the goals Dashboards and experiment reads Comms, analysts and partners The narrative and launch moments Coverage, outside credibility, reach The table is a summary built from the sources above and from 2026 job postings. One row comes with a caveat. Analysts come up in Bessemer's guide, and Lauchengco's evangelist job covers press and other outside voices, but analysts rarely show up as a full job of their own in postings. How to get a yes from people who don't report to you None of these teams reports to you. You can't assign sales a task or tell a PM what to build, so influence is how anything gets done. Lauchengco's advice is to bring product marketers into product planning and have them talk with their PMs often, because what ships and when inevitably changes. Beyond that, three habits help: Trade, don't ask. Bring sales a win/loss insight before asking them to use your new deck. Show your evidence. \"Six of the last ten lost deals cited setup time\" moves a PM more than \"customers want it easier.\" Close the loop. Tell people what happened with their input, or they stop giving it. Common traps Talking only to the PM. Sales and support often hear about the market's problems before product does, because they talk to customers every day. Shipping enablement nobody asked for. Ask sales what's blocking deals before you build anything. Forgetting customer success. They own the customers who renew, and they know why people leave. In an interview Expect cross-functional questions such as walking through a project and your part in it, or describing a time you influenced another leader's decision. Pick stories where you changed someone's mind with evidence. Then say what you gave them in return, because that trade is how PMMs get things done without authority. Play video: How to get people to listen to you How to get people to listen to you Harvard Business Review, Apr 2024 · 10 min · YouTube Self-check: Your PM won't share the roadmap until it's final, and launch is six weeks out. What do you offer to get in earlier? Something the PM can use right away: the top objections sales hears, recent win/loss findings, or a draft of how you'd position the feature. Trading insight first gives the PM a reason to bring you in early."}, {"id": "pmm/getting-started/interviews", "text": "A PMM interview loop is the job in miniature. In one loop you may position a product, plan a launch, explain a metric and answer behavioral questions about your past work. That sounds like a lot to prepare for. The good news is that the question types are predictable, so you can walk in with a story or an approach ready for each one. The questions you'll hear PMA's interview guide splits questions into behavioral and role-specific ones, and its examples cover the types below. It also suggests preparing by studying the job description, using the product, reading its reviews and putting numbers on your results: Behavioral questions about past work Walking through a launch you ran Launch and metrics cases (for example: how would you launch X, and how would you know it worked?) Funnel diagnosis (for example: why are paying users dropping off?) Adapting the marketing mix for B2B vs. consumer Cross-functional questions The same guide makes a point to carry into every answer: \"the launch itself is not the end goal.\" Other questions worth preparing: walking through your last launch, how you'd measure product marketing, what you think of the company's messaging, and what you'd do if a new feature had low adoption. Francisco Bram, who led product marketing for several Uber businesses, comes at it from the skills side. He lists five hard skills these interviews test: market sizing, segmentation, narrative design, go-to-market strategy and measurement. What a real loop looks like: Google Loops vary by company and level, so treat this as one example. Google says the same basics apply whether you're applying for an engineering job or a marketing one. Its process typically takes about 6 to 8 weeks. You may take an online assessment, then have one or two short recruiter conversations. Depending on the role, you may be asked to do a small project, such as preparing a case study, before a panel of structured interviews with open-ended, role-related questions and no brainteasers. Guides go out of date, so check any company's current process with your recruiter. Play video: Onsite product marketing mock interview Onsite product marketing mock interview Henry Wang, ex-Google PMM, Aug 2024 · 31 min · starts at 0:35 · YouTube When they ask for a 90-day plan Many PMM loops end with a presentation, and a 90-day plan is a common one. It's an odd assignment: you're planning a job you haven't started, at a company you've only seen from the outside. Two pieces of guidance help: Start with learning. Michael Watkins, author of The First 90 Days , says to be systematic about deciding what you need to learn, and to set goals for the end of the first month, the second month and day 90, with a few early wins. Build it around their definition of success. Early in the process, ask the hiring manager how they define success in the role, then keep the plan high level. A typical one runs 3 to 4 pages. Play video: How to survive your first 90 days as a PMM How to survive your first 90 days as a PMM Product Marketing Alliance, Sep 2026 · 7 min · YouTube What interviewers listen for Google's interview advice is to expect two kinds of questions: behavioral ones about past situations, and hypothetical ones about how you would approach a problem. Back up your impact with data, and structure answers with STAR. Chicago Booth's guide to PM and PMM cases says interviewers judge your thought process, not just your answer, and one student found that pinning down a clear target customer made the rest of a case easier. Play video: How to prepare for Google's non-technical interview questions How to prepare for Google's non-technical interview questions Life at Google, Feb 2025 · 3 min · YouTube A prep plan for each question type The fastest prep is one concrete rep for each question type, built on products you already use: Question type How to prepare \"What is product marketing?\" A two-sentence definition, plus one real launch you can explain from start to finish Customer research, segments and personas Walk through how you'd find the target customer for a product you use Positioning and messaging Write a positioning for a product you like, starting from its real alternatives Launch and go-to-market cases Practice structuring a launch: target, message, motion, readiness, goals Pricing and packaging Explain how a product you pay for decides what's free and what costs more Enablement and competitive questions Write a one-page battlecard against a real rival Growth and campaign ideas Pick a product and say which stage of its funnel you'd fix first Market sizing, metrics and funnel diagnosis Practice one estimate and one \"this metric dropped, why?\" question out loud Behavioral and cross-functional stories Prepare four or five STAR stories, each with a number in the result Self-check: Your PMM onsite is one week away. What three things do you do first? Use the product and read its reviews, so your examples are real. Write the company's positioning in your own words and check it against their homepage. Then drill one launch case and one funnel diagnosis out loud, with a timer."}, {"id": "pmm/getting-started/career-path", "text": "Don't trust a product marketing title. Titles don't travel well between companies, and a posting called \"associate\" at one company can ask for more experience than a plain \"PMM\" at another. The title alone won't tell you how senior a job is. Four other things will: the scope it owns, whether it manages people, the years it asks for and, more and more often, the pay range printed right on it. Real postings show all four, which makes them the best map of the ladder you'll find. How people actually get in Few people start their careers as PMMs. Even entry-level postings usually ask for a few years of related work. Perplexity's associate PMM role asks for two to three years in product marketing, customer education, business development or similar work. The Bureau of Labor Statistics says marketing managers typically need a bachelor's degree, often in business, marketing or communications, plus experience in a related marketing, promotions or sales job. So where do PMMs come from? The postings also show which backgrounds count. Robinhood's senior PMM role asks for experience in demand generation or growth marketing, and Perplexity's accepts customer education. Teams that already work next to PMMs are a natural source of new ones, because their people know the product and the customer. For MBA students, the summer internship is the usual door. An Intuit posting gives a good picture of what one looks like. Its summer 2027 MBA product marketing internship runs 12 weeks. Interns help develop the ideal customer profile for its lending products, research customers and competitors, size segments and present to senior leaders. The posting lists pay of $29.50 to $56.50 an hour, though another line on the same page says $40 to $53. New-grad rotational programs in product marketing exist but are rare. Play video: So, you want to become a product marketing manager? So, you want to become a product marketing manager? Product Marketing Alliance, Mar 2024 · 16 min · starts at 2:13 · YouTube The ladder, read from real postings Here's the ladder from associate to VP, with one real US posting from 2026 for each rung and the base pay range it lists: Level Posting Experience asked What it owns Manages people? Posted base pay Associate PMM Perplexity, San Francisco (Jul 2026) 2 to 3 years Product education, webinars, launch coordination No $135,000 to $180,000 PMM Mercury, banking and payments (Nov 2026) 2 to 3 years in B2B PMM Whole launches, positioning from customer research, adoption No $128,600 to $160,700, by location Senior PMM Datadog, New York (Sep 2026) 5+ years in enterprise B2B Go-to-market for part of the product line, sales enablement, case studies Not stated $154,000 to $205,000 Staff PMM Okta, device access (Aug 2026) 8+ years Go-to-market and lifecycle strategy for a product, launch planning, field enablement Not stated $147,000 to $226,000, by location Director Zocdoc, patient products (Sep 2026) 12 to 15+ years, 8+ in PMM Go-to-market and positioning for a consumer portfolio, and coaching a PMM team Yes $210,000 to $270,000 VP Celigo (Nov 2026) 12+ years, 6+ leading teams Positioning, launches, enablement, analyst strategy, pricing input, budget and team design Yes, including managers $240,000 to $275,000 Read down the table and three things change with each step: Scope widens: from pieces of a launch, to a whole product, to a portfolio, to the whole function. In these postings, the first role that clearly manages people is director. The senior and staff postings don't say they manage anyone, though some companies do have PMM managers below director. Pay tends to rise, but it overlaps a lot. Perplexity's associate role pays more than Mercury's PMM role, and some postings, like Okta's and Mercury's, list different ranges by location. Now the catch: titles still don't line up. DoorDash's SevenRooms team posted an \"Associate Manager, Product Marketing\" role asking for three to six or more years of B2B product marketing, at $115,600 to $170,000. That's more experience than Mercury asks of a PMM. Robinhood's senior PMM, launching a new premium wealth offering in a B2B-hybrid role, asks for six or more years and lists $166,000 to $195,000 in its top pay zone, which includes New York. For a wider view, the Bureau of Labor Statistics puts median pay for US marketing managers at $166,790 a year as of May 2025, and projects 7% job growth from 2025 to 2035. Why the pay is printed right there Pay ranges show up because several US states now require them. Washington has required employers with 15 or more employees to include a wage scale or salary range in job postings since January 1, 2023, and New York requires employers with four or more employees to list a compensation range. That's good news for you as a candidate. The posting is now a solid first source on base pay, though it leaves out equity and bonuses. What a normal week looks like Titles and pay tell you where a job sits on the ladder. They don't tell you what Tuesday feels like. Here's a sample week for a mid-level B2B PMM a few weeks before a launch, pieced together from the responsibilities in the postings above: Day What fills it Monday Launch check-in with product, sales and lifecycle marketing, then final edits to the messaging doc Tuesday Two customer interviews and a short write-up of patterns for the product manager Wednesday Sitting in on sales calls and updating the objection-handling notes Thursday Training the sales team on the launch and reviewing a customer case study draft Friday Checking adoption numbers from last month's launch and planning the next test A consumer PMM's week swaps the sales calls and training for in-app message tests, app store updates and lifecycle campaigns. Common traps Comparing titles instead of scope. An \"associate manager\" at one company can be more senior than a \"manager\" at another. Reading the top of the range as the offer. Ranges often cover several cities and a spread of experience, so your offer de"}, {"id": "pmm/getting-started/figma-buzz", "text": "The launch. At its Config conference on May 7, 2025, Figma announced Figma Buzz, designed for brand and marketing teams. That's a different user from the designers Figma is best known for. Figma launched Buzz in open beta, with templates, brand components, AI image tools and bulk asset creation. Your task, in about 150 words: Name one launch decision the PM likely owned, one the PMM owned, and one the brand team owned. Which internal team did the PMM need to win over first, and why? You don't know how Figma actually split the work, so reason from the roles. +++ reveal Sample answer A sample answer, not a description of how Figma actually split the work. PM: what ships in the open beta, such as whether AI image tools come on day one. PMM: who Buzz is for first and how to frame it. A strong pick is marketers at companies that already pay for Figma, because designers there can set up the templates once and marketers can then make on-brand assets without waiting on design. That becomes the message. The PMM also sets an adoption goal, such as the share of customer teams that create a Buzz file in 30 days. Brand: how Buzz looks and sounds as part of Figma. Win over first: the account teams for existing Figma customers. Buzz spreads fastest inside companies already on Figma, and those teams own the relationships. If they can't pitch it to marketers, it never reaches the buyer, however good the product is. Why it works It keeps the three roles distinct: the PM owns scope, the PMM owns audience and message, and brand owns identity. It picks one segment instead of \"all marketers,\" and gives a reason grounded in the launch facts. It names a measurable adoption goal, because the launch isn't the end goal. What interviewers push on \"Why existing customers and not new ones?\" Be ready to argue the trade-off: existing customers convert faster, but new logos make Buzz bigger. \"What's the main competitive alternative for this marketer?\" Today it might be a template-based design tool such as Canva, or asking a designer for every asset. \"How would you know the launch worked by day 90?\" Practice twist Same launch, but Figma has no sales or account team, and every user signs up on their own. Which internal team do you win over first now?"}, {"id": "pmm/getting-started/hubspot-two-motions", "text": "The facts. Each HubSpot product line, which it calls a Hub, comes in free and paid tiers, so customers can get value before they pay. HubSpot had 288,706 customers in more than 135 countries at the end of 2025, and says it targets mid-market companies with 2 to 2,000 employees. Its sales reps work by phone, email and web meetings, and some upgrades inside the product happen with little rep involvement. Larger customers get a dedicated customer success manager, while others are served mainly through digital channels. Partners who resell and refer, and the customers they bring, account for about 25% of customers and about 49% of revenue. In fall 2025, HubSpot announced more than 200 new products and features at once, including AI agents such as a Prospecting Agent. Your task: You're a HubSpot PMM launching one new paid AI feature. It has to reach two customers: a 12-person agency on HubSpot's free tools, and a 1,500-person company on a top-tier plan with a customer success manager. For each customer, say who decides, how they'd buy, and where your message should show up. List what you'd build for each. Pick one metric for each that tells you the launch worked. +++ reveal Sample answer 12-person agency, free tools 1,500-person company, top tier Who decides The owner, often alone A buying group: the marketing lead, sales operations, IT and finance How they buy Inside the product, by upgrading Through their customer success manager, a sales rep or a partner Where the message lives An in-product prompt at the moment they need it, email, the pricing page An account review, a demo, a business case What the PMM builds An in-app announcement, a short video, upgrade page copy A pitch deck, demo script, one-page business case, and training for reps, success managers and partners Metric Upgrades from free to the paid tier that includes the feature Pipeline and win rate on deals that include it All of the table is inference from the facts above. Why it works It treats one feature as two launches, because the two customers buy in completely different ways. It doesn't forget partners, who bring in about half of HubSpot's revenue, so they need enablement too. It ties each metric to the motion: upgrades for self-serve, pipeline and win rate for sales-led. What interviewers push on \"Which motion do you prioritize?\" Whichever drives more of the feature's revenue, but with partners near half of all revenue, leaving them out would be a mistake either way. \"How do you keep the message consistent?\" One message house, framed differently for an owner who decides alone and a buying group that needs a business case. Practice twist Now the feature is free for every customer. What's the PMM's goal for each motion instead, and how do you measure it?"}, {"id": "pmm/getting-started/peloton-app-retention", "text": "The facts. In the quarter ended September 30, 2026, Peloton had 487,000 paid App subscribers, down from 542,000 a year earlier. Monthly churn among its equipment-owning subscribers rose to 1.7% from 1.6%. About three in four paid App subscribers, and nearly all equipment subscribers, pay month to month. The decline isn't new. Paid App subscribers went from 621,000 in fiscal 2024 to 552,000 in fiscal 2025 and 503,000 in fiscal 2026. The Peloton App starts with a 30-day free trial that requires a card. After that, App One covers strength, yoga, meditation, outdoor running and three cardio classes a month, while App+ adds unlimited cardio classes. Your task: You're the PMM for the Peloton App. Leadership wants to slow the loss of paid App subscribers without cutting prices. Where would you look first, and what data would you pull? Write two short messages: one for people who canceled in the last 90 days, and one for App One members who use their three cardio classes every month. How would you test the messages before sending them to everyone? +++ reveal Sample answer 1. Find out who leaves, and when. Most paid App subscribers can cancel any month, so every month is a decision. Pull cancellations by how long people had subscribed: If most leave right after the free trial, it's an onboarding problem: they never built a routine. If they leave after several months, it's a value problem: the classes stopped feeling new, or life got in the way. Each group needs a different fix, so don't write a message until you know which one is bigger. 2. Two messages: Canceled in the last 90 days: \"Ten minutes is enough to start again. Here are three short classes to get you back in.\" The ask is small on purpose, because a lapsed user's first barrier is restarting, not price. App One members who hit the cardio limit: \"You've used all three cardio classes this month. App+ gives you unlimited cardio, so your plan doesn't stop in week two.\" It shows up at the moment the limit bites. 3. Test against a holdout. Send each message to a random slice of its group, keep a similar slice that gets nothing, and compare reactivation or upgrades after 30 days. The holdout tells you whether the message caused the change or the month did. Why it works It diagnoses before it writes: when people cancel points to either onboarding or value. It uses a real product limit, three cardio classes on App One, as the upgrade moment. It measures against a holdout, so a good month isn't mistaken for a good message. What interviewers push on \"Why not just offer a discount?\" It can win people back for a month, but it teaches them to wait for discounts and doesn't fix why they left. \"How do you know it worked?\" Reactivation and 90-day retention against the holdout, not opens or clicks. Practice twist Now the goal is the other end of the funnel: more free-trial users still paying after day 30. What changes in your plan?"}, {"id": "pmm/insight/segmenting-a-market-and-picking-a-target", "text": "A sales team gets handed the target \"mid-size companies.\" Where do they start? Which industry, which city, which problem do they open with? Nobody knows, so each rep picks their own, and the message ends up saying a little to everyone and a lot to no one. Every later choice you make as a PMM, from positioning to pricing, depends on who the product is for. You can't build, position and sell a product to everyone at once, so the first job is to split the market into groups that behave alike and pick the one you'll win first. Get this wrong and even sharp positioning ends up aimed at the wrong people. Where to draw the lines Consumer markets usually get split by where people live, who they are, how they buy and what they value. Business markets have their own set of cuts, which OpenStax's Principles of Marketing lays out. Each row is a different way of looking at the same pool of companies: Basis What it divides by A software example Firmographics Industry, location, size, structure, performance Hospitals with more than 500 beds Technographics The hardware and software a company already uses Companies already running a particular CRM Needs-based The features or benefits a customer wants Teams that need audit trails for compliance Value-based How much a customer could be worth, often called tiering Accounts likely to spend $100,000 a year or more Behavioral How a customer engages with the company Free users who invited three or more teammates Who they are vs. why they'd buy Look closely at those five and you'll see two kinds. Firmographics and technographics describe who a customer is. A needs-based cut gets much closer to why they'd buy. The argument for looking past the who isn't new. Daniel Yankelovich made the case in Harvard Business Review back in 1964 for segmenting by more than demographics, and in 2006 he and David Meer argued that segmentation had narrowed into a tool for serving advertising. You need both halves. For a tech PMM, the segment worth chasing usually pairs them: A descriptive cut you can find and reach, such as industry, company size or the tools a team already uses. It's what sales builds a list from. A need that explains why they'd buy, such as a compliance deadline or hours lost to manual work. It's what your message speaks to. Compare the two. \"Mid-size accounting firms\" is a list. \"Mid-size accounting firms losing billable hours to manual data entry\" is a segment you can sell to. Choosing where to start Once you've sliced the market, you still have to decide how many slices to go after. OpenStax describes four ways to answer that: Undifferentiated: one offer for everyone. Differentiated: a separate plan for each of several segments. Concentrated, or niche: one segment, with the aim of dominating it. It suits companies with limited resources. Micromarketing: tailoring to individuals or very small groups. If you're a startup launching something new, you'll usually land on the third one. A startup can't afford to fight on several fronts at once. Geoffrey Moore's Crossing the Chasm explains why focus pays off in tech: mainstream buyers want proof that a product works for companies like theirs, so a company should pick one segment and one demanding use case, win it decisively, and become the leader in that niche. Marketers often call that first segment the beachhead. To choose between candidates, score each one on the same six questions, so you're comparing segments instead of defending a favorite: Question What a good answer looks like Is it big enough? Enough customers to build a business on, with room to grow Is the pain urgent? They're losing money or time now, not someday Can you reach them? A list, a channel or a community where they gather Can you win? Your difference matters more here than anywhere else Do they talk to each other? Word of mouth makes the next customer cheaper Where does it lead? Nearby segments you can win next Worked example: Toast bets on one industry Toast built its point-of-sale and restaurant software for exactly one kind of customer. Its 2021 IPO filing described the platform as purpose-built for restaurants and the company as \"exclusively focused on the restaurant industry\". You might expect a focus that tight to cap growth. It didn't. Toast went from 19,891 restaurant locations in June 2019 to 47,942 in June 2021, across about 29,000 customers, and still had only about 6% of roughly 860,000 US restaurant locations. Its plan for growing further was to sell more products to the same restaurants, add financial services, expand internationally and win larger restaurant groups. Both acquisitions it named, for payroll and for invoicing, were built for restaurants too. Three things to take from it: A narrow segment can still be huge. Restaurants were specific enough to speak to directly and large enough that 6% share left plenty of room. Go deeper before going wider. Toast grew by selling more to restaurants before reaching for other industries. Specific needs make the message easy. Kitchen tickets, tips and menus are restaurant problems a general-purpose system handles poorly, which gave Toast a clear difference to talk about. Common traps A segment you can't reach. If there's no list, channel or community, you can't sell to it, however good the fit. Demographic segments with no buying difference. If two groups would buy for the same reason, they're really one segment. Picking the biggest segment instead of the most winnable one. A huge market where you have no edge loses to a smaller one where you do. Never choosing. Going after several segments at once spreads a small team thin before it wins any of them. In an interview When you're asked who you'd target first, don't hedge. Name one segment, give two or three reasons from the scoring questions, and say what you'd turn down for now. The no matters as much as the yes, because it shows you understand the trade-off. Self-check: A startup selling expense software says its target is \"small and m"}, {"id": "pmm/insight/personas-and-b2b-buying-groups", "text": "Every B2B deal has a cast. Someone signs. Several people will use the product. An IT lead can stop the whole thing, and someone in finance is usually asking what it costs. Most teams sketch that cast from memory in an afternoon and call it a persona. The teams that win go find out who's really involved and what each of those people needs to hear. How far can you trust a persona? Write for \"everyone\" and nobody recognizes themselves in what you wrote. A persona fixes that by turning a group into one concrete person: a short, realistic profile of a typical customer, so the team designs and writes for someone specific. The catch is that a persona is only as good as the research behind it. Nielsen Norman Group sorts personas into three kinds on exactly that basis: Type Built from Good for Watch out for Proto-persona The team's assumptions and data already on hand, usually in a two- to four-hour workshop Getting a team aligned fast It can be wrong, and a weak one can sour people on personas Qualitative persona About 5 to 30 interviews or field studies, coded into themes Most PMM work It can't tell you what share of buyers each persona represents Statistical persona Qualitative work, then a survey of at least 100 people (ideally 500 or more), then clustering Sizing segments It's costly and needs statistical help Whichever kind you build, watch what goes into it. That same NN/g article advises against personas built only from demographics or analytics. Those details leave out what people are trying to do and why. \"38, lives in Denver, likes hiking\" might feel vivid, but it tells sales nothing about how to win the deal. The person who pays isn't the person who uses Take a VP of sales who signs for a new CRM, and the reps who'll have to log every call in it. The VP wants forecasts she can trust. The reps want fewer clicks between calls. One persona can't speak to both, because they're judging the product on different things. In B2B, the person who pays and the person who uses are often different people, so each gets a persona of their own. Pragmatic Institute describes buyer personas as profiles of the people who make or influence the purchase, such as sponsors, approvers and influencers, capturing their goals, concerns, authority and success criteria. User personas profile the people who use the product, capturing their workflows, needs, context and behavior. Pragmatic treats the two as separate kinds of persona. That matters because a buyer's approval doesn't guarantee that users adopt the product. Buyer persona User persona Who Sponsors, approvers, influencers The people who use the product every day Captures Goals, concerns, authority, success criteria Workflows, needs, context, behavior What the PMM uses it for The business case, sales messaging, pricing Onboarding, product messaging, adoption campaigns The last row is one reading of how a PMM puts each persona to work. Map the buying group from your own deals A generic list of roles gets you started. The buying group that matters, though, is the one that actually shows up in your deals, and you can map it from records you already have: Pull 10 to 20 recent deals, won and lost. List everyone who appeared: who joined calls, who signed, who asked for a security review, who went quiet. Interview a few of them, especially from lost deals, because they can tell you where things stalled. Group people by role, not title: sponsor, approver, influencer, user and possible blocker. The same title can play different roles at different companies. For each role, write down three things: the worry, the question they'll ask, and the proof that answers it. Find the person who can move the group. That last step matters more than it looks. You might assume a big deal stalls because one stakeholder says no. In The Challenger Customer , built on CEB research with thousands of B2B marketers, sellers and buyers, Brent Adamson, Matthew Dixon, Pat Spenner and Nick Toman argue that's rarely the reason. Deals stall because the stakeholders can't agree on what the problem is. The people who get deals done are what the authors call \"Mobilizers,\" who have the credibility and the will to push colleagues past the status quo. Deals reach the finish line far more often with them than with friendlier \"Talkers\" who seem so receptive at first. That changes who you build for. The contact who always takes your call isn't necessarily your champion. Your champion is whoever can make the case inside the company when you're not in the room, so your job is to arm them with a business case, answers for the skeptics and a short pitch they can forward. Worked example: a persona card for the security reviewer Say you sell a security product, and your deal reviews keep turning up the same person: the security lead who has to sign off before procurement will move. Here's what a research-based buyer persona for that person might hold. Every field ties back to the decision: Field Example Role in the decision Influencer who can block: signs off on security before procurement moves Goals No new risk to company data, and fewer tools to monitor Concerns Where data is stored, who can access it, how the vendor handles incidents Success criteria Passes the security review without exceptions, and fits the current tools Proof they need A security overview, certifications, a reference from a similar company Where they learn Peers, analyst reports, the vendor's security documentation Notice what's missing: no age, no hobbies, no stock photo. A good rule: keep only the details that change what you design or say. Common traps Building personas from demographics alone. They describe people without explaining what they're trying to do. One persona for the buyer and the user. The CFO who approves and the analyst who logs in every day want different things. Treating the friendliest contact as the champion. Ask whether they can actually move the rest of the group. Making personas nobody uses. Build them with s"}, {"id": "pmm/insight/jobs-to-be-done-and-win-loss-interviews", "text": "Ask a customer why they bought something and you'll get a neat, sensible answer. It's often not the real one. People aren't lying. They just tidy up the story after the fact and leave out the months of low-grade frustration, or the worry that almost stopped them. Segments and personas tell you who buys. They can't tell you why someone switched from what they used before, or why a deal you expected to win went to a rival or to nobody at all. Two kinds of interview can: jobs-to-be-done interviews with people who recently switched, and win/loss interviews with buyers who just made a decision. Both work only if you ask about what actually happened, not what people think they'd do. What are they hiring it to do? Ask people why they buy a milkshake at a drive-through and they'll talk about flavor. The Clayton Christensen Institute uses that example to show what the flavor answer misses: morning buyers were really hiring the milkshake to stay full, make a dull drive more interesting and eat with one hand, so its competition was bananas and bagels, not other milkshakes. Its software example is a tax company that built a powerful platform for maximizing refunds, but the extra complexity meant customers spent more time on their taxes. For many of them the real job was spending less time on taxes, and simplifying the product increased sales. That's the idea behind jobs to be done. The institute describes it as a lens on the circumstances that push people toward or away from a decision. A job is the progress someone is trying to make in a particular situation, and people \"hire\" a product when that job comes up. Every job has functional, social and emotional sides, which is why it explains choices that demographics and feature lists miss. Why should you care as a PMM? Because the job does two things for you: It names the real competitor. If the job is \"get my taxes off my plate,\" the rival may be an accountant or a deadline extension, not another app. It's the competition, seen from the customer's side. It hands you the message. \"Done in under an hour\" speaks to that job. \"Maximize your refund\" speaks to a different one. Rewind to the moment they started looking A man buys a new mattress at Costco and calls it an impulse buy. Bob Moesta's firm, the Re-Wired Group, uses his interview to show why you shouldn't take that at face value. Probing showed he'd been thinking about replacing the mattress for more than a year, and that Costco's return policy and the absence of high-pressure salespeople mattered more to the decision than any feature. That's what a switch interview is for. It walks someone through a recent purchase, back to the first moment they noticed a problem. The same approach sorts what drives or blocks a switch into four forces. Progress happens when the push of the current situation and the pull of the new solution outweigh anxiety about the new and the habit of the present. Say a team runs its projects in spreadsheets and is weighing a project-management tool. Here's how the four forces might look: Force Direction Example What the PMM can do Push Toward switching Missed deadlines because nobody sees the whole plan Name the pain plainly in the message Pull Toward switching One view of every project Show the result in the first session Anxiety Against switching \"Will the team actually use it? What about our data?\" Import help, a free trial, a customer like them Habit Against switching Everyone already knows the spreadsheet Make the first week feel familiar, such as importing the sheet as is Look at the bottom two rows. Most marketing pushes harder on pull, with more features and bigger promises. Many deals are won by lowering anxiety instead. Ask about the past, not the future Ask someone \"would you use...?\" and they'll happily answer. The answer just isn't worth much. Nielsen Norman Group warns that people are poor at predicting their own future behavior, so hypothetical questions give unreliable answers. Ask about specific past experiences instead, and avoid wording that hints at the answer you expect. How many interviews should you run? Keep going until new conversations stop teaching you anything. A narrow group needs fewer than a broad one. A few switch-interview questions that follow those rules: \"When did you first think about changing how you did this?\" \"What were you using before, and what was going wrong?\" \"What almost stopped you from switching?\" \"Walk me through the day you decided.\" Win/loss: the reason behind the reason Every lost deal ends up with a reason in the CRM, and it's usually a word or two long. Win/loss interviews go back to the buyers and ask why they chose you, or didn't. Pragmatic Institute's advice is specific. Interview both won and lost buyers two to four weeks after the decision. Have someone outside sales run the interviews, because buyers speak more freely to a non-seller. Review at least ten deals before calling something a pattern. Its best-practice guide adds that reps often blame price, product or timing, assumptions that only conversations with the buyers themselves can test. Forrester makes the same point about CRM close reasons like \"budget\" or \"pricing\": they're shallow, and sellers' reading of a loss often differs from the buyer's real reason. Here's what that short reason often hides: What the CRM says What the interview often finds \"Price was too high\" The buyer didn't see enough value for the price, or the champion couldn't make the case internally \"Went with the incumbent\" Anxiety about switching outweighed the pull of something new \"Bad timing\" Nobody owned the problem, so the group never decided Common traps Asking \"why did you buy?\" People give tidy reasons after the fact. Ask what happened, in order. Letting sales run win/loss. Buyers soften the truth for the person who sold to them. Interviewing only wins. Wins tell you what worked. Losses tell you what to fix. Treating one vivid story as a pattern. Wait until the same theme shows up acro"}, {"id": "pmm/insight/from-research-to-an-insight", "text": "You've done the interviews. Now you're staring at pages of notes, a few win/loss summaries, maybe a survey export and a handful of quotes you love. None of it helps until it becomes a few statements a team can act on. This is where research most often stalls: a deck of quotes and charts that everyone finds interesting and nobody uses. The skill is getting from \"we talked to customers\" to \"so here's what we'll change.\" From one quote to a decision Take a single line from an interview: \"I spent the first week just figuring out which columns to import.\" On its own, it's an anecdote. Your job is to work out whether it's part of something bigger, and what to do if it is. Nielsen Norman Group's guide to thematic analysis separates three layers. Observations are the raw data, such as a quote or something you watched a person do. Codes are labels you attach to pieces of that data. Themes are patterns that recur across several participants or sources. The guide's six steps: gather the data, read all of it, code it (plain descriptive labels first), add interpretive codes, step away for a while, then evaluate the themes. An insight goes one step past a theme, adding why the pattern happens and what it means for your product or message. Follow that one quote all the way through: Layer Example Observation \"I spent the first week just figuring out which columns to import.\" Theme New admins get stuck importing their existing data Insight New admins stall at import because they don't know how their old fields map to ours, and many trials end there Action Lead onboarding with an import template, and say \"bring your spreadsheet as is\" in the trial message The whole table is an illustration, not real research. Sort first, name later An affinity diagram is the hands-on version. NN/g's method runs like this. Each person writes notes on their own for 5 to 10 minutes. The group sorts the notes by content first and names each cluster only afterward. Small clusters stay, because they can reflect a distinct view. A note that fits two clusters gets duplicated instead of forced into one. Then the team ranks the clusters, using dot voting if it can't agree. Naming last can feel backward, which is exactly why it works. Name the clusters first and you'll sort every note into the categories you already believed in. Write it in one sentence A fill-in-the-blanks sentence keeps insights honest, because it won't let you skip the why or the action: [Who] does or believes [what] because [why] , so we should [action] . Before sharing one, check it against three questions: Is it backed by more than one source? A pattern across interviews, or interviews plus data, beats one memorable quote. Does it change what the team would do? If it changes no decision, it's a reminder, not an insight. Does it point to an action? If nothing would change, keep digging. Watch the gap between saying and doing In a 2025 study by the research group METR, experienced developers using early-2025 AI tools believed AI tools had made them about 20% faster, while their tasks actually took 19% longer. What they felt and what happened had simply come apart. That's why you want more than one kind of evidence. Interviews and surveys capture what people say. Usage data and experiments capture what they do. The two often disagree, and that gap is usually where the insight is. Share it so it gets used Research readouts often run in the order the work happened: method first, findings next, recommendations at the very end. Flip that. Lead with the insight, then the evidence, then the action, and keep it to the three or four insights that matter most. A team will act on three clear insights. Thirty findings get filed away. Common traps Summarizing instead of interpreting. \"Users mentioned onboarding\" is a topic, not an insight. Treating one vivid quote as a pattern. Check that the theme shows up across several people. Naming the clusters first. You'll find what you expected to find. Skipping the \"so what.\" An insight without an action is trivia. In an interview When a case hands you research or data, the temptation is to recite it. Don't. Give one insight, the two pieces of evidence behind it, and what you'd do about it. Self-check: Your research summary says \"Users find setup confusing.\" Is that an insight? How would you make it one? It's a theme. To turn it into an insight, add who exactly, why it happens and what should follow. For example: \"First-time admins at small companies abandon setup at the permissions step because they don't know which roles they need, so we should offer three preset role templates and mention them in the trial email.\" Then check that more than one source backs it."}, {"id": "pmm/insight/pick-a-beachhead-segment", "text": "The setup. You're the PMM at a startup building an AI phone receptionist for small businesses that run on appointments. It answers calls, books and moves appointments, and sends reminders. You have the budget to go after one segment this year. The product is hypothetical; the market numbers are real. The facts. US Census Bureau County Business Patterns, 2023: Segment Establishments Employees Average employees per establishment Share with fewer than 5 employees Dental offices 135,665 1,028,889 7.6 37.7% Veterinary services 34,296 475,106 13.9 27.7% Beauty salons 84,176 391,064 4.6 68.7% These counts cover businesses with paid employees. They leave out the self-employed, so the true number of salons, many of them one-person businesses, is higher. The averages and shares are calculated from the Census file. Your task: Score each segment on the questions from the lesson: size, urgency of the pain, reach, your ability to win, word of mouth, and where it leads. Pick one and defend it in three sentences. Say what you'd need to learn to change your mind. +++ reveal Sample answer Scores: Question Dental offices Veterinary services Beauty salons Big enough? Yes: the most establishments Smaller, but larger offices Many, but most are tiny Urgent pain? High: a small front desk juggles calls and patients in the chair Medium: bigger staffs can cover the phone Mixed: many already use online booking apps Reachable? Yes: dental associations, practice software, industry events Yes: veterinary associations and practice software Hard: scattered, many sole operators Can you win? Yes, if it books into the practice's existing schedule Yes, same condition Hard: low budgets and existing booking apps Word of mouth? Strong among dentists who know each other locally Moderate Strong, but tied to apps they already use Where it leads Other appointment-based health care: vets, optometry, physical therapy Dental and other clinics Spas and barbershops The pick: dental offices. They're the largest segment with paid staff, and the average office of about eight people has a front desk small enough that missed calls are a daily cost. They're reachable through associations and the software they already use. Winning there opens a clear path to other appointment-based clinics, starting with veterinary practices. What would change my mind: interviews showing dental offices already miss few calls, or that the main practice software is about to ship its own receptionist. Either one would push the choice toward vets. Why it works It scores all three segments on the same questions instead of picking a favorite and defending it. It reads past the biggest raw number: salons look large, but most are tiny and already use booking apps. It names an expansion path, so the first segment leads somewhere. What interviewers push on \"Why not salons? There are more of them than the Census shows.\" Count isn't the same as winnable. Most are one- to four-person businesses with small budgets and an app they already like. \"How would you check this before spending the budget?\" Fifteen interviews with office managers in each segment, a look at how many calls go unanswered, and a landing page test aimed at each segment. Practice twist The leading dental practice-management company announces its own AI receptionist, included free for its customers. Does your pick change, and how do you position against \"free and already installed\"?"}, {"id": "pmm/insight/map-a-buying-group", "text": "The facts. Workday sells cloud software for HR, finance, spend management, planning and AI agents. Its annual report names the buying office for each product line: HR software sells to the office of the chief human resources officer (CHRO), financial and spend management to the office of the CFO, planning to both, and its platform and industry products to the CIO, CFO and CHRO. Direct sales teams are organized by geography, industry or customer size, with resellers and systems integrators alongside them. On November 18, 2026, Workday announced it would bring its payroll product to seven new countries, including Singapore and Spain. It also announced \"autopilot\" capabilities for its Payroll Agent, which checks every employee's pay before and after each payroll run, flags likely errors and prepares corrections for the payroll team to approve. Singapore is planned for full availability in January 2027, Spain is planned to follow starting in 2027, and autopilot is planned for early adopters in early 2027. More than 11,500 organizations use Workday, including more than 65% of the Fortune 500. Your task: You're the Workday PMM for payroll. Your target is an existing Workday HR customer, a multinational that runs payroll in Singapore and Spain through other providers today. Map the buying group: each member's role in the decision (sponsor, approver, influencer, user or possible blocker), their worry, and the proof they need. Name the person most likely to move the group, and say what you'd give them. Name the biggest risk to the deal. +++ reveal Sample answer Member Role in the decision Worry Proof they need CHRO Sponsor Employees paid correctly everywhere; HR's reputation A similar multinational's results; how accuracy is checked CFO Approver The cost of several payroll providers; fines for errors A business case: providers retired, errors avoided Head of global payroll User and likely mobilizer Manual checks, changing local rules, payday errors A demo of autopilot catching errors, with the team approving every change CIO and IT security Influencer, possible blocker Data protection, integrations, how the AI is governed Security and AI governance documentation Country payroll leads Users Changes to their work; local rules handled correctly Local rule coverage and a migration plan Procurement Approver of terms Contract terms with current providers Timing that matches when current contracts end The whole table is inference from the facts above. The mobilizer: the head of global payroll. They feel the problem every pay cycle, and they're credible to both HR and finance. Give them a one-page business case they can take to the CFO, a before-and-after of a pay cycle with autopilot, and a ready answer for IT on how the AI is controlled. The biggest risk: timing. Spain isn't on a firm date yet and autopilot is planned for early 2027, so a promise the product can't keep would hurt trust. Sequence the deal: start with Singapore and the countries already live, and plan Spain for when it's ready. Why it works It sorts people by their role in the decision, not their title. It picks the mobilizer by who can move the group, not by who's most senior or friendliest. It names a real risk from the facts, the uneven country timing, instead of a generic objection. What interviewers push on \"Who signs off on an AI that touches everyone's pay?\" Likely IT security and legal along with the CHRO, which is why governance documentation belongs in the plan from day one. \"Why would they leave a provider that works?\" Fewer systems and errors caught before payday. Quantify both in the business case. Practice twist Now the customer doesn't use Workday for HR at all. How does the buying group change, and how much bigger does the decision get?"}, {"id": "pmm/insight/turn-research-into-an-insight", "text": "The setup. You're the PMM for an AI coding assistant aimed at professional developers. Leadership wants a new homepage message. Here's the research you have. The facts. In Stack Overflow's 2026 Developer Survey, 65.9% of respondents said they use AI coding assistants or agents. Asked when they trust AI output, 48.0% said when they can easily verify it, 16.3% only for low-risk tasks, 12.3% not for most work tasks, and 6.6% for many tasks including important decisions. In the 2025 survey, 66% named \"almost right, but not quite\" answers as their top frustration with AI tools, and 45.2% said debugging AI-generated code takes more time. In a 2025 randomized trial by the research group METR, 16 experienced open-source developers worked on 246 real tasks in their own projects. Tasks where AI was allowed took 19% longer. Before the study, the developers expected AI to save 24% of their time, and afterward they still believed it had saved 20%. METR notes the sample was small and the tools were from early 2025. In February 2026, METR said its newer data from late 2025, covering 57 developers, pointed to speedups instead, but called that result unreliable, partly because more developers declined to take part, since they didn't want to work without AI. Your task: Write three findings from the research. Group them into one theme. Write one insight in the format: who does or believes what, because why, so we should what. Recommend one change to the homepage message and one to the product or onboarding. +++ reveal Sample answer Findings: Most developers use AI coding tools, but nearly half trust the output only when they can check it, and just 6.6% trust it for important decisions. Near-misses are the main pain: answers that are almost right cost time to find and fix. Developers' sense of how much time AI saves isn't reliable. In one controlled study, they felt faster while measurably slower. Theme: developers treat AI output as something to verify, not something to trust. Insight: \"Professional developers use AI tools widely but don't trust them on important work, because near-miss answers cost them time to check and fix, so we should compete on how easy our output is to verify, not on how fast it writes code.\" Homepage message: lead with confidence, not speed. Talk about code you can check quickly, with clear explanations and tests, and drop \"10x faster\" claims that developers already doubt. Product or onboarding: show the explanation and the tests with every suggestion by default, so checking takes seconds. Why it works It combines what developers say (the surveys) with what they did (the trial), and builds the insight on the gap. It doesn't hang everything on one small study. The trust pattern shows up in both surveys. Each part of the insight leads to a specific change. What interviewers push on \"Isn't the METR study out of date?\" Partly. The tools have improved and METR's newer data points to speedups, though METR calls it unreliable. The insight rests mainly on trust and verification, which both surveys support. \"Won't 'easy to verify' sound weaker than 'faster'?\" It's specific and believable, and it answers the frustration developers name most. Practice twist A competitor's homepage says \"Ship code 10x faster with AI.\" Using only this research, write the two-sentence response your sales team should use when prospects bring it up."}, {"id": "pmm/positioning/alternatives", "text": "Your toughest competitor might be a spreadsheet. Buyers never judge a product on its own. They judge it against whatever they'd do instead, and that's often not a competitor in the usual sense. That's why positioning so often goes wrong at the very first step. The team starts from its features and only later asks what customers compare them with. Get the alternatives wrong and everything you build on top, from the positioning statement to the message house, inherits the mistake. Positioning is the frame buyers use Tell a buyer your product is a \"CRM\" and watch what happens. In an instant they've guessed who you compete with, what features you have and roughly what you cost. You haven't said a word about any of it. That reaction is positioning at work. It's the place a product holds in a buyer's mind compared with the alternatives, an idea Al Ries and Jack Trout made famous in their 1981 book Positioning . April Dunford, a widely cited voice on B2B positioning, frames it as context: the frame a buyer uses to make sense of a product. Good positioning sets off assumptions that are true about your product. Bad positioning sets off ones that aren't, because the category you picked promises things you don't do. The five parts, and why the order matters Dunford breaks positioning into five components that depend on each other: Competitive alternatives: what customers would use if you didn't exist Differentiated capabilities: what you have that those alternatives lack Value: what those capabilities do for the customer Target customers: who cares most about that value Market category: the frame that makes the value obvious The order isn't arbitrary. Each step uses the answer to the one before it. You can't say what's different about you until you know what you're compared with. So you start with the alternatives, and with Dunford's core question: what would a customer do if you didn't exist? This can look like it clashes with advice to pick your target customer first. It doesn't, quite. Dunford's order assumes you already have customers who love the product, because they're the people you ask about alternatives, so it fits repositioning something that exists. When you're entering a brand-new market with no customers yet, choosing a segment first comes naturally. Play video: Competition in a positioning exercise Competition in a positioning exercise April Dunford, Feb 2026 · 29 min · starts at 3:26 · YouTube How to find what customers would really do The alternatives are often not your named competitors. They can be a spreadsheet, a manual process, an intern, or doing nothing at all. That last one deserves special respect. Harvard's John Gourville found that buyers overvalue what they already have while sellers overvalue what's new, a mismatch he puts at about nine to one. So the status quo starts every deal with a head start. Newer research points the same way. In a study of more than 2.5 million recorded sales conversations, Matthew Dixon and Ted McKenna found that 40% to 60% of deals are lost to buyers who intend to purchase but never act. To build the real list, start with your happiest, best-fit customers and ask what they would do if your product didn't exist. Expect their answers to be humbler than the competitor slide in your sales deck. Group them into a few alternatives and see which come up most. Once you know where you're strong against those alternatives, you pick a strategy. Dunford describes three: Strategy When it fits Head to head You claim the leader's category with no qualifiers. It works best if you are the leader, or a strong challenger when the leader is weak Big fish, small pond You win a sub-segment of an existing category, one that cares most about what you do best. Most tech companies start here Create a new category No existing category explains your value. The hardest route, so use it only when nothing else fits Worked example: how Janna Systems stopped losing to Siebel Dunford told this story from her own career in a 2019 talk. She doesn't name the company there, but the details match Janna Systems, the CRM maker Siebel bought in 2000. Here's how it went, by her account: Before: Janna sold itself as enterprise CRM and kept losing to Siebel, which had 8,000 employees and $2 billion in revenue. By Dunford's account, her company had 30 people and about $1.3 million in revenue. The real strength: Janna could model relationships between people across organizations. For most CRM buyers, that didn't matter much. The insight: bankers at Goldman Sachs and Merrill Lynch lit up in demos, because banks run on relationships. After: Janna repositioned as CRM for investment banks. Dunford says revenue reached a little under $80 million in about 18 months, and that Siebel paid $1.3 billion for the company. Before you repeat those numbers, check them against the press. Computerworld's coverage of the deal in September 2000 gives smaller ones: $13 million in revenue for 1999 and $12.6 million for the first half of 2000. It valued the deal at $975 million in stock, below Dunford's figure, and named customers including Merrill Lynch and Lehman Brothers. The press figures are the safer ones to quote, since they were reported at the time, and the positioning point holds either way. Notice what didn't change: the product. Janna changed its context. It picked the customers who valued its difference and named a category they already understood. That's the big fish, small pond strategy from the table above. Common traps Listing every possible competitor instead of what customers actually do today. Fixating on the rival your team talks about most. Check which alternatives actually come up in won and lost deals, because the one you lose to most is often the status quo. Assuming you need a new category. Most products win by framing themselves inside one buyers already know, since a new category means teaching the market from scratch. In an interview \"How would you position X?\" is a classic PMM que"}, {"id": "pmm/positioning/parity-difference", "text": "A feature can go from a reason to buy to something buyers simply expect in about two years. Basic AI assistants in work software just made that trip, while more advanced AI moved into pricier plans. Teams also tend to pitch only their differences, then lose deals over a missing basic. So once you know the alternatives, ask two separate questions. What do you have to match? What makes you the better choice? Keeping them apart, and watching how the frame you pick changes the answers, keeps your positioning honest. Three parts of a position The framework comes from brand management. Kevin Lane Keller, Brian Sternthal and Alice Tybout laid it out in a 2002 Harvard Business Review article. They argued that positioning tends to focus only on differences, and neglects two other parts that matter just as much. It works the same way for software, where parity points usually go by \"table stakes\". Tybout later boiled a position down to three parts. Each one answers a question a skeptical buyer might ask: Part Question it answers Frame of reference What are we compared against? Point of difference Why are we the better choice within that frame? Reason to believe Why should anyone believe that claim? Then there are points of parity : what you have to match to belong in the frame at all. A CRM has to import your contacts and sync with your email. Nobody picks one for that. Leave it out, though, and you're off the shortlist. Play video: Points of parity and points of difference Points of parity and points of difference Corey Nelson, Carlson School of Management, Jul 2021 · 11 min · YouTube The frame decides what counts as a difference A feature doesn't carry its status around with it. The same feature can be a difference in one frame and parity in another, and in software the status can flip fast, because rivals can ship a copy within months. AI assistants, 2023 to 2025. In 2023, AI was something you paid extra for. Notion AI launched as an add-on at $10 per member a month, and Microsoft 365 Copilot went on sale to large companies at $30 per user a month. By 2025, rivals were folding it into their plans. Google stopped selling its Gemini add-ons and put its AI features into Workspace Business and Enterprise plans, and Atlassian made its Rovo assistant part of every paid plan at no extra cost. In about two years, basic AI assistance went from a reason to buy to table stakes, while premium AI features moved into higher tiers. Database or BI tool? April Dunford describes a database product whose prospects kept saying they already had Oracle. Same product, new frame: once it was positioned as a business intelligence tool, buyers compared it with different products, and the conversation changed. Worked example: the difference Adobe tried to buy In 2022, Adobe agreed to buy Figma for about $20 billion and described it as a \"web-first collaborative design platform\". Why pay that much for a rival? The UK's Competition and Markets Authority (CMA) reviewed the deal, and its findings lay out both sides of the table. It treated Figma Design and Adobe XD as the same kind of product: all-in-one design tools for professionals who design apps and websites. Same frame, very different results: Figma Design Adobe XD Where it runs In the browser A desktop app Share of revenue in that market Over 80% 5 to 10% Parity: designing screens and building prototypes. Both tools did it, so neither won on it. Difference: Figma runs in the browser, so a whole team can work in one file at the same time. Adobe planned a web tool, Project Spice, to match Figma's real-time collaboration, then canceled it in September 2022, days before the deal was announced. The CMA judged XD a weaker product, and Adobe told it XD had been in maintenance mode since early 2022. The CMA warned that the deal would remove the competition XD still gave Figma, and the companies called it off in December 2023. Real-time collaboration was a difference big enough that the leader in creative software tried to build it, then tried to buy it. Build your own parity table Put your product next to the main alternative, one attribute per row, and be honest in the last column: Attribute Us Main alternative Parity or difference? Does the target care? Example: real-time co-editing Built in Export the file and email it Difference Yes, a lot Example: single sign-on (SSO) Yes Yes Parity IT expects it; not a reason to buy Two rules keep the table useful. A difference only counts if the target customer values it. Competitors will copy your differences over time, so revisit the table. Common traps Claiming a difference the customer doesn't care about. \"First to use technology X\" means nothing if the outcome is the same. Ignoring the frame. Your differences change depending on what you're compared against. Skipping parity. Missing a must-have kills deals before your difference even gets heard. In an interview When you position anything, say the frame out loud first. A difference only means something next to what you're compared with: \"In the project-management tool frame, our parity points are A and B, and our difference is C.\" Self-check: An expense app built for small businesses starts selling to large companies. What happens to \"set up in an afternoon\"? It stops being much of a difference. Large companies expect a longer rollout and judge vendors on approval workflows, audit trails and links to their finance systems, so those become the parity points to match. The difference has to move to something they value, such as how quickly employees actually start using it."}, {"id": "pmm/positioning/perceptual-maps", "text": "Ask developers which code editor they want to use next year, and Neovim trails far behind VS Code. Ask the people who already use Neovim whether they want to keep it, and it beats every editor on the map below, VS Code included. A feature list would never show you that. Two numbers on one chart show it at a glance. That chart is a perceptual map. Positioning work leaves you with two answers: who buyers compare you with, and on what. A perceptual map puts both on one page: two attributes buyers care about, and where each product sits in their minds. It's a fast way to check whether buyers see you the way you think they do, and whether there's open space worth claiming. What a perceptual map shows OpenStax's Principles of Marketing describes a perceptual map as a diagram of how the average target customer perceives your product next to competitors, plotted on two attributes that drive the decision. Marketers use it for three jobs: checking whether customers see the product as intended, finding the closest rivals, and spotting empty areas that might be opportunities. None of this is new. Researchers have built these maps from survey data for decades, and Hauser and Koppelman were already comparing mapping techniques in the Journal of Marketing Research in 1979. The key word is perceives . Two products with the same feature list can sit far apart on the map if buyers see them differently. Play video: Understand in Under 5: Perceptual Mapping Understand in Under 5: Perceptual Mapping Choice Based Market Insights, Mar 2022 · 5 min · YouTube How to build one The steps are simple. The discipline is in where the data comes from. Pick the buyer and the frame. Use the real alternatives customers consider, not the rivals you'd like to have. Choose two attributes buyers use to decide. Take them from interviews and win/loss notes, not your feature list, and make sure they don't move together. If every fast product is also cheap, one axis would do. Place each product with buyer data. Surveys, review scores and the reasons deals were won or lost all work. Your team's opinions don't. Read it. Where are you, who's closest, and where is the empty space? Ask why a space is empty before you move into it. Sometimes nobody wants that combination. Worked example: mapping code editors with real survey data Here's a map built from what users say, not what a product team believes. Stack Overflow's 2026 Developer Survey drew 30,903 responses from 169 countries. For development environments, 13,193 developers answered, and the survey reports two numbers for each tool. Desired is the share of respondents who want to use it next year. Admired is the share of its current users who want to keep using it. That gives a real two-axis map: how many developers are drawn to a tool, and how loyal its users are. Perceptual map of code editors, 2026 Stack Overflow Developer Survey Desired on the horizontal axis, admired on the vertical axis. VS Code is far right. Neovim is top left. Zed, IntelliJ IDEA and PyCharm cluster in the upper left. Cursor, Kiro and Windsurf sit low on the left. 0% 10% 20% 30% 40% 70% 60% 50% 40% 30% Desired: want to use it next year Admired: users who want to keep it VS Code IntelliJ IDEA Neovim Zed PyCharm Cursor Kiro Windsurf Each dot is one editor. Source: Stack Overflow Developer Survey 2026, development environments (13,193 answers). Editor Desired Admired Visual Studio Code 40.6% 55.7% IntelliJ IDEA 14.5% 53.3% Neovim 12.9% 69.9% Zed 9.7% 57.3% PyCharm 8.2% 49.7% Cursor 7.9% 33.0% Kiro 1.7% 31.2% Windsurf 1.7% 22.7% Now read it: VS Code owns the right side alone. It's the default choice, wanted by far more developers than anything else, with solid loyalty. Neovim is the opposite. Few developers overall want to use it next year, but the people who use it love it. That's a small, loyal niche, a classic big fish, small pond position. Cursor, Kiro and Windsurf sit low. All three score lower on admiration than the established editors on this map, which suggests their users are still shopping around. If you were the PMM at one of them, your gap to close would be loyalty, not awareness. Strictly speaking, desire and admiration are outcomes rather than the attributes buyers decide on, so this is a popularity-versus-loyalty map. A classic perceptual map would plot attributes, such as speed against extensibility. One more caution: the survey reflects Stack Overflow's own audience, not every developer, so the map shows how that group sees the tools. Analyst maps aren't perception maps If you work in tech, you'll also run into two-by-two charts that look a lot like perceptual maps but come from analysts. Gartner's Magic Quadrant places vendors on completeness of vision and ability to execute, sorting them into leaders, challengers, visionaries and niche players, and buyers use it to narrow their shortlists. Forrester's Wave launched in 2002, plotting a vendor's current offering against its strategy, with bubble size showing market presence. Since late 2024, customer feedback has replaced market presence on that graphic. These charts show what analysts judge, not what buyers feel. Buyers still read them, though, so where you land shapes perception too. That's why analyst relations shows up in so many PMM roles. Common traps Picking axes that flatter you. If the axes are your two strongest features, the map can't tell you anything new. Using attributes that move together. Price and a premium feel often rise together, which squeezes the map into a single line. Chasing every empty space. A gap can mean nobody wants that mix. Placing products from your team's opinions. Without buyer data, the map just shows what your team believes. In an interview Asked to position a product? Reach for the whiteboard. A quick sketch of two axes and three or four rivals shows you understand the market. Say where the axes come from, meaning what buyers actually decide on, then say where your product sits and where it should move. Sel"}, {"id": "pmm/positioning/statement-message-house", "text": "Ask the sales team, the homepage and the latest ad campaign what your product does. If you get three different answers, your positioning never made it out of the room where it was decided. Two tools carry it out. A positioning statement records the choices for your own team. A message house turns them into language that sales, the website and campaigns can all share. Skip them, and every team ends up writing its own version of what the product does. The positioning statement, and its catch The best-known template comes from Geoffrey Moore's Crossing the Chasm . Here are its slots: Slot What goes there For The target customers (for Moore, one beachhead segment) Who are dissatisfied with The current market alternative Our product is a The new product category That provides The key problem-solving capability Unlike The product alternative We have assembled The key features for this customer's specific use The appeal is obvious: it forces every part of positioning into one sentence. April Dunford pushes back hard anyway. She argues you should never write a positioning statement at all, because the blanks assume you already know each answer. Once the work is done, she records the results in a dated positioning template so the team shares one agreed version. If your team still wants one, treat the statement as a receipt for decisions you've already made, written after the thinking is done. That's a compromise Dunford herself wouldn't bother with. The message house: from choices to words Nobody puts a positioning statement on a homepage. It's internal. The message house turns it into language that sales, web and campaign teams can reuse, and the name tells you the shape. Atlassian's template has these parts: Part What goes there Roof The top-line message and who it's for Pillars Value themes that hold the roof up Proof points Evidence under each pillar: numbers, customer stories, features Boilerplate Standard descriptions in a few lengths Pragmatic Institute's guide to message maps describes the same structure: three message pillars (four at most), proof points under each ranked by their impact on revenue, and positioning statements in a few lengths. Preparation starts with gathering product information and defining the target buyers. Interviews rarely leave time for the whole house. RocketBlocks' guide to PMM hard skills offers a lighter three-step version of the same idea: why (the customer's pain), how (the value proposition and what makes it different), and what (about three benefits that act as reasons to believe). Play video: How to build positioning and messaging How to build positioning and messaging Henry Wang, ex-Google PMM, Sep 2023 · 23 min · starts at 1:13 · YouTube Worked example: Mailchimp outgrows email On May 13, 2019, Mailchimp's CEO, Ben Chestnut, announced that the company was moving from email marketing to an all-in-one marketing platform for small businesses. Landing pages, ads, social posting and CRM tools came into one app, with new plans. TechCrunch reported about 11 million active customers, projected 2019 revenue of $700 million, and a focus on businesses with fewer than 100 employees. Mailchimp also offered an Essentials plan designed for customers who mainly use email, and existing customers could stay on their current plans. That's a lot to explain to customers who came for email. Here's how it could look as a statement and a message house. It's an illustration built from the public facts, not Mailchimp's internal document. Statement: For small businesses that market themselves without a marketing team, Mailchimp is an all-in-one marketing platform that runs email, ads, landing pages and customer data from one place. Unlike stitching together separate tools, Mailchimp keeps your audience and results in one view. Pillar 1: One place Pillar 2: Know your customers Pillar 3: Grow at your pace Message Run every channel from one app Your audience data, shared across channels Start with email, add more when you're ready Proof Landing pages, ads, social and CRM in one app One audience view for every campaign A plan built for email-first customers; current customers keep their plans Common traps Pillars that are features. \"Landing pages\" is a feature. \"Run every channel from one app\" is value. No proof. A pillar with nothing under it is just a claim. Too many pillars. Past four, nobody remembers any of them. In an interview If an interviewer asks you to \"write the messaging\" for a launch, don't start with taglines. Sketch the house: one roof line, three pillars, and one proof point each. It shows you think in structure before you think in slogans. Self-check: Sales asks for a fourth and fifth pillar so they can mention every feature. What do you say? Keep three, four at most, and put the extra features under the pillar they prove. A pillar for every feature turns the house back into a feature list, and nobody remembers five messages."}, {"id": "pmm/positioning/audience-stage", "text": "Here's the meeting where a software purchase gets decided. The CFO asks what it costs. The IT lead asks what it might break. The person who'll use it every day asks whether it's any good. You walked in with one message. One message rarely works for all of them, and a buyer who has just noticed a problem needs something different from one comparing vendors. The skill is changing the framing for each audience and stage without changing what you stand for. Same value, different framing Pragmatic Institute draws the line clearly: positioning keeps every team on the same playbook, while the messaging built on it speaks to each type of buyer, such as functional, financial and technical buyers. In B2B, split the buyer from the user, because the person who signs off on the purchase is often not the person who uses it every day. Then ask what each one needs to see. Play video: Personas: where to use them in B2B messaging Personas: where to use them in B2B messaging Wynter, Mar 2023 · 4 min · YouTube A buying group, moving in three stages That meeting tends to be crowded, because in B2B the buyer is usually a group. Gartner's survey of 632 B2B buyers found buying groups of five to 16 people from as many as four functions. In 74% of them, members showed \"unhealthy conflict,\" such as clashing goals or being overruled by someone outside the group, and groups that reached consensus were 2.5 times more likely to call their deal high quality. So your B2B messaging has two jobs: speak to each member, and give the whole group a shared reason to agree. To plan which message goes where, it helps to compress the buying stages. OpenStax's Principles of Marketing lists eight for B2B, from problem recognition through supplier selection. Marketers often fold the early ones together and work with three: Stage What the buyer is doing What helps Awareness (need recognition) Naming a problem Educational content with no pitch Consideration (search and evaluation) Comparing ways to solve it Case studies, testimonials, demos Decision (purchase, or supplier selection in B2B) Comparing vendors Objection handling, sharp differences, trials Don't expect anyone to march through that table in order. Real buyers rarely move in a straight line, as Martina Lauchengco notes, and each member of a buying group can be at a different stage. Worked example: one house, two people Take an illustrative Mailchimp message house aimed at small shops. Same shop, different worries: Owner of a small shop Part-time marketer at that shop Cares about Time and money Getting campaigns out without juggling tools Awareness message \"Marketing shouldn't eat your weekend.\" \"Stop copying lists between five tools.\" Decision message One plan, one bill, and you can start with email only Your audience data in every channel, with no re-uploading Proof A plan for email-first customers; current customers keep their plans Landing pages, ads and CRM in the same app Common traps Changing the core value per persona. If every audience hears a different product, you have no positioning. Pitching at the awareness stage. People naming a problem want help first, so save the demo for later. Writing only for the buyer. Users have a big say in whether a B2B product gets renewed. In an interview If an interviewer asks you to \"write messaging,\" answer with two questions of your own: for whom, and at what stage? Then give two versions and point out what stayed the same. That's how you show the core value held. Self-check: A CFO is in the decision stage and an IT lead is in consideration for the same security product. What does each need? The CFO needs cost, risk and proof of return, plus answers to objections. The IT lead needs to compare approaches: a technical demo, case studies from similar companies, and how it fits their stack. The core value stays the same for both."}, {"id": "pmm/positioning/message-testing", "text": "Every message gets tested eventually. The only question is when. Skip it before launch, and the first real test happens on launch day, in front of buyers, when changing anything is slow and public. A few days of testing beforehand shows whether people understand the product, which words stick and which claims they doubt. Which test you run depends on how much traffic and time you have. Why your team can't judge its own copy Read your own homepage and it makes perfect sense. Of course it does: you already know what the product does. Your team fills in the gaps without noticing, so it can't judge whether a message is clear. Testing with your target audience catches that before launch. Play video: Why isn't everyone testing their messaging? Peep Laja of Wynter Why isn't everyone testing their messaging? Peep Laja of Wynter Envy, Nov 2024 · 15 min · YouTube Four ways to test, and when each fits Pick by what you need to learn: Method What it tells you What it needs Use it when Qualitative message test Why copy works or fails: clarity, relevance, value, difference A panel of about 12 to 13 people from your audience Before launch, especially in B2B Five-second test What people notice and remember at first glance A screenshot and a small panel Checking a homepage or ad A/B test Which version converts better Enough traffic, often 500+ conversions a month After launch, on high-traffic pages Interviews and sales feedback How real buyers react in context Access to customers and the sales team All the time Sources: Nielsen Norman Group , Kuric et al. 2023 . The fine print: Qualitative tests explain why. For a typical qualitative usability study of one user group, Nielsen Norman Group recommends five participants to uncover most common problems. Message tests work on similar logic: a small panel of the right people tells you why a message lands or doesn't, while an A/B test needs far more traffic and still can't tell you why. Five-second tests check first impressions. Show a design for five seconds, then ask people to describe what they saw. A 2023 peer-reviewed study showed web pages for 2, 5 or 10 seconds and found that people who saw complex pages too briefly struggled to say what the page was for, so match the exposure to the page. Positioning reviews don't stop at launch. April Dunford held a standing positioning check-in about every six months with sales, product and the CEO, to ask what had changed among competitors. Common traps Testing on coworkers. They already know what the product does, so they can't tell you whether the page explains it. Running an A/B test on a page with little traffic. You'll wait months for a result you can't trust. Testing a headline without saying who should read it. The right panel matters more than the method, because a clear message for the wrong reader still fails. In an interview \"How would you know the messaging works?\" The reflex answer is \"A/B test it,\" which often won't work on a low-traffic B2B site. Name a method that fits the situation instead: a qualitative test before launch, then conversion and win rates after. Self-check: Your team loves a clever headline, but in a message test, most of your target readers can't say what the product does. What do you do? Side with the readers. Rewrite the headline in plain words that say what the product does and for whom, then test it again. The team already knows the answer, so it can't judge whether the headline is clear."}, {"id": "pmm/positioning/docusign-status-quo", "text": "The facts. In April 2024, Docusign launched Intelligent Agreement Management (IAM) and called it a new software category. Its pitch: most companies are stuck in an \"agreement trap,\" with agreements stuck in static files, disconnected from the company's systems of record. It cited an upcoming study with Deloitte estimating that each business function loses about 25,000 hours a year developing agreements. IAM includes Navigator, which uses AI to turn stored contracts into searchable data, and Maestro, for building agreement workflows without code. By January 2026, Docusign had more than 1.8 million customers. More than 25,000 of them used IAM, which made up 10.8% of annual recurring revenue. Matthew Dixon and Ted McKenna analyzed more than 2.5 million recorded sales calls and found that 40% to 60% of deals are lost to buyers who intend to buy but never act. Your task: You're the PMM for Docusign IAM, selling to a company that already signs its contracts in Docusign. List the real alternatives for that buyer. Include doing nothing. Pick the one you lose to most, and write a two-sentence positioning against it. Name one thing you'd give sales for a buying group that agrees with the problem but stalls. +++ reveal Sample answer The alternatives: Keep signing in Docusign and storing contracts in shared drives, as they do today A contract management tool from another vendor Build workflows in tools they already own, such as their CRM Do nothing, because no single team owns the problem The one that matters most: the way they work today. Contracts already get signed, and the cost of the agreement trap is spread across legal, sales and finance, so no one feels it enough to push for a change. That's the indecision Dixon and McKenna describe. Positioning: \"Your contracts already get signed in Docusign, then disappear into folders no system can read. IAM turns them into data your sales, legal and finance teams can search and act on, without changing how anyone signs.\" For a stalled group: a small first step that limits the risk of a bad call, such as starting with one team's contracts, plus a one-page business case each function can check against its own numbers. Why it works It treats the current process, not another vendor, as the main competitor. It builds on what the buyer already trusts (e-signature) instead of asking them to replace anything. It plans for the stall, because a buying group can agree on the problem and still not act. What interviewers push on \"Why not lead with the Deloitte numbers?\" Docusign helped run that study, so a skeptical buyer will discount it. The buyer's own numbers persuade better. \"Who in the buying group cares most?\" Legal owns the contracts, but sales and finance feel the delays, so the message needs a line for each. Practice twist Now the buyer has never used Docusign and still signs on paper. How does your list of alternatives change, and which one matters most?"}, {"id": "pmm/positioning/ai-assistant-parity", "text": "The facts. Notion: Notion AI launched to everyone in February 2023 as an add-on at $10 per member a month, less with annual billing. Microsoft: Microsoft 365 Copilot went on sale to large companies on November 1, 2023, at $30 per user a month. In January 2025, Microsoft added Copilot to its Personal and Family plans and raised the US price by $3 a month. Google: in January 2025, Google stopped selling its Gemini add-ons and included its AI features in Workspace Business and Enterprise plans, with new pricing. Atlassian: its Rovo AI assistant started rolling out to Premium and Enterprise plans at no extra cost in April 2025. Your task: It's April 2025, and you're the PMM for Notion AI. Your VP asks whether selling AI as an add-on still makes sense. Is \"has an AI assistant\" still a point of difference for Notion? Build a parity-and-difference table against Google Workspace, for a team choosing where its docs and wiki should live. Recommend what Notion should do with the add-on. +++ reveal Sample answer 1. No. Once Google and Atlassian put AI into their plans, basic AI assistance became table stakes for work software. Charging extra for it makes Notion look more expensive, not better. 2. The table: Attribute Notion Google Workspace Call AI that drafts and summarizes in docs Yes, as a paid add-on Included in Business and Enterprise plans Parity , and Notion charges extra for it Docs, wiki and project tracking in one place Yes Spread across separate apps Difference , for teams that run projects inside their docs Email and calendar Newer apps of its own Gmail and Google Calendar, which many teams already live in Google's strength. Don't fight on it 3. The call: fold AI into the main plans, and move the message from \"has AI\" to what the AI does with a team's own workspace, such as finding answers across its docs and projects. What Notion actually did: in May 2025, it launched a new Business plan with its newest AI features, such as search across connected tools, built in. That put AI into a higher tier rather than giving it to everyone, so AI became a reason to upgrade. Why it works It spots the moment a difference turns into parity: when the main rivals build it into their base plans. It moves the fight to a frame Notion can win (docs, wiki and projects together) and away from Google's home ground. It checks the answer against what the company did. What interviewers push on \"Notion moved AI into a pricier plan instead of giving it to everyone. Is that the same thing?\" Not quite. It's a packaging choice that turns AI into a reason to upgrade. \"Doesn't bundling AI cut revenue?\" Maybe at first. The case is that a separate charge now reads as a fee for table stakes, and the plan price can carry the cost. \"What's the next difference?\" Something the AI can do that rivals' can't, because of what Notion already knows about a team's work. Practice twist Flip it. You're the PMM for Google Workspace, and Notion now includes AI too. What becomes your difference?"}, {"id": "pmm/positioning/switch-2-message-house", "text": "The facts. Nintendo Switch 2 launched June 5, 2025, at $449.99. It has a 7.9-inch 1080p screen, up to 4K output when docked, 256GB of storage, and it plays compatible original Switch games, though Nintendo says some aren't fully supported. GameChat, a built-in chat feature, could be used without a Nintendo Switch Online membership until the end of March 2026. The new Joy-Con 2 controllers can work like a mouse. The screen supports variable refresh rates up to 120Hz. Your task: It's spring 2025, before launch. Your audience is people who already own an original Switch and are deciding whether to upgrade. Write: A positioning statement (For / who are dissatisfied with / our product is a / that provides / unlike / we have assembled) A message house with a roof line and three pillars, each with one proof point +++ reveal Sample answer Statement: For Switch owners who love their library but are dissatisfied with how it looks and plays on the original hardware, Nintendo Switch 2 is the next Switch, and it plays the compatible games they already own. Unlike the Switch they have now, it adds a bigger, sharper 7.9-inch screen, 4K on the TV and built-in chat. Roof: \"Everything you love about Switch, bigger and better.\" Keep your library See it better Play together Message Your games come with you A bigger, sharper screen, and 4K on the TV Chat and play with friends built in Proof Plays compatible original Switch games 7.9-inch 1080p screen at up to 120Hz; up to 4K docked GameChat (Switch Online membership required after March 2026) Why it works The real alternative for this audience is keeping the Switch they have , so the first pillar removes the biggest reason not to upgrade: losing their games. Each pillar has a concrete proof point from the facts. It's honest about the membership requirement instead of hiding it. What interviewers push on \"How do you handle the $449.99 price?\" Lead with the library pillar, so the price reads as an upgrade to what they already own. \"Why not lead with the mouse controls?\" They're new, but it's unclear yet whether upgraders care. Test them before making them a pillar. Practice twist Same product, new audience: a family that has never owned a Switch. What changes in the roof and pillars?"}, {"id": "pmm/positioning/agentforce-personas", "text": "The facts. Salesforce made Agentforce generally available on October 29, 2024. At launch, pricing started at $2 per conversation. It includes a low-code Agent Builder and the Einstein Trust Layer, with an audit trail. In Salesforce's announcement, the publisher Wiley reported more than a 40% increase in case resolution. Your task: It's late 2024, right after launch. Write the launch message for a VP of Customer Service in the awareness stage. Rewrite it for a CIO in the decision stage who is worried about trust and control. Design a quick test that tells you whether each message works for its audience. +++ reveal Sample answer VP of Customer Service (awareness): \"Your customers wait in a queue while your team answers the same questions over and over. AI agents can now resolve routine cases any time of day, so your people can focus on the ones that need a human.\" CIO (decision): \"Agentforce runs inside the Salesforce platform you already govern. The Einstein Trust Layer and an audit trail show what every agent did and why. Pricing starts at $2 per conversation, so cost is tied to use, and you can start with one use case. Wiley, an early customer, reported more than a 40% increase in case resolution.\" Same core value: customers get answers faster, at lower cost. The VP hears about the queue and the team. The CIO hears about control, audits and cost tied to use, because a CIO answers for what runs on company systems. The test: a qualitative message test with about 12 people from each audience (VPs of service, CIOs). Ask each group what the message says, who it's for, whether it's relevant to them, and what would make them doubt it. Add a five-second test on the launch page hero to see whether people catch \"AI agents for customer service\" at a glance. Why it works The core value stays the same while the framing, stage and proof change. Proof shows up at the decision stage, where the buyer is comparing vendors, so the CIO gets governance, pricing and a customer result while the awareness message for the VP stays educational. A qualitative test fits because the page hasn't launched yet and the panel needs specific roles, like CIOs, that a site-wide A/B test can't target. What interviewers push on \"One customer's 40% isn't proof for everyone. How do you use it?\" Name it as one customer's result, and gather more cases quickly. \"What would make the CIO say no?\" Data access and accuracy risks, so the message has to answer those directly. Practice twist Add a third persona: the frontline support agent who worries an AI agent will replace them. What do they need to hear, and who should say it?"}, {"id": "pmm/launches/the-go-to-market-plan", "text": "Most launches don't fail on launch day. Launch day usually looks great. They fail over the next few months, when sales can't explain the product, support can't answer the questions coming in, and the people who signed up never come back. A go-to-market plan is how you head that off. It's less a document than a handful of decisions you make before anyone announces anything: who you're going after, what you'll tell them, how they'll buy, who has to be ready, and what counts as winning. The three things to settle first Pragmatic Institute boils launch planning down to three questions, and each one catches a different kind of failure: Goals: what does winning look like, and by when? Pragmatic's example is a revenue target for the first nine months. Set it before launch, or every result will look like a success afterward. Readiness: can the whole company sell and support this on day one? That means support, accounting, delivery and legal, plus a sales team that understands the economic, user and technical buyers. Pragmatic warns that tossing a product to sales \"over the wall\" can delay pipeline by at least three months. Constraints: does the plan fit the people you actually have? In Pragmatic's example, seven salespeople can work about 42 opportunities a month. If your goal needs far more than that, the plan is broken before launch day. Its pre-launch checklist adds the questions teams skip when they're excited about a launch: how urgent the problem really is for buyers, exactly which segment you're aiming at, and whether your positioning and messaging actually say the same thing. Seven questions every plan answers Put it all together and a launch plan comes down to seven questions: The question Target Who exactly are we going after first? Message Why should they care, compared with what they use today? Motion How will they buy: on their own, through sales or through a partner? Channels Where will they hear about it? Readiness Can sales, support and billing handle it on day one? Size How much effort does this launch deserve? Goals What counts as success, and by when? When a team can't answer these, it shows fast. Joan Schneider and Julie Hall, who wrote about launch failures for Harvard Business Review , say they can often predict a flop from a single conversation with a team that calls its product revolutionary without the research to back it up. How will they buy? Two products launch the same week. One is a $12-a-month tool a designer can buy with a credit card before lunch. The other is a $150,000-a-year platform that needs a security review, a procurement process and six weeks of setup. Same launch date, completely different launches. That difference is the motion, and a few signals usually make it obvious: Signal Points to self-serve Points to sales-led Price Low, monthly, on a card High, annual contract Who decides One person or a small team A buying group Reviews before buying None, or a quick trial Security, legal, procurement Time to value Minutes to days Weeks of setup Existing customers Mostly self-serve accounts Mostly accounts with an account manager There's a third option that's easy to forget: let someone else sell it. On AWS Marketplace, for example, a software company can make private offers to customers and authorize channel partners to resell its product. AWS bills the customer and pays the seller, and sellers can work deals alongside AWS's own account teams. A partner-led motion makes sense when a partner already owns the relationship or the budget, such as budget a customer has already set aside with a cloud provider. Plenty of launches end up using more than one motion. Just decide which one leads. A plan that tries to do everything at once usually does nothing particularly well. Worked example: Asana's spring 2025 release Here's what a real launch looks like through this lens. On May 28, 2025, Asana released ready-made AI workflows for request tracking, creative requests, ticketing and work intake, plus new AI Studio rules for summarizing and sorting tasks. It aimed the release at the teams that live on requests and process (operations, IT, support, marketing, design, HR, finance and legal) and pitched it as AI for everyone, helping teams move faster and share what works. It also said AI would be included in every paid plan, with AI Studio's basic tier rolling out to all of them from June 2025. The pricing page shows how people actually buy. The free Personal plan and the paid Starter ($10.99 per user a month, billed annually) and Advanced ($24.99) plans are self-serve. Enterprise and Enterprise+ go through sales. Paid plans include monthly AI Studio credits, and teams can buy more. Read it as a plan and the choices jump out: Target: teams that already run the same requests over and over, where a template saves time on day one. Message: AI for everyone, not just power users. Including AI in every paid plan backs that up instead of just saying it. Motion: product-led for the self-serve plans, where the templates do the onboarding, and sales-led for Enterprise, where buyers want governance and higher limits. Goals: Asana didn't publish them. A PMM there would probably watch template use and upgrades to paid plans. Prices and AI credits change, so check Asana's pricing page before quoting these numbers. Common traps Launching before anyone's ready. A great announcement that sales can't explain and support can't handle costs you deals for months. Trying every motion at once. Pick the one that leads, and add the others once it works. Setting goals after launch. Then every number looks like a win, and nobody learns anything. A plan the team can't staff. Check the goal against the sales and marketing capacity you really have, not the capacity you wish you had. In an interview \"How would you launch X?\" is a common PMM case question, and it rewards structure more than creativity. Walk through the seven questions in order, then slow down on the one or two that matter most for thi"}, {"id": "pmm/launches/launch-tiers", "text": "Most releases shouldn't be launches. A team that turns every update into an event wears out its sales reps, the press and its own customers. Then, when something truly big ships, it sounds like one more announcement in the pile. Launch tiers are the fix. They give everyone a shared way to decide, before anything ships, how much attention each release gets. The one rule: impact, not effort A team that just spent six months on a project will want it launched loudly. That's a natural instinct, and it's usually the wrong guide. Pragmatic Institute's guide to launch tiers puts one rule at the center: size the launch by its expected impact on the business and the market, not by how much engineering went into it. Tier 1 is for the biggest changes, such as a new category or a major shift, and it gets the most resources. Tier 2 covers smaller or incremental releases that still deserve enough support to get noticed. Tier 3 is for minimal-impact releases like maintenance updates, which need only routine communication. Pragmatic also notes that circumstances can override a tier. In practice, each tier comes with a default package, so nobody rebuilds the plan for every release: Tier What usually qualifies What it gets 1 A new product, a new paid plan or price, or a change that opens a new segment A full go-to-market plan, press and analyst briefings, sales training, a campaign, an in-product announcement 2 A meaningful new feature for existing customers, or one that closes a gap competitors use against you A blog post, email to the customers it affects, a sales update, an in-product message 3 Improvements and fixes Release notes or a changelog entry Let the tier set the process, too Tiers decide how loud a launch is. They can also decide how much process a release has to go through before it ships. GitLab's public handbook draws that line clearly. A feature that fits its existing plans simply ships in a monthly release. Mainly new products, services or SKUs, or changes just as complex to run, go through its new product introduction process, with a steering committee, one directly responsible person and a launch checklist. GitLab's marketing runs on a matching rhythm: smaller launches for the monthly releases, major launches twice a year and campaigns each quarter. That rhythm hides a useful trick. Small releases don't each need their own moment. Bundle several into one bigger launch, and customers hear one clear story instead of a trickle of minor announcements. Play video: PM war stories, how to launch a product at Google (w/ ex-Google PM) PM war stories, how to launch a product at Google (w/ ex-Google PM) RocketBlocks, May 2023 · 12 min · YouTube Five questions that settle the tier Tier debates get heated when someone's favorite project is on the line. Ask these first, and the answer is usually clear: Does it change what we sell or what we charge? If it adds a paid plan or raises prices, it's probably Tier 1. A price cut on an existing feature can be Tier 2. Does it open a new segment or help win deals against a rival? Tier 1 or 2. Who needs to know? Every customer, some of them, or almost nobody. Does sales need to say something new? Then it needs enablement, which means at least Tier 2. What happens if nobody hears about it? If the honest answer is \"not much,\" it's Tier 3. Where tiering goes wrong Tiering by engineering effort. A six-month rebuild that customers barely notice is still Tier 3. Making everything Tier 1. If every launch is big, none of them are. Setting a tier and forgetting it. A Tier 2 feature that starts winning deals may deserve a second, bigger push. Skipping Tier 3 notes. Small changes still need a changelog entry, because customers and support need to know what changed. In an interview Asked how you'd launch a small feature? Start with how you'd tier it. Recommending a smaller launch can feel risky, but explaining why a release deserves less noise shows judgment, not a lack of ambition. Self-check: Engineering spent six months rebuilding the search backend. Users will notice that results come back a little faster. What tier is it, and why? Tier 3 by default. The impact on customers and the business is small, however much work went in. It could move up to Tier 2 if slow search was a top complaint in support tickets or lost deals, because then it answers a real objection and sales should know about it."}, {"id": "pmm/launches/launch-metrics-and-goals", "text": "It's the Monday after launch. Page views are up, sign-ups are up, and someone is drafting the celebration email. Can anyone say whether the launch worked? Only if you decided before launch which numbers count and what level means success. Otherwise the launch gets judged by whichever number looks best afterward. Numbers that flatter and numbers that teach Eric Ries, author of The Lean Startup , has a name for numbers like these: vanity metrics. Page views, message counts and \"billions served\" all qualify. They look good, but they don't tell you what to do next. Worse, when they rise, every team credits its own work, so nobody learns anything. Ries argues that metrics should be actionable, accessible and auditable. Vanity numbers may be fine for press, he says, but they're dangerous for running a company. An actionable metric shows cause and effect. If it moves, you can tell why. In a launch, that usually means total sign-ups and page views. A better number is the share of new users who reach a first real result. If it drops, you can go find where people got stuck. Early warnings and final scores Revenue is the number everyone wants from a launch, and it's one of the last to arrive. By then, it's too late to fix anything. McKinsey separates leading indicators, which look forward and let a team act early, from lagging indicators, which measure results after the fact. It recommends tracking three to eight key metrics at any level, and warns that relying mostly on lagging ones makes a team reactive. It wrote that about product development, but the same split works for launches: Leading (first weeks) Lagging (months later) Self-serve launch Share of new users who reach a first real result; trial starts Conversion to paid; retention after 90 days Sales-led launch New pipeline that mentions the launch; sales calls using the new pitch Win rate; deal size; revenue Feature for existing customers Share of target accounts that try it Ongoing use; renewals; fewer support tickets on the old problem How many goals each tier gets A bug fix doesn't need a dashboard. The tier sets how ambitious the goals are and how many you track: Tier 1: a small set, about three, with at least one leading and one lagging goal, each with a target, reviewed weekly for the first month. Tier 2: one adoption goal among the customers it's for. Tier 3: no launch goal. Quality and support tickets are enough. The five parts of a real goal \"Drive awareness\" sounds like a goal. It isn't. It has no metric, no target, no date, no baseline and no owner. Compare: \"Raise the share of new trials that invite a teammate within seven days from 25% to 40% by March 31, owned by the growth PM.\" That one has all five parts, so on April 1 everyone will know whether it happened. Then decide in advance what you'll do if you miss. A leading indicator well below target in week two is your cue to change the message, the onboarding or the audience. Don't wait for the quarterly numbers. Where launch goals go wrong Reporting only sign-ups. They prove interest, not value. Tracking everything. Twenty metrics on a dashboard means nobody acts on any of them. No baseline. \"10,000 users\" means nothing until you know what normal looks like. Setting goals after launch. Every result then looks like a win. In an interview When someone asks how you'd measure success, give one leading and one lagging metric, each with a target. Then say what you'd change if the leading one comes in low. That shows you'd act on the numbers. Self-check: The week-one dashboard shows 50,000 page views and 8,000 sign-ups, and leadership is calling the launch a success. What do you ask before agreeing? What share of the 8,000 reached a first real result, and how that compares with the goal and with past launches. Then which segments signed up: the target customers or curious visitors? Sign-ups and page views show interest. Activation and early retention show whether the launch is working."}, {"id": "pmm/launches/after-launch-adoption-onboarding-and-lifecycle", "text": "Ninety days after launch, nobody will care how many people opened the announcement email. What matters is how many of them changed the way they work because of it. That's adoption. A launch announces a feature. Adoption is whether anyone actually uses it. For B2B products in particular, it's what renewals and upsells are built on, so your job doesn't end on launch day. It shifts from getting attention to getting use. Why adoption is the real finish line Start with what's at stake. For a software company with $500 million in annual recurring revenue, Bain estimates that each point of net revenue retention is worth about $25 million over five years. Net revenue retention is the revenue a company keeps and grows from its existing customers over a year. Bain reported in November 2026 that the median for public software companies fell from about 115% in 2023 to about 107%. Customer success hiring rose by more than half over six years as companies tried to protect that revenue. Customers renew and expand when the product keeps proving its value. A feature nobody uses can't prove anything. That's why adoption belongs in the launch plan, with its own goal and a date to hit it. Who adopts first, and who waits People don't all show up at once, and the ones who arrive first aren't much like the ones who come later. Everett Rogers introduced the diffusion of innovation theory in 1962. It sorts the people who adopt something new into five groups: innovators (2.5% of adopters), early adopters (13.5%), the early majority (34%), the late majority (34%) and laggards (16%). OpenStax notes that early adopters, often opinion leaders whose advice others follow, are usually the best first target for something new. The handoff from one group to the next is where adoption often stalls. Geoffrey Moore made the same point about tech markets: mainstream buyers want proof that a product works for companies like theirs. Each group needs something different from you: Group What gets them to adopt What the PMM provides Innovators and early adopters The new capability itself Early access, a direct line to the product team Early majority Proof it works for companies like theirs Customer stories, benchmarks, simple setup Late majority Low risk and low effort Defaults that work, migration help, support on hand Laggards Little choice Sunset notices for the old way, plenty of support Teach in the moment, not up front You've probably opened a new app, been walked through a tour of every button before you'd clicked anything, and hit \"Skip\" halfway through. Nielsen Norman Group warns that up-front tutorials, the walkthroughs that appear before someone has done anything, tend to interrupt people, get skipped and often don't improve how well they do the task. What works better is help that appears when someone needs it, such as a tip on an unfamiliar tool. It should be easy to dismiss and easy to find again, and it should give more detail only on request. NN/g suggests saving help for the features that really need it. Empty screens are another chance to teach: a new user's blank dashboard should explain what will appear there and offer a direct path to the first key task. That changes where your launch messaging should live. The announcement email reaches people when they're not using the product. An in-product prompt reaches them at the exact moment the feature would help. The right message for where each person is Someone who's never touched the feature and someone who uses it daily shouldn't get the same email. After launch, what people need depends on what they've done so far: Who What they need Example Customers who haven't tried it A reason that fits their work An in-product prompt when they do the task it improves Tried it once Help getting to a real result A short guide or template for the first real use Using it regularly The next step The related feature, or the paid tier that extends it Admins and buyers Proof it's paying off A usage summary for their next business review Sales and customer success What to say now Updated talk tracks and the first customer results Check adoption at 30, 60 and 90 days against the goal you set. Wherever people drop off, that's the message to change. When adoption stalls: know, can or want? A stall usually looks like one of two things. Early users signed up, but the next wave isn't following. Or people try the feature once and never come back. Before you change anything, work out which of three gaps is to blame: Gap The question Typical fix Know Do the right users know it exists? In-product prompts, targeted email, a sales and success briefing Can Can they get it working? Templates, better defaults, help in context, setup support Want Is it worth it to them? Better positioning for the next group, proof from peers, or a product change Getting the diagnosis right matters because the fixes cost very different amounts. You can send all the emails you like, and they still won't solve a \"want\" problem. Where adoption plans go wrong Treating launch day as the finish line. Most of the value comes from adoption in the months after. One onboarding tour for everyone. It interrupts people at the wrong time, so they skip it. Reading early-adopter usage as proof. The early majority needs different evidence. Fixing the wrong gap. More awareness won't help users who can't get the feature to work. In an interview If you're asked what you'd do after launch, walk through how you'd measure adoption, where you'd look for drop-off, and how you'd tell an awareness problem from a value problem. That last distinction is where you show you'd fix the right thing. Self-check: Three months after launch, 20% of target accounts have tried a new reporting feature, but only 5% use it weekly. What do you look at, and what might you change? There are two gaps here. The 80% of target accounts that never tried it point to \"know.\" The drop from trying to keeping points to \"can\" or \"want.\" Find where people stop: while settin"}, {"id": "pmm/launches/marketing-ai-products-trust-demos-and-usage-base", "text": "Your AI demo probably isn't the first one your buyer has sat through. They've watched demos that worked perfectly on stage and fell apart on their own data. They've read the headlines about AI that got rolled back. So they show up skeptical, and they have good reason to. Positioning and launch planning still apply. An AI launch just adds four questions you have to answer: what can you honestly claim, how will you demo it, what controls does the buyer get, and how will you charge for it? What buyers mean by trust \"Trustworthy AI\" can sound vague until someone breaks it into parts. The US National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) does that in its AI Risk Management Framework, which lists seven characteristics of trustworthy AI: valid and reliable; safe; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; explainable and interpretable; privacy-enhanced; and fair, with harmful bias managed. Valid and reliable is the base the others rest on. Frameworks like this get revised, so check for the newest version. Each one turns into a question a buyer will ask, and something you can hand them in reply: Trait What the buyer asks What the PMM provides Valid and reliable \"Will it work on our data?\" A pilot or test on the buyer's own data, with results Accountable and transparent \"Who's responsible when it's wrong?\" Clear human handoff and approval steps Explainable \"Why did it answer that?\" Sources or reasoning shown with each answer Privacy-enhanced \"What happens to our data?\" A plain data-use policy, including whether data trains models Secure \"Will it pass our security review?\" Certifications and a security overview Claims you could defend to a regulator DoNotPay marketed itself as the world's first \"robot lawyer.\" According to the FTC's complaint, it did so without testing whether its output matched what a human lawyer would produce. In September 2024, the US Federal Trade Commission announced Operation AI Comply, five enforcement actions against deceptive AI claims and schemes, and DoNotPay was one of the targets. Regulators are watching AI claims, which gives you a simple test for every one you write: could you show the evidence to a skeptical buyer, or a regulator, today? \"Answers 60% of support questions in our pilot with three customers\" passes. \"Replaces your support team\" doesn't. Demos that survive the buyer's data A scripted demo on clean data proves one thing: the product can work once. Your buyer wants to know whether it'll work for them: Use realistic data, including the messy questions and edge cases their customers actually send. Show how it handles low confidence. It feels backward, but the buyer already expects the AI to be wrong sometimes. Let them see what happens when it isn't sure: does it hand off, ask for approval, or make something up? Show how success is measured. Name the metric and how it's calculated, so the number in the demo can be checked later. Offer a pilot. The strongest demo is the buyer's own results. Charging for use, or for results AI changes how software gets priced, because every use has a real cost. Bain groups AI pricing into three kinds of meter: effort (such as compute used), output (such as drafts written or records updated) and outcome (a business result). In its review of about 200 B2B software companies' public pricing in June 2026, among companies adding an AI meter, about 55% charged by output, 35% by effort and 10% by outcome. About four in five offered capacity models, where customers commit to a set amount up front. Bain sees outcome pricing fitting customer support best, because a resolved conversation is easy to observe and credit, and it expects outcome pricing to stay narrow for several years. Support is where outcome pricing caught on early. In August 2024, for example, Zendesk said customers of its AI agents would pay only for issues the AI resolved on its own. Notice what that does for trust. \"You pay only when it works\" is a pricing model and a promise at the same time. The promise only holds if the definition of \"works\" is clear and fair, so put that definition in the launch materials, not the fine print. Worked example: Klarna's big claim, and what came next In February 2024, Klarna announced that its AI assistant had handled 2.3 million conversations in its first month, two-thirds of its customer service chats. Klarna said that was the work of 700 full-time agents, that resolution time fell from about 11 minutes to under 2, and that customer satisfaction matched human agents. Then the story changed. In May 2025, Klarna's CEO told Bloomberg that cutting costs in customer service had gone too far and led to lower quality. Klarna started recruiting so customers would always be able to reach a real person. What you can take from it: Replacement claims age badly. \"The work of 700 agents\" made headlines, then became the benchmark the company was judged against. Lead with control, not replacement. Many buyers trust AI that handles routine work and hands the rest to people more than AI pitched as a substitute for people. Quality is part of the claim. Speed and cost numbers need a matching quality measure, or the launch story can turn against you. Where AI launches go wrong Demoing only on clean data. It proves nothing about the buyer's world. Claims you can't back up. If you couldn't show the evidence to a regulator, don't publish it. A metric definition hidden in the fine print. If the number changes when the definition changes, say so. Pitching replacement without the quality evidence to back it. It invites the hardest version of every trust question. In an interview If an interviewer hands you an AI feature to launch, cover four things in order: the claim you can prove, the demo, the controls the buyer gets, and the pricing. Interviewers want to see that you plan for skepticism instead of hoping enthusiasm wins. Self-check: Your AI feature resolved 80% of tickets in a pilot with one customer. Marketing wants \"80% resolution rate\""}, {"id": "pmm/launches/tier-these-launches", "text": "The facts. Seven real releases from 2026 appear in the changelog of Linear, the project-tracking tool for software teams: Date Release What it does Late May Linear Diffs Review code from an issue with a linked pull request, make follow-up changes with a background coding agent, and ship from inside Linear. On all plans, with guided reviews in beta for Business and Enterprise Jun 11 Coding sessions An agent writes code in the cloud from inside Linear. On Basic, Business and Enterprise plans, using AI credits Aug 13 Team initiatives Teams can lead initiatives, and those led by private teams are visible only to their members Aug 20 Coding sessions: environments, browser use and updated pricing Configurable environments, browser testing by the agent, and a cheaper usage-based pricing model Sep 3 Priority inbox A Priority tab separates urgent notifications from the rest Nov 12 Bring your own key Teams can use their own AI provider keys for the coding agent and be billed by the provider directly Nov 12 A small command-menu improvement Exact-match commands now rank above issues with the same title in the command menu Your task: You're a PMM at Linear. Assign each release a tier (1, 2 or 3), say in one line what it gets, and name one pair of releases you'd bundle into a single launch moment. +++ reveal Sample answer Release Tier What it gets Coding sessions 1 Full launch: a new way to use Linear and a new source of usage revenue. Launch post, demo video, press, sales and success briefing, in-app announcement Linear Diffs 1, bundled Launch it with coding sessions as one story about doing the whole job from issue to shipped code Environments and updated pricing 2 A pricing change needs direct notice to every affected customer, plus a sales and support update. The new features ride along Bring your own key 2 Matters most to larger companies with security and procurement rules. Brief sales and success, and email admins of eligible plans Priority inbox 2 or 3 An in-app message for existing users and a changelog entry. Tier 2 only if notification overload is a common complaint Team initiatives 3 Changelog entry, with a note to admins of private teams Command menu improvement 3 Release notes only The bundle: Diffs and coding sessions shipped two weeks apart and tell one story, so they make a stronger single moment than two separate announcements. Why it works It tiers by impact on customers and revenue, not by how big each engineering project was. It treats the pricing change as more than a feature update, because it changes what customers pay. It uses bundling to make the Tier 1 story bigger instead of launching everything loudly. What interviewers push on \"Why isn't the pricing update Tier 1?\" It changes the price of an existing feature rather than opening something new. It could become Tier 1 if many customers' bills rise. \"Isn't bring your own key a small technical change?\" Small to build, but it can unblock deals with security-minded buyers, which is impact. Practice twist Linear's sales team says bring your own key is the top request from enterprise prospects this quarter. Does its tier change, and what would you add to the plan?"}, {"id": "pmm/launches/choose-the-motion", "text": "The facts. Cloudflare's website and network plans run from Free ($0) to Pro ($25 a month, or $20 billed annually) and Business ($250 a month, or $200 annually), all bought online, plus custom Contract plans, billed annually and sold through sales. At the end of 2025, Cloudflare had about 332,000 paying customers in more than 190 countries, and 4,298 large customers paying more than $100,000 a year, up from 2,756 two years earlier. Self-serve customers sign up on the website or through hosting partners and pay by card without talking to sales. Enterprise customers are sold through inside and field sales teams and channel partners, and a substantial majority of 2025 revenue came from those contracted customers. On July 1, 2025, Cloudflare launched Pay per crawl, which lets website owners allow, charge or block each AI crawler that visits their site, at a flat price per request, with Cloudflare collecting the payments. Your task: You're the PMM for Pay per crawl, before launch. Decide how it should reach customers first: self-serve to every plan, sales-led to large customers, or partner-led through hosting partners. Defend your choice, and say what would make you add the other motions later. +++ reveal Sample answer Lead with sales. The sites AI companies most want to crawl, such as large publishers and media companies, are the ones with valuable content, and many of them are likely among Cloudflare's large enterprise customers. Charging AI crawlers is new, so the first customers need help setting prices and policies, and their legal teams will want a say. That's a conversation for an account team, not a sign-up page. Add self-serve later. Once a few large sites show what prices work and how crawlers respond, sensible defaults can go to the self-serve plans, where most of Cloudflare's customers are. Hosting partners come after that, for the long tail of small sites. What Cloudflare actually did: it launched Pay per crawl as a private beta with two ways in: a public sign-up form for interested publishers and crawlers, and the account executive for existing Enterprise customers. Why it works It starts with the customers for whom the product is worth the most, and with the motion that can handle a new, judgment-heavy decision. It uses the company's real mix: most customers self-serve, but most revenue comes through sales. It names the trigger for adding motions: proven prices and defaults. What interviewers push on \"Why not open it to all paying customers at once for maximum reach?\" Small sites get few valuable crawler requests, and a confusing new pricing decision could turn into support tickets instead of revenue. \"What would make you lead with self-serve instead?\" If the product could set good prices automatically and most of the value sat with small sites. Practice twist Now the product is a usage-priced developer tool with a free tier, aimed at individual engineers. Which motion leads, and when would sales get involved?"}, {"id": "pmm/launches/set-the-launch-goals", "text": "The facts. On October 29, 2025, Canva relaunched Affinity, its professional app for photo editing, vector design and page layout, as completely free. New users need a free Canva account to download it. Canva premium subscribers get Canva's AI tools inside Affinity, and projects can be exported to Canva to collaborate and publish. On November 3, Canva said more than one million people had signed up for Affinity in four days. It explained that its model is to offer powerful tools free and charge for advanced features such as premium content, collaboration and AI, and that it has more than 28 million paying customers and $3.5 billion in annualized revenue. Your task: It's October 2025, before launch, and you're the PMM for Affinity. Set one leading goal for the first four weeks and one lagging goal for the first year. Include the metric, target, date, baseline and owner, and estimate the numbers you don't have. Write the failure condition: what result in week four would make you change the plan? After launch, Canva announced one million sign-ups in four days. Is that a good measure of success? What would you want to know next? +++ reveal Sample answer Leading goal: the share of new Affinity users who save a real project and come back in their second week, with a target of 30% by the end of November and the growth lead as owner. Sign-ups are tracked, but they aren't the goal. Lagging goal: the number of Affinity users who become paying Canva customers, through Canva AI inside Affinity or by moving work into Canva to collaborate, measured over the first 12 months against Canva's normal conversion rate for new free users. Failure condition: sign-ups beat expectations but fewer than one in five new users returns in week two. That would suggest people tried it out of curiosity, and that the onboarding or the fit with professional work needs fixing before more promotion. On the million sign-ups: it's a strong signal of demand and a good press number, but it's exactly the kind of metric Ries warns about. It doesn't show whether professional designers switched their work to Affinity, or whether any of those users will pay Canva. Next you'd want week-two return rates, the share who are professional designers, and early conversion to Canva premium. Why it works It ties the goals to how Canva actually makes money from a free product: accounts, then paid AI and collaboration in Canva. It separates a press metric (sign-ups) from a decision metric (return and conversion). It sets a failure condition in advance, so the team knows when to change course. What interviewers push on \"Why not just aim for more sign-ups?\" Sign-ups were never the business goal. A free product that nobody uses for real work costs money and builds no revenue. \"You estimated the targets. Is that a problem?\" No, as long as you say so and set the baseline from Canva's own data on new free users before launch. Practice twist Canva's leadership decides the real goal is winning professional designers from paid tools. Rewrite both goals for that audience."}, {"id": "pmm/launches/get-a-new-tier-past-its-early-adopters", "text": "The facts. Box sells cloud content management to businesses. In its fiscal 2026, Box launched Enterprise Advanced, its top tier, which adds advanced AI, automated workflows and secure content management. About a year later, Box said Enterprise Advanced customers already made up 10% of its revenue. Box told investors that customers moving up to Enterprise Advanced paid an average of 30% to 40% more per seat than they did on Enterprise Plus, the tier below. Its net retention rate was 104% at the end of fiscal 2026, up from 102% a year earlier. In Rogers' adoption curve, innovators and early adopters together make up 16% of adopters, and the early majority another 34%. Your task: You're the PMM for Enterprise Advanced. The first year's buyers were the customers most excited about AI. Next year's growth has to come from mainstream Enterprise Plus customers who are cautious about AI and about paying 30% to 40% more. Describe who the next wave is and what's stopping them, using know, can and want. Name the proof they need and where it comes from. Plan two adoption moves for customers who already bought the tier, and one upgrade move for those who haven't. Pick the metric that tells you the tier is reaching the mainstream. +++ reveal Sample answer 1. The next wave: Enterprise Plus customers in regulated or cautious industries. They know the tier exists, so \"know\" isn't the gap. The gaps are \"want,\" because they aren't sure the AI is worth 30% to 40% more, and \"can,\" because they doubt they can roll it out safely with their content and permissions. 2. The proof: results from first-year customers in similar industries, such as hours saved on a specific workflow, plus a clear account of how the AI handles permissions and sensitive content. Peer proof matters more than feature lists to this group. 3. The moves: Adoption, for existing buyers: templates for the two or three workflows with the clearest payoff, offered in the product when a customer does that task, plus a usage summary for each account's business review so the buyer can show the upgrade paid off. Adoption, for existing buyers: a customer advisory group of first-year champions whose results become the case studies. Upgrade, for those who haven't bought: a time-limited trial of one AI workflow on the customer's own content, run with their customer success manager, ending in a business review that compares results with the price difference. 4. The metric: the share of Enterprise Advanced revenue coming from customers outside the first-year industries, alongside renewal rates for first-year buyers. If first-year customers renew and expand, the proof for the mainstream gets stronger. Why it works It uses know, can and want to find the real gap instead of defaulting to more awareness. It turns early adopters' results into the proof the early majority needs. It ties adoption inside existing accounts to the next wave of upgrades. What interviewers push on \"Why not just discount the upgrade?\" It lowers the barrier once, but it doesn't answer whether the AI is worth it, and it trains customers to wait. \"How do you know the first-year results are real?\" Use the customers' own usage data and agree on the measure with them, then let them tell the story. Practice twist First-year renewals come in and a third of Enterprise Advanced customers want to move back down to Enterprise Plus. What do you investigate first, and what does it change about the plan for the next wave?"}, {"id": "pmm/launches/launch-an-ai-feature-buyers-don-t-trust-yet", "text": "The facts. In October 2026, Intercom launched Fin 4, the latest version of its AI agent for customer service. It added proactive conversations with customers, an agent for back-office tasks, and \"Human in the Loop,\" which uses a confidence score to decide when Fin should ask a teammate to approve, edit or take over. Fin's site says its resolution rate averages 76% across more than 12,000 customers. Fin is priced at $0.99 per \"outcome.\" An outcome counts when the customer confirms the issue is solved, stops asking after Fin answers, or Fin completes a defined workflow. There's no charge when Fin can't answer or the customer asks for a human, and a reopened conversation is refunded. In July 2026, Intercom changed how it reports Fin's performance. Automation rate equals involvement rate times resolution rate. Conversations where Fin was active but never had a chance to answer, for example because escalation rules sent them to a person, stopped counting as \"involved.\" In Intercom's own example of 1,000 conversations, the resolution rate rises from 33% to 50% while the automation rate stays at 25%. Intercom says the number of resolutions and billing don't change. Your task: You're launching Fin 4 to heads of support at mid-size companies. Many tried an AI chatbot before and got burned by wrong answers. Write: The headline claim, and the evidence behind it. The demo plan. The two controls you'd lead with. How you'd present the 76% figure, given the July 2026 change. The offer that gets a skeptical buyer to try it. +++ reveal Sample answer 1. Headline claim: \"Fin handles the routine questions and hands the rest to your team, and you only pay when it resolves one.\" The evidence is the outcome-based pricing itself plus a pilot on the buyer's own conversations. It leads with control and fair pricing, not with a percentage. 2. Demo plan: run it on a sample of the buyer's real past tickets, including the messy ones. Show one case where Fin isn't confident and Human in the Loop sends it to a person. Then show the reporting, with the metric definitions on screen. 3. Controls to lead with: Human in the Loop, because the burned buyer's fear is a wrong answer going out unchecked, and the pricing rules, because no charge when Fin can't answer or the customer asks for a human puts much of the risk on the vendor. 4. The 76% figure: use it carefully, with its definition. First confirm which definition the 76% uses, then say so, and pair it with the metric a head of support budgets around: the automation rate across all conversations. Hiding the definition change would hand a competitor an easy attack, and a buyer who later spots the jump in their own dashboard would stop trusting every number. 5. The offer: a time-limited pilot on one queue of routine questions, with a spending cap and a pre-agreed success measure, such as the automation rate on that queue after 30 days. Why it works It answers the burned buyer's real fear, wrong answers going out unchecked, before talking about scale. It uses the pricing model as a trust signal, not just a revenue model. It treats metric definitions as part of the message, which is exactly where AI claims tend to break. What interviewers push on \"Isn't 76% your strongest selling point? Why downplay it?\" It's strong, but it's easy to misread. Pairing it with the automation rate keeps it credible after the buyer's own data comes in. \"Won't 'only pay when it works' cut revenue?\" It can lower revenue per conversation early, but it removes the main reason not to start, and usage grows as trust does. Practice twist A competitor's homepage claims a 90% resolution rate with no definition. Write the two-sentence answer your sales team should give when a prospect brings it up."}, {"id": "pmm/pricing/value-based-pricing-and-willingness-to-pay", "text": "Here's a number that should make any PMM care about pricing. In a 2003 analysis, McKinsey modeled the income statement of an average S&P 1500 company, a 1% price increase, with volume holding steady, raised operating profit by 8%. That's nearly 50% more than a 1% cut in variable costs, and more than three times the effect of selling 1% more. McKinsey's more recent work puts the typical gain from a 1% price increase at 6% to 14% of operating profit. Even so, most companies don't price from value. As IMD professor Stefan Michel puts it, practitioners and researchers broadly agree that value-based pricing earns higher profits than pricing from costs or competitors, yet study after study finds those simpler methods are still the norm. Three ways to land on a price OpenStax's marketing textbook describes three starting points: Cost-based: add up what it costs to make and sell the product, then add a margin. It's simple, but customers don't know or care what your costs are, so the price may have nothing to do with what they think it's worth. Competition-based: price at, below or above rivals. The textbook's example is a retailer that tracks competitors' prices and sets its own just below them. Value-based: start from the benefits customers get, learned through research, and set the price from there. Each one answers a different question. Your costs tell you the lowest price you can survive on. Competitors tell you what buyers are used to paying. Value tells you the most a customer would pay. Your price lives somewhere between that floor and that ceiling. Value-based pricing means starting from the ceiling and working down, instead of starting from costs and working up. What a product is worth depends on the alternative Kellogg professor Eric Anderson explains it with a beer. You might pay $10 or more for your favorite beer at a fancy hotel bar and only $5 for the same beer from a beachside vendor, and that can be completely rational, because the alternatives in front of you are different. Simply asking people what they'd pay, he argues, misses the comparisons they're making. Willingness to pay is the most a customer would pay, and it's never a fixed property of the product. It moves with whatever else the buyer could do instead. For software, that alternative might be a rival product, a spreadsheet, an agency, an extra hire or doing nothing at all. That gives you a simple way to estimate value: Value to the customer = what the next-best alternative costs + what your differences are worth to them Differences can add or subtract. Saving a team five hours a week adds value. A painful migration takes some away. Here's how the math might look, with illustrative numbers. A support team pays $15 per agent a month for its current tool. Yours saves each agent two hours a month, and the company values an agent's hour at $30. The alternative is worth $15, and the difference is worth $60, so the ceiling is about $75 per agent a month. You wouldn't charge all of it. Customers need a reason to switch and switching takes effort, so a price of $30 to $40 leaves them a clear gain. One caution: finance teams often discount saved hours, so hard savings, like a tool retired or a hire avoided, make a stronger case. Michel also names the catch. The hardest part of value-based pricing is measuring that difference: customers have to understand their own processes well enough to see what one option saves compared with the next-best alternative, and the research to find out costs money. Different customers, different prices If willingness to pay varies from customer to customer, one price leaves money on the table with some and scares others away. Spotify's US plans show one way around that: Plan Price a month Who it's for Individual $12.99 One account Student $6.99 Verified students at accredited colleges Duo $18.99 Two people at the same address Family $21.99 Up to six accounts at the same address The music is the same in every plan. What changes is who's paying and how much they're likely to afford. Students usually have less money, so they pay about half, but only after verification. Duo and Family let a household pay more in total while each person pays less. Student verification and the shared address are fences: conditions that stop everyone else from claiming the cheaper price. Worked example: GoToMeeting's flat rate Before launching GoToMeeting, Jim Semick's team ran dozens of interviews with potential customers. What they heard shaped the price as much as the product. They launched at $49 for all-you-can-meet, a flat rate that was unusual in conferencing, because the interviews showed customers valued a price structure that was easy to understand. Read it as a value-based decision: The research came first. The team didn't pick a number and write messaging around it. It learned what customers valued, then built the price to match. Predictability had value of its own. A bill that never surprised anyone was part of what customers were buying. How you charge is part of the price. Flat, per seat or per use can add value or destroy it, separately from how much you charge. Common traps Pricing from cost by default. Your costs set the floor, not the price. One price for everyone when customers differ a lot in what the product is worth to them. Charging the whole ceiling. If you capture all the value, customers have no reason to switch. Ignoring the do-nothing option. For many products, the real competitor is the spreadsheet or the old process. In an interview For \"How would you price X?\", start with the customer and their alternative, not your costs or your competitors. Estimate the value of the difference in rough numbers, say where you'd set the price between the floor and the ceiling and why, and explain how you'd check it with research. To hear a full pricing case worked through out loud, here's a mock interview with consultants from McKinsey and EY. It's consulting style, so expect more math than most PMM loops "}, {"id": "pmm/pricing/packaging-good-better-best-freemium-and-usage-ba", "text": "When Dropbox filed to go public in 2018, it had more than 500 million registered users in 180 countries. Only about 11 million of them paid. The free version, Dropbox Basic, wasn't a leak in that funnel. It was the funnel: Dropbox called it \"a major funnel\" for its paid plans, and more than 90% of its revenue came from people who bought through the app or website on their own. The gap held: by the end of 2025, Dropbox served more than 700 million registered users, and 18.08 million of them paid. That split between free and paid was designed. So is every pricing page you've ever scrolled. Deciding what goes in each plan, what's free and what you charge for is called packaging. Price is the number. Packaging is everything the number buys. Why three plans usually beat one Rafi Mohammed opens his Harvard Business Review case for tiered pricing with car insurance. The industry long assumed drivers just wanted the cheapest plan. Allstate's research in the early 2000s found something more interesting: price mattered, but many drivers worried about their premium jumping after an accident, and drivers with clean records wanted credit for it. One price can't answer all of those worries. A few tiers can. That's the idea behind good-better-best. Instead of one product at one price, you offer a basic version, a fuller one and a premium one, so each kind of buyer finds a plan built for them. Three also works because of how people choose. Researchers call it the compromise effect: an option gains share when it sits in the middle of a set instead of at one end. Shown three plans, plenty of buyers skip the cheapest because it feels risky and the priciest because it feels like too much. So build the middle plan as the one you most want people to buy, and let the top plan make it look reasonable. What goes in which plan Naming three plans is easy. Deciding which features sit behind which price is the real work. A useful rule: put a feature in a higher plan when it's worth a lot to the customers who'd pay more and very little to everyone else. Those features work as fences. They keep the customers who need them from buying down. Zapier, the automation tool, shows the pattern: Plan Price (billed yearly) Built for What it adds Free $0 Anyone trying their first automations Two-step Zaps Professional From $19.99 a month Solo builders Multi-step Zaps, premium apps, webhooks Team From $69 a month Small teams 25 users, shared Zaps and folders, shared app connections, SAML SSO Enterprise Contact sales Large organizations Unlimited users, advanced admin controls, a technical account manager Look at what Team adds. Nothing on that list helps one person automate more. It's all sharing, security and control, which only matter once several people depend on the same workflows. Single sign-on is the classic fence: the IT team at a 200-person company insists on it, and a freelancer never thinks about it. It's also a common complaint, sometimes called the \"SSO tax,\" so weigh the extra revenue against annoying security-minded smaller customers. Zapier also prices along a second line. Every plan comes in task tiers, from 100 tasks a month up to 2 million. Hit your limit and you switch to pay-as-you-go, unless you turn that off or move up a tier, and the higher the tier, the less each task costs. The plan sets what you can do. The tier sets how much of it. Free: a funnel or a giveaway? Every free plan raises the same worry: will people who would have paid just stay free? Deng, Lambrecht and Liu tested that on game apps in Apple's App Store that added a free version next to an existing paid one. Apple's review process made each free launch date hard to predict, so the timing worked like a natural experiment. In that market, the free version didn't eat the paid one. It let people sample the game first, and demand for the paid version went up, with its daily ratings rising 8.9% at the average. Dropbox's free plan worked for a similar reason. It was useful on its own, and its limit grew with use: the more you stored and shared, the closer you got to needing more space. A good free plan passes three tests: It's useful by itself, so people keep it and bring others in. Its limit grows with value, such as storage, tasks or users, so the people who hit it are the ones getting the most out of the product. It's cheap to serve, because most free users will never pay. Usage-based: charge for what customers use Some products don't fit neat plans at all. Twilio sells the building blocks for texts, calls and email inside other companies' apps, and the majority of its revenue is usage-based. Most of those customers have no long-term commitment and can cut back or stop at any time without penalty. That cuts both ways. When customers grow, revenue grows with them, no sales call needed. Twilio tracks this with a dollar-based net expansion rate, which compares what existing customers spend now with what they spent a year earlier, and for its Communications business the rate was 105% in 2024. When customers shrink, revenue shrinks too, and Twilio lists exactly that among its risks. The big decision in usage pricing is the value metric, the unit you charge for. A good one: Grows as the customer gets more value, like messages sent, tasks run or records stored. Is easy for a buyer to understand and estimate before the bill arrives. Tracks your own costs closely enough that heavy users don't lose you money. AI features push many products toward meters, because every request costs something to run. Many companies settle on a hybrid: a plan with an included amount, plus pay-as-you-go above it. Zapier's task tiers are exactly that. Picking a model Treat this as a rule of thumb, not a law: Model Works best when Watch out for Good-better-best Buyers differ a lot in what they need and will pay Too many tiers, or fences nobody cares about Freemium The product spreads through use and is cheap to serve A free plan so generous nobody upgrades Usage-based Value r"}, {"id": "pmm/pricing/pricing-research-and-price-changes", "text": "Ask streaming subscribers whether a $5 price hike would make them cancel their favorite service, and 60% say they'd likely do it, according to Deloitte's Digital Media Trends survey. The average subscribing household already pays for four services. Stated answers like that aren't a hard ceiling, but they're a clear warning about how much patience customers have left. Price changes are where pricing theory meets real customers, and they go better when the research comes first and the change arrives in a shape people can absorb. Why \"what would you pay?\" fails Kellogg researchers Sharlene He, Eric Anderson and Derek Rucker point out that many studies have measured willingness to pay by simply asking people what they'd spend, with no details about the situation or the alternatives. Their fix, the Comparative Method of Valuation, measures value against the options a buyer would actually weigh. A KitKat in a vending machine competes with other candy bars. In a grocery store, it also competes with apples and crackers. The researchers found that conjoint analysis, another popular method, is an efficient way to explore many contexts at once, while their comparative method does better when the alternatives are clear. The takeaway: research price in context, against the alternatives buyers really have. Ways to research willingness to pay Van Westendorp's price sensitivity meter. Peter van Westendorp introduced it in 1976, and it's been a market research staple ever since. It asks four questions: At what price would this be so expensive you wouldn't consider it? At what price would it be so cheap you'd doubt the quality? At what price is it getting expensive, but you'd still consider it? At what price is it a bargain? Plot everyone's answers, and the points where the curves cross mark out a range of acceptable prices. Researcher Mike Pritchard warns against treating any single crossing as the \"optimal\" price, since pricing research is usually just one input to the decision. He also contrasts this direct approach with indirect ones like conjoint, which show people whole packages of features and prices and work out from their choices how much the price matters. Testing in the market. The strongest evidence is what people actually pay. Offer a new price to new customers in one region or channel, then compare conversion and retention with everyone else. Each method answers a different question: Method What it tells you Watch out for Asking directly A rough gut check People answer without alternatives in mind Van Westendorp A range of acceptable prices Stated answers, with competitors left out of the frame Conjoint What each feature and the price are worth, relative to each other Needs a careful design and a decent sample Comparative method Value against the specific alternatives buyers weigh You have to know the real alternatives first Market test What people really pay Slower, and customers notice when prices differ Raising prices without a revolt Kellogg's Suraj Malladi explains why so many companies raise prices in small steps. Firms act as if demand is \"kinked\": cutting prices barely brings in more customers, but raising them can drive customers away fast. For businesses that set one price and change it rarely, he says, the order of price changes matters. A price increase plan answers six questions: Question What good looks like Why now? New value customers can see, or costs they'd recognize How much? Small enough to absorb; big jumps invite comparison shopping Who's affected? New customers first, or existing customers with time to adjust Where can they land? A cheaper plan to step down to instead of canceling How will they hear? Early, plain notice from you, not a surprise on a bill What will you watch? Cancellations, downgrades, support tickets and win rates Worked example: Disney+ in 2025 On September 23, 2025, Disney announced price increases starting October 21. The ad-supported Disney+ plan went up $2 to $11.99 a month, and the ad-free Premium plan rose $3 to $18.99, or $30 a year to $189.99. Bundles rose $2 or $3, and NFL+ prices stayed put. Disney had hinted at the increases on its earnings call, and its previous round, in October 2024, raised most plans by $1 to $2. The timing was rough: the news arrived in a week when some fans were already posting about canceling over an unrelated programming decision. A year later, Disney did it again: in September 2026, ad-free Disney+ rose $2.50 to $21.49, its sixth US increase in six years. Run it through the six questions: How much: every increase stayed at $3 a month or less, below the $5 that most surveyed subscribers said would likely make them cancel. Treat that survey as a rough guide, not proof, since stated answers aren't the same as behavior. In percentage terms, though, the Premium increase was still about 19%. Where they land: the ad-supported plan stayed much cheaper than Premium, so price-sensitive subscribers had somewhere to go besides the exit. Cadence: about once a year, in steps, which fits the gradual approach Malladi describes. Disney did it again in 2026. Timing: some of it is out of your hands. A good plan includes what you'll do if an increase hits during bad news. Common traps Trusting stated answers. What people say they'd pay and what they actually pay often differ. Researching price without alternatives. Value only makes sense next to the options buyers weigh. An unexplained big jump. A large increase with no new value attached invites customers to rethink the whole purchase. Raising prices with nothing new to point to. Customers accept increases more easily when they can see what changed. In an interview For \"How would you raise prices for X?\", walk through why now, how much, who's affected, where customers can step down to, how you'll tell them and what you'll watch. Then name the research you'd run first, and be honest about what it can't tell you. Self-check: A B2B tool plans to raise prices 40% for every customer next "}, {"id": "pmm/pricing/price-a-new-add-on", "text": "The facts. Airtable's self-serve plans, billed annually, are Free, Team at $20 per seat a month and Business at $45 per seat a month. Enterprise Scale is sold through sales. Airtable AI gives customers access to models from developers including OpenAI, Google, Anthropic, Meta and IBM. Airtable has sold it to Team and Business customers as a per-seat add-on. Every AI request costs money to run, and use inside a workspace tends to be uneven: a few people may do most of the AI work while others barely touch it. Your task: Put yourself in spring 2025, when Airtable still sold AI as a per-seat add-on. You're the PMM for Airtable AI. Decide how AI should be priced from here: keep the per-seat add-on, include unlimited AI in every plan, or try something else. Say what each plan includes, what extra use costs and how you'd move current add-on customers over. +++ reveal Sample answer Include a monthly allowance in every plan, and sell extra by usage. Per seat is the wrong meter. A customer pays for every seat even if two people do all the AI work, so many customers skip it. Unlimited AI has the opposite problem, because heavy users cost real money. Plans: give every plan a pool of AI credits that grows with the plan, so Business gets more than Team. Share the pool across the workspace so heavy users can draw on it. Extra use: sell credit packs at one simple rate. Free: a small allowance that shows the value, with more only after upgrading. Current customers: refund the unused part of the add-on, so nobody pays twice during the switch. What Airtable actually did Starting June 24, 2025, Airtable stopped charging Team and Business customers for AI per seat, and refunded unused add-on time as billing credits. Every plan now includes monthly AI credits: 500 per editor on Free, 15,000 per paid user on Team, 20,000 on Business and 25,000 on Enterprise Scale. Credits are pooled across the workspace or organization. Extra credits come in packs from 10,000 to 200,000 a month at $2 per 1,000, and Free customers have to upgrade to Team to buy more. Why it works The meter matches the cost. Credits track actual AI use, not headcount. Pooling fits how teams work. One power user and ten light users can share the same allowance. AI makes the higher plans better. The included allowance grows by plan instead of being a separate purchase. Free stays a funnel. Free customers get a taste, and buying more requires a paid plan. What interviewers push on \"Won't usage pricing make bills unpredictable?\" That's why there's a generous included pool and fixed-size packs, not open-ended metering. \"Why not keep the add-on and cut its price?\" A cheaper per-seat price still charges for people who never use AI. \"How would you set the included amounts?\" Look at how much AI a typical workspace uses now, and size the pools so most customers never need a pack. Practice twist Now the AI feature does one specific job, like an agent that answers customer questions on its own. Would you still sell credits, or charge for each question it resolves?"}, {"id": "pmm/pricing/redesign-the-plans", "text": "The facts. In April 2024, Salesforce made Slack AI available to every paid Slack customer as a paid add-on, at $10 per user a month on the Pro and Business+ plans. It included search answers with citations, channel and thread summaries, and a daily recap of channels a user follows. Salesforce said customers were saving an average of 97 minutes per user a week, based on its own internal analysis. It also cited Slack research showing that while 94% of executives called AI an urgent priority, only one in four desk workers had tried AI tools at work. AI features cost money to run every time someone uses them. Your task: It's early 2025. Leadership wants far more Slack customers using AI, without simply giving away what makes it valuable. Redesign the plans: which AI features belong in which plan, what happens to the $10 add-on and whether any prices change. Defend your choices. +++ reveal Sample answer Put a taste of AI in every paid plan and the heavier features in the plans above it. Every paid plan: summaries. They're easy to understand, useful on day one and build the habit, which is the real problem when only one in four workers has even tried AI. Business+: the features bigger teams care about most, such as search across everything, daily recaps and workflow automation. Raise the plan's price, but by less than the $10 add-on, so a customer who'd have bought both pays less than before. A new top plan: company-wide search and governance for the largest customers, sold through sales. The add-on: stop selling it, and let current buyers keep it until renewal so nobody loses features mid-contract. What Slack actually did On June 17, 2025, in its first pricing change since 2022, Slack folded AI into its paid plans. Pro kept its price and gained core AI features. Business+ rose from $12.50 to $15 per user a month on annual billing ($15 to $18 on monthly billing) and gained advanced AI. A new Enterprise+ plan added enterprise search. The $10 add-on stopped being sold on Slack's website, and customers who had it kept it until their first renewal after August 17, 2025. Summaries and huddle notes now come with every paid plan. Search, recaps, translations, file summaries and workflow automation come with Business+ and Enterprise+, and enterprise search is Enterprise+ only. Why it works AI becomes a reason to pick a plan, not a separate purchase most customers skip. The math favors AI buyers. The $2.50 Business+ increase is a quarter of the old add-on price, so customers who wanted AI pay less, while everyone on the plan pays a little more. Summaries in Pro go after the adoption problem with the easiest feature to try. The costliest feature gets its own price. Search across a whole company's tools lives in the new top plan. What interviewers push on \"Business+ customers who never wanted AI now pay 20% more. Won't some leave?\" Some may step down to Pro, which is why Pro got core AI and kept its price. \"Why not make all AI free everywhere?\" Running it isn't free, and the advanced features are what justify the higher plans. Practice twist Your company sells a design tool with Starter, Pro and Enterprise plans, and it's launching an AI image generator that's expensive to run. Which plan gets it, and do you meter it?"}, {"id": "pmm/pricing/plan-a-price-increase", "text": "The facts. Going into October 2025, Xbox Game Pass had three main tiers: Core at $10 a month, Standard at $15 and Ultimate at $20, after Ultimate went up from $17 in July 2024. PC Game Pass cost $12. Microsoft was about to add more to Ultimate: Ubisoft+ (about $16 a month on its own), Fortnite Crew from November (usually about $12 a month), at least 75 day-one games a year and better cloud streaming. Sony's PlayStation Plus Premium cost $160 a year on an annual plan. New partner content costs money to license, so the Ultimate price is going up. Your task: You're the PMM for Game Pass in September 2025. Plan the Ultimate price increase: how big, how fast, who's protected, what you say and what you'll watch. +++ reveal Sample answer Raise it, but not all at once. How much: move Ultimate to around $25 now, with room to go higher once the new content proves itself. A one-step jump of 50% invites every subscriber to rethink the purchase. Who's protected: add a discounted annual or quarterly option, and give current members a few months at their old price. Where they land: keep Standard at $15, so price-sensitive players step down instead of canceling. What you say: lead with the new value in dollars. Ubisoft+ and Fortnite Crew alone cost about $28 a month bought separately. What you watch: cancellations, downgrades and annual renewals, weekly for the first two months. What Xbox actually did On October 1, 2025, Microsoft raised Ultimate to $30 a month, 50% more, with no annual or quarterly option. That was a 76% rise in 15 months, from $17. Core and Standard were renamed Essential and Premium at unchanged prices, and PC Game Pass went from $12 to $16.50. Ultimate got the new content, and the Game Pass cancellation page appeared to be overloaded that day. Less than seven months later, on April 21, 2026, Xbox cut Ultimate to $22.99 and PC Game Pass to $13.99. It also said future Call of Duty games would no longer join those plans at launch, arriving about a year later instead. Xbox said the change responded to a lot of feedback. What you can learn from it One big jump gave subscribers a reason to cancel. That's the reaction a gradual approach is meant to avoid. Offering no annual or quarterly option left loyal members no cushion to soften the increase. The reversal came with a trade. The price fell, but day-one Call of Duty went away. Cutting a price usually means cutting value too. Real value can still get drowned out. The new content was substantial, but the size of the jump became the story. What interviewers push on \"Isn't $30 still a good deal for all that content?\" Maybe on paper, but customers judge an increase against what they paid last month, not against a bundle they never bought. \"Why not raise it and accept the churn?\" Lost subscribers are expensive to win back, and the April 2026 cut suggests the trade didn't pay off. Practice twist You're raising the price of a B2B tool by 15% for existing customers on annual contracts. How does the plan change when customers renew once a year and a procurement team reads every notice?"}, {"id": "pmm/enablement/what-sales-needs-from-pmm-and-how-to-measure-it", "text": "In a 2026 Gartner survey of 646 B2B buyers, 67% said they'd prefer to buy without a sales rep at all. So the time a rep does get with a buyer is scarce, and it has to count. Reps don't have much time to spare either. In Salesforce's 2026 State of Sales survey of 4,050 sales professionals, the average seller spent 40% of their time actually selling. That's the real brief for sales enablement: make the few minutes a rep gets with a buyer count, and take enough busywork off the rep's plate that they get more of those minutes. What reps need, stage by stage Sales enablement covers everything that helps reps sell better, from onboarding and training to content and tools. Product marketing usually owns the product and market side: what to say, who to say it to, how to beat competitors and what proof to show. The easiest way to see what reps need is to walk through a deal: Stage What the rep is trying to do What PMM gives them Finding accounts Pick who to call The customer profiles that fit, and signals that an account is ready First call Earn a second meeting A short talk track: the problem, who it's for, why now Discovery Learn what the buyer needs Questions that surface the problems your product solves Demo Show it working A demo flow that opens with the result the buyer cares about Evaluation Survive the comparison Battlecards, objection answers and proof Decision Help the buyer sell it internally A business case, pricing guidance and references Notice how little of that is a slide deck. Most of it is an answer to a question a buyer asks at a specific moment. Worked example: PostHog's playbook PostHog, which makes product analytics and other tools for software teams, publishes its company handbook online, including how its product marketers do enablement. There, product marketers make the sales material for the products they own. A few of their rules: Start with the hardest product to sell. They ask the go-to-market team to rank products from easiest to hardest to sell, then work on the hardest first. Visual products like session replay are easier to pitch. Products with little to show on screen, like a data warehouse, need the help. Give signals, not call lists. Reps know their accounts better than marketing does. So instead of telling them who to call, PMMs explain which customer profiles fit and which signals show an account matches: rising use of a related product, a fresh funding round, a new hire in the right role. Explain why it's worth selling. A rough number for what a customer might spend helps reps see how the product gets them to quota. Demo the result, not the setup. For a product that's hard to show, demo the answer it gives. Watching data sync convinces nobody. Training follows the same logic. One PostHog session had 15 to 20 minutes of content and 45 minutes of questions, and the questions turned out to be the most useful part, because they showed what reps didn't understand and what to write next. The handbook even asks PMMs to write material that the AI agents reps use to prepare for calls can read. Partners need enablement too Sometimes the people selling your product don't work for you. A 2026 Stanford GSB case on Cumulus Networks, a networking software start-up, shows the price. Partners could close the gap between Cumulus's software and what corporate IT teams could set up on their own, but each partner cost a share of every sale plus months of training, joint selling and engineering support from a small team. A partner channel multiplies your enablement work. Each partner's reps need the same talk tracks, demos and objection answers your own team gets, while they juggle other vendors' products at the same time. Play video: Everything you need to know about sales enablement in 5 minutes Everything you need to know about sales enablement in 5 minutes Sales Enablement Collective, Oct 2024 · 5 min · YouTube How to tell if it's working Enablement is easy to do and hard to prove. In a 2023 Gartner survey, 77% of sellers said they struggle to complete their assigned tasks efficiently, even at companies investing in enablement. Gartner's advice is to measure which specific behaviors enablement changed, then tie those changes to sales goals. Think of it as three questions, each harder to answer than the last. The examples use illustrative numbers: Question What to measure Example Did reps use it? Views, and which assets reps send to buyers 70% of reps opened the new battlecard in its first month Did it change how they sell? What happens on calls Call recordings show reps asking the new discovery questions Did it move deals? Win rate, deal speed, deal size, how fast new reps close a first deal Win rate against the main rival rose from 30% to 38% over two quarters The first row is the easiest to measure and tells you the least. The third is what leadership cares about, but deals take months and plenty besides your battlecard moves a win rate. Usage and behavior are your early signals, and results confirm them later. Common traps A content library nobody opens. If reps can't find it mid-call, it might as well not exist. Training once, at launch. Objections change as rivals respond, so the material has to keep up. Counting downloads as success. Usage shows interest, not impact. Writing for marketers. Reps need the answer to the buyer's question in a line or two, not your positioning document. In an interview For \"How would you enable sales for this launch?\", walk a deal from first call to close and name what reps need at each stage. Then say how you'd train them, how you'd collect what they hear from buyers, and the one or two numbers you'd watch to know it worked. Self-check: Your team built 40 assets for a launch. Three months later, reps have opened four of them and win rates haven't moved. What would you do? Ask reps which buyer questions they can't answer, and sit in on a few calls. The four assets they use show what's valuable, so improve those and retire most of the rest. T"}, {"id": "pmm/enablement/battlecards-and-objection-handling", "text": "This might surprise you: the US Federal Trade Commission encourages companies to name their competitors. Its policy on comparative advertising, on the books since 1979, says that disparaging a rival is permissible as long as it's truthful and not deceptive. A rival can still challenge a comparison it thinks is false, so have the proof in hand before the claim goes out. Permission isn't the same as good strategy, though. Wharton's Pinar Yildirim, with Gorkem Bostanci and Kinshuk Jerath, studied what negative advertising does to a market. The threat of attacks pushes companies toward safer, more similar products, which cuts variety and can slow innovation. Yildirim also points out the boomerang: if your product is much like a rival's and you highlight its weakness, you probably share the same flaw. A good battlecard strikes that balance. It helps a rep win against one specific rival, honestly, without turning the call into a takedown. What goes on a battlecard A battlecard is a one-page brief on a single competitor, written for a rep in the middle of a deal. It answers what a rep needs to know the moment a buyer says, \"We're also looking at X.\" A good one fits on a screen and covers six things. The example lines are illustrative: Section What it answers Example line Who they are Who they sell to and how they win \"Wins small teams on price and a fast setup\" Why we win Your two or three strongest differences, each with proof \"Customers like Y cut setup from six weeks to two\" Where they're strong What to concede honestly \"Their mobile app gets better reviews\" Questions to ask Discovery questions that bring out your strengths \"How will approvals work once you pass 50 users?\" Quick answers Responses to what they say about you \"They'll call us expensive. Here's the three-year cost.\" Proof Customers who switched, with numbers A reference customer in the buyer's industry The \"where they're strong\" row is the one teams often skip. A rep who admits a rival's real strength sounds believable when making the case for everything else. Objections aren't FAQs PostHog's public handbook draws a line worth borrowing. An FAQ is a question about the product, such as how pricing works. An objection is a reason a customer gives not to buy, such as \"We can build this ourselves for less.\" Objections are the valuable part. The handbook tells product marketers to collect them from customer calls and the sales team, keep updating battlecards as new ones appear, and answer honestly, even when the honest answer is \"not yet\". Most objections belong to a handful of families: Objection What's often behind it What answers it \"It's too expensive.\" The value isn't clear yet, or the budget sits with someone else The cost of the problem next to the price \"We're fine with what we have.\" Switching feels riskier than staying What staying costs, and a low-risk way to start \"We can build it ourselves.\" The upkeep is underestimated The full cost to build and maintain it \"Your competitor says...\" A rival planted a doubt Facts and proof, not a counterattack \"Security won't approve it.\" A real requirement, or a polite no Certifications and documents ready before anyone asks \"Not this quarter.\" No urgency, or no budget yet A reason the timing matters, or a smaller first step Handling one in the moment Clever comebacks rarely win. A short routine works better: Hear the whole thing. Cutting in tells the buyer you weren't listening. Ask what's behind it. \"Expensive compared with what?\" turns a wall into a problem you can work on. Answer with proof. A customer result or a document beats an adjective. Check that it landed. \"Does that cover it?\" An objection left unresolved comes back later, usually at the worst time. Teach, don't just defend The strongest objection handling starts before any objection comes up. In \"The End of Solution Sales,\" Brent Adamson, Matthew Dixon and Nicholas Toman of the Corporate Executive Board describe star reps who get in early with provocative ideas about what the customer should do, instead of waiting for the buyer to define the problem. They seek out skeptical change agents over friendly informants, and they end up selling insights, not just solutions. For a battlecard, that means going beyond rebuttals. Give reps one insight that reframes the buyer's problem in a way that plays to your strengths, so the comparison happens on your ground. Play video: Positioning, value, and objection handling Positioning, value, and objection handling April Dunford, Feb 2025 · 25 min · starts at 7:24 · YouTube Common traps The takedown card. Attacks make a rep sound defensive, and claims you can't back up can get you in trouble. The novel. If it doesn't fit on a screen, nobody reads it mid-call. The stale card. Rivals ship features and change prices. A six-month-old card can be flat wrong. Rebuttals without proof. \"That's not true\" convinces nobody. A customer's number does. In an interview If you're asked how you'd help sales beat a competitor, start with where deals are lost and why, using win/loss interviews and call recordings. Then describe the battlecard, how you'd keep it current and how you'd know it's working, such as win rate against that rival. Self-check: A rep wants a battlecard line saying the rival's product \"crashes constantly.\" You've seen a handful of complaints online. What do you do? Leave it out unless you can prove it. A claim about a rival has to be truthful and not deceptive, and a handful of complaints doesn't show \"constantly.\" Even a true attack can backfire, especially if your product shares the same weakness. Give the rep a discovery question about the buyer's reliability needs instead, plus proof of your own uptime."}, {"id": "pmm/enablement/running-competitive-intelligence", "text": "In March 2025, the HR software company Rippling sued its fiercest rival, Deel, with an allegation straight out of a spy novel. According to the lawsuit, Rippling suspected an employee of passing information to Deel. So it created a fake Slack channel, told key Deel executives its name along with a hint that it held embarrassing details about Deel, and waited to see whether the employee would search for it. The filing says the employee had viewed and downloaded information on how to compete against Deel, and had also seen material on sales calls and demos. Deel denied all legal wrongdoing. Then the accusations ran the other way. In a June 2025 filing, Deel alleged that a Rippling employee whose job title was Competitive Intelligence had spent six months impersonating a Deel customer to study its products. Rippling said it was looking into the allegations and that it's committed to fair competition. The case was still going in September 2026, when a federal judge rejected Deel's bid to strike the key witness's testimony and sent Deel's most serious counterclaims to arbitration. Deel has denied the spying claims. Look at what was allegedly worth taking: material on how to win deals against a rival. A legitimate competitive intelligence program produces exactly that every day, from sources nobody has to sneak into. Where good intelligence comes from Competitive intelligence, or CI, is the work of learning what rivals are doing and turning it into decisions your company can act on. Most of it is public. The FTC's antitrust guidance answers the obvious question directly: collecting price and other competitive information from public sources, such as trade journals, securities filings and press releases, and circulating it inside your company is fine. Each source tells you something different: Source What it tells you Watch out for Win/loss interviews Why buyers actually chose you or a rival Small samples, and buyers being polite Rivals' annual reports Strategy, segments and how they say they compete Written for investors, so it's polished Pricing pages and release notes What they charge and what they just shipped Pages change, so save dated copies Job postings Where they're investing next One posting isn't a strategy Your own sales calls What buyers are hearing from rivals Reps remember losses better than wins Trade shows Demos and how they pitch Ask openly, as yourself Annual reports are underrated. SEC rules ask public companies to describe their business, and that description may cover trends in market demand and competitive conditions. That's why many 10-Ks include a section on who the company competes with and on what basis, in its own words. Where the line is The lines are clearer than the Rippling and Deel fight makes them look. SCIP, the professional association for intelligence work, publishes a seven-point code of ethics. Two points carry most of the weight: comply with all applicable laws, and disclose your identity and organization before every interview. Pragmatic Institute's guide to CI guardrails shows what that means in practice. At a trade show, walking up to a rival's booth and asking questions is fine. Pretending to be someone else, like a customer, while you do it is not. Fraud generally means misrepresenting yourself or your purpose to get information, and trade secret violations carry steep penalties. For gray areas, its rule of thumb is \"when in doubt, leave it out\". There's a line running the other direction too. The same FTC guidance warns that your company's future plans, such as an upcoming price increase, should be closely guarded. Sharing them outside the company, even with a consultant or analyst who also talks to your rivals, could change competitors' decisions and raise antitrust concerns. Before you gather anything, a quick test: Would you be comfortable if the rival knew exactly how you got it? Did you say who you are and who you work for? Is it public, or does someone owe their employer confidentiality? Are you giving away anything about your own future prices or plans? Play video: Competitive intelligence, with Professor Patrick Reinmoeller Competitive intelligence, with Professor Patrick Reinmoeller Cranfield School of Management, Mar 2015 · 5 min · YouTube Turning intel into something people use A folder of screenshots helps nobody. Intelligence matters when it changes what someone does: For sales: an updated battlecard, or a heads-up the day a rival changes its pricing. For product: patterns from win/loss interviews about why deals are lost. For leadership: a short, regular summary of what moved and what it means. Keep a steady rhythm, like a monthly digest, plus quick alerts for big moves such as a price change or an acquisition. Separate what you know from what you're guessing, and say which is which. Common traps Collecting everything, using nothing. Start from a decision someone needs to make. Watching only the famous rival. Buyers often compare you with a spreadsheet or the way they work today. Treating rumors as facts. Label how confident you are. Crossing the line for an edge. Misrepresentation can cost far more than it gains. In an interview If asked how you'd set up competitive intelligence, start with the decisions it should feed, such as battlecards, pricing or the roadmap. Name your sources, starting with win/loss interviews and public filings, explain how you'd share what you find, and mention the ethical lines you'd hold. Self-check: A teammate suggests signing up for a rival's free trial under a made-up company name, \"just to see the product.\" What do you say? Don't. Using a fake identity to get information is misrepresentation, the line Pragmatic draws on posing as someone else, and it may break the rival's terms of service. It's also close to what Deel accused a Rippling competitive-intelligence employee of doing. Sign up as yourself if the rival allows it, or learn the product from public demos, documentation and buyers who've used it."}, {"id": "pmm/enablement/customer-proof-case-studies-references-and-demos", "text": "Ask B2B buyers who they trust, and the answers form circles. In Forrester's research, the most trusted sources are buyers' own coworkers and managers, at 82%, followed by vendors they already work with, at 79%. Next come independent voices, such as industry peers, analysts, vendor executives and customers, trusted by 66% to 72%. Other outsiders rank lowest. A new vendor's salesperson starts in that outer circle. Customer proof is how you borrow trust from someone closer to the middle. Three kinds of proof Each kind does a different job at a different moment: Proof What it is When it helps most Case study A published story of a customer's results Early, while buyers research on their own Reference A live conversation with a current customer Late, when a buyer needs to hear it from a peer Demo The product shown solving the buyer's problem In the middle, once you know what they need Proof matters most where nobody knows you yet. Risto Salminen and Kristian Möller, reviewing how references are used in business marketing, note that many smaller firms lean on them to overcome a \"liability of anonymity\" when they enter new overseas markets. What makes a case study work Buyers read case studies looking for themselves. The ones that persuade usually have four things: A customer like the buyer: same industry, similar size, a similar problem. A specific number: \"cut onboarding from three weeks to four days\" beats \"improved efficiency.\" The before: the problem and what they'd already tried, so the result means something. A real person: a named customer with a title makes it believable. The best story for a deal isn't always your biggest logo. A 40-person company may trust a story about another 40-person company more than one about a household name. References without burning out your best customers Your happiest customers get asked constantly, so treat references as a scarce resource: Match on industry, size and use case, not just on who's available. Brief the customer on who's calling and what they care about. Track how often you ask each one, and spread the load. Thank them in ways that matter to them, like early access or a speaking slot, and know that some perks have to be disclosed. Demos: show the result first Peter Cohan's Great Demo! method starts with a reversal. Show the most valuable result first, which he calls \"Do the Last Thing First,\" then show how you got there. Before a demo, Cohan recommends filling in a Situation Slide with six items: the person's job title, their critical business issue, the problems making it hard to solve, the specific capabilities they need, the \"delta\" (the tangible value of making the change) and the critical date they need a solution by. The specific capabilities tell you exactly what to show and what to leave out. A demo that tours every feature makes the buyer figure out what matters. A demo built from a Situation Slide does that work for them. Play video: Great Demo! overview Great Demo! overview Peter Cohan, Aug 2009 · 3 min · YouTube The rules Published customer proof, like case studies and testimonials on your website, is advertising. The FTC's Endorsement Guides explain how it applies the law to endorsements, and two points matter most for PMMs: Typical results. If a featured customer's results aren't representative of what customers generally get, the ad should clearly disclose the generally expected performance, and you need evidence to back that figure. Hidden connections. If an endorser has a connection to you that the audience wouldn't expect and that could affect how much they trust the endorsement, it has to be disclosed clearly and conspicuously. A discount given in exchange for a case study is the kind of connection worth checking with your legal team. Reviews have their own rule. Since October 21, 2024, the FTC's rule on consumer reviews and testimonials has let courts impose civil penalties for knowing violations involving fake or deceptive reviews and testimonials. Common traps Leading with your biggest logo when the buyer looks nothing like it. Vague results. \"Improved efficiency\" proves nothing. The feature-tour demo. If it isn't tied to their problem, it's noise. Overusing your happiest customer. References get tired too. In an interview If asked how you'd build a customer proof program, start with where deals stall and what proof would get them moving. Then cover how you'd find and document stories, how you'd match references to prospects, and how you'd keep every claim accurate. Self-check: One customer cut costs 60%, but most customers see about 15%. Marketing wants to headline the 60%. What do you advise? Feature the story if you like, but say clearly that it isn't typical and what customers generally achieve, around 15%. Without that, the headline implies 60% is normal, which the FTC's guides warn against. Better yet, lead the main campaign with a story closer to typical, and save the 60% for buyers whose situation actually matches."}, {"id": "pmm/enablement/build-a-one-page-battlecard", "text": "The facts. Toast describes itself as a cloud-based, all-in-one platform built for the entire restaurant community, which it calls the restaurant operating system. It had about 164,000 locations at the end of 2025, up 22%, and estimates that its US restaurants make up about 20% of the US restaurant market. It's starting to expand into food and beverage retail. Its annual report doesn't name rivals. It says it competes on an all-in-one software and payments platform with partner integrations, on ease of use and reliability, on brand and customer satisfaction, on support and onboarding, and on low-cost integration. Toast's Starter Kit costs $0 a month for single-location restaurants and is paid for through a 3.09% card processing fee. Its traditional option has restaurants buy hardware up front and pay monthly for software, with a 2.49% processing fee. The Point of Sale plan starts at $69 a month, and contracts typically run 12 to 36 months, then renew automatically one year at a time. Square for Restaurants has a free plan charging 2.6% plus 15 cents per in-person payment, Plus at $49 a month per location (2.5% plus 15 cents) and Premium at $149 (2.4% plus 15 cents). Plus and Premium come with a 30-day free trial, and hardware starts at $59. Block, Square's parent company, says it differentiates on its broad commerce ecosystem, transparent pricing and no mandatory long-term contracts. Square's payment volume grew 10% in 2025, driven mainly by food and beverage sellers, and Block has been building a field sales team to win larger sellers. CNBC reported in May 2025 that Block faces growing competition from rivals including Toast and Fiserv's Clover, though Square still gained share in food and beverage. Your task: You're the PMM at Toast. Sales keeps running into Square at small, independent restaurants opening their first location. Build a one-page battlecard: why Toast wins, where Square is strong, the questions reps should ask, quick answers to what Square's reps will say about Toast, and the proof you'd want. +++ reveal Sample battlecard Square in one line: an easy, low-commitment way to start taking payments, with a free plan, a 30-day trial on paid plans and no mandatory contract. Why Toast wins Built for restaurants first, so kitchen, menu and online ordering tools fit how restaurants actually run. One system for front and back of house, with a large partner ecosystem. Restaurant-specific onboarding and support, which its annual report lists among the ways it competes. Where Square is strong, and reps should say so No mandatory long-term contract, and a free plan. Simple, published processing rates. A familiar brand for owners who already use Square somewhere else. Questions to ask \"How many orders do you expect on a Friday night, and how will they get to the kitchen?\" \"Are you planning a second location?\" \"How will you handle online orders and delivery?\" Quick answers \"Toast locks you into a contract.\" Be straight: terms often run 12 to 36 months. Then show what the term buys, such as restaurant-specific onboarding and support, and compare total cost over the same period. \"Square is cheaper.\" Toast has a $0-a-month Starter Kit too, so compare the full monthly cost at the restaurant's real volume: processing, including any per-transaction fee, plus software and hardware, not just the plan price. Proof to get: a story from a single-location restaurant that switched from Square, with a number that matters to owners, like faster ticket times or fewer order errors. What the public record shows Both companies are reaching for the same small restaurant. Toast leans on being built for restaurants. Block's annual report leans on transparent pricing and no mandatory contracts, and food and beverage sellers drove Square's growth in 2025. Toast's own pricing shows its answer to \"cheaper to start\": a $0 Starter Kit for single locations. Why this card works It concedes Square's real strengths, which makes the rep believable. The questions steer the conversation to restaurant operations, where Toast is strongest. The answers compare total cost and value instead of attacking. What interviewers push on \"Isn't the contract a real weakness?\" For some owners, yes. The card should help reps qualify: a restaurant unsure it'll last a year is a poor fit for a long term. \"How would you keep this current?\" Recheck both pricing pages every month, and update the card after each round of win/loss interviews. Practice twist Suppose Square launched a 12-month price lock for new restaurants, still with no contract. What would change on your card?"}, {"id": "pmm/enablement/answer-the-top-three-objections", "text": "The facts. Microsoft Bookings comes with many Microsoft 365 and Office 365 plans, including Microsoft 365 Business Basic, Business Standard and Business Premium and the E3 and E5 enterprise plans. It offers personal booking pages that anyone can use to book time, plus shared booking pages for teams. A Bookings page supports up to 100 staff members. Calendly's Standard plan costs $10 per seat a month and Teams costs $16, billed yearly. Teams adds round-robin and team scheduling, lead qualification and routing, sending meetings to Salesforce and an optional single sign-on add-on. Seats are needed only for people who host meetings, and invitees don't need one. Enterprise starts at $15,000 a year. Calendly lists SOC 2 Type 2, SOC 3, ISO/IEC 27001 and other certifications, and offers documentation for customers running security reviews or procurement assessments. Your task: You're the PMM supporting Calendly's sales team. A 300-person company on Microsoft 365 Business Premium wants scheduling for its 40-person sales development team, which books meetings with prospects and routes them to account executives. Three objections keep coming up: \"We already get Bookings with Microsoft 365. Why pay for Calendly?\" \"$16 a seat for 300 people? No way.\" \"Security will never approve another vendor.\" For each, write the question a rep should ask first, a short answer and the proof to use. +++ reveal Sample answers 1. \"We already get Bookings.\" Ask first: \"What does your team need to happen after a meeting is booked?\" Answer: Bookings covers plenty of simple scheduling, and the rep shouldn't pretend otherwise. The case for Calendly rests on what this team does all day: routing leads to the right rep, round-robin across 40 people and sending meetings to Salesforce. Check what Bookings offers today before claiming any gap. Proof: a short trial with five reps, measured on meetings booked and time spent routing. 2. \"$16 a seat for 300 people.\" Ask first: \"Who actually hosts the meetings?\" Answer: Only people who host meetings need seats: the 40 SDRs plus the account executives who take the routed meetings, still far below 300. With 20 account executives, that's 60 seats × $16 × 12 = $11,520 a year. Compare that with the value of one extra qualified meeting per rep each month. Proof: the seat rule on the pricing page, and a simple cost-per-meeting calculation. 3. \"Security won't approve it.\" Ask first: \"What does your security team usually need to see?\" Answer: Send the certifications and trust documentation before they're requested, and offer single sign-on through the Teams add-on. Proof: the security documentation itself, plus a reference customer in a similar industry. Why these work Each starts with a question, which turns a flat no into a problem you can solve. The seat math reframes price around the people who actually need seats. Nobody badmouths Microsoft. The case rests on this team's workflow. What interviewers push on \"What if Bookings really does cover their needs?\" Then say so, and offer Calendly only for the team where it clearly wins. Credibility pays off in the next deal. \"Where does PMM come in?\" Turn these three answers into a battlecard section with proof attached, and update it as reps hear new versions. Practice twist The buyer's IT lead says, \"Let's wait and see what Microsoft adds next year.\" How do you answer a \"wait for the bundle\" objection?"}, {"id": "pmm/enablement/pick-the-right-customer-story", "text": "The facts. Stripe processed $1.9 trillion in total payment volume in 2025, up 34%. Co-founder John Collison told Bloomberg that Stripe is a preferred vendor for companies building AI applications and is winning share among large enterprises. PepsiCo's story: many of the small stores and restaurants supplied by PepsiCo's Away From Home division still paid for deliveries with paper checks handed to drivers. In 2024 the team launched an app with Stripe so customers could order and pay online. Stripe's story says four PepsiCo divisions covering 23 or more brands now use Stripe, local payment methods are supported in more than 20 markets, and the work saved a year of engineering time. Cloudbeds' story: Cloudbeds sells software that helps hotels run their operations, and Stripe has powered Cloudbeds Payments, its built-in payments product, since 2019. Stripe's story says hotels using Cloudbeds Payments saw 15% revenue growth, and properties that added payment methods saw an average 14.8% revenue boost. Your task: You support Stripe's sales team, and three prospects are in play this week. For each one, pick the story you'd send, or say that neither fits and what you'd use instead: A regional snack distributor whose corner-store customers still pay drivers by check. A company selling booking software to 3,000 independent yoga studios, which wants studios to take payments inside its product. A 15-person AI startup about to start charging for subscriptions, worried about failed payments and fraud. +++ reveal Sample answer Snack distributor: PepsiCo. The match is the situation, not the size: small business customers paying by check, moved to ordering and paying in an app. Lead with the problem they share, then the results. Yoga software: Cloudbeds. Both are software platforms adding payments for the small businesses they serve. Stripe's reported 15% revenue growth for Cloudbeds' hotels speaks to the question this prospect will ask, whether payments will help its studios grow, though it's Stripe's own figure with no comparison group. AI startup: neither. PepsiCo is the biggest logo, which makes it tempting, but a 15-person startup won't see itself in a global food company. Since Stripe says it's a preferred vendor for AI companies, a story about a similar early-stage AI company very likely exists, so find it. If there isn't one, offer a reference call with a startup customer that uses subscription billing. Why matching matters Buyers look for customers like themselves, so the situation beats the size of the logo. A mismatched story can backfire by suggesting you don't understand the buyer. When no story fits, a live reference is usually stronger than a stretched case study. What interviewers push on \"Why not just send all of them?\" A pile of stories makes the buyer do the matching. One well-chosen story shows you understood them. \"How would you fill the gap for AI startups?\" Find a customer like them with a clear result, get permission and write the story, then track whether it helps win similar deals. Practice twist The snack distributor's CFO asks whether PepsiCo's results are typical. How do you answer honestly?"}, {"id": "pmm/growth/where-growth-comes-from-acquisition-activation-r", "text": "When Snowflake filed to go public in 2020, one number stood out. Its net revenue retention rate was 158%: customers who'd been around for a year were spending 58% more than they had the year before. The number of customers paying Snowflake more than $1 million a year had jumped from 22 to 56 in twelve months. As Snowflake grew, the rate came down, to 126% by mid-2026, but existing customers were still spending more each year. Most people picture growth as new customers. Snowflake's filing shows another source: customers already in the door, using more. Growth comes from several places, and the first job is knowing which one is stuck. Five stages, one funnel In 2007, investor Dave McClure gave growth teams a vocabulary they still use. His \"Startup Metrics for Pirates\" broke a customer's journey into five stages, abbreviated AARRR (say it like a pirate): Stage McClure's definition The question it asks A number to watch Acquisition Users come to the site from various channels Are the right people finding us? Visitors or sign-ups by channel Activation Users enjoy a happy first visit Do new users get value fast? Share who reach a first real result Retention Users come back and visit many times Do they keep coming back? Share still active after 30 or 90 days Referral Users like it enough to refer others Do they bring anyone? Invites sent, and referred sign-ups Revenue Users do something that makes money Do they pay, and pay more over time? Paid conversion, revenue per customer Play video: Startup Metrics for Pirates: AARRR! Startup Metrics for Pirates: AARRR! Dave McClure, Ignite Seattle, Aug 2007 · 5 min · YouTube Find the stage that's actually stuck The stages chain together, so a weak one limits everything after it. Here's an illustrative product. Each month 2,000 people sign up, 30% of them activate and a quarter of those are still active after 30 days. That's 150 lasting users a month. Now compare three fixes: Get 20% more sign-ups: 2,400 × 30% × 25% = 180 lasting users, and you pay for every extra sign-up. Lift activation from 30% to 45%, a 50% jump that's realistic when the first session is broken: 2,000 × 45% × 25% = 225, with no extra ad spend. Lift 30-day retention from 25% to 30%: 2,000 × 30% × 30% = 180. Notice that a 20% lift at any single stage gets you to the same 180, because the stages multiply. So the stage to fix is the one where you can realistically win the biggest lift, and that's often a neglected one, like a confusing first session, rather than the one getting the most attention in meetings. Why retention usually comes first The economics lean hard toward keeping customers. Depending on the study and the industry, winning a new customer costs five to 25 times more than keeping one, and research by Bain's Frederick Reichheld found that raising retention by 5% lifts profits by 25% to 95%. Treat that range as a direction rather than a promise. It isn't an argument against acquisition, either: the point is that a product has to hold on to the users it wins before you pay for more of them. Retention also tells you whether growth is even possible. Plot the share of each month's sign-ups still active over time, and you get a retention curve. Peter Fader and Bruce Hardie, who build statistical models of these curves, note that cohort retention rates typically rise over time. Much of that comes from sorting: the customers most likely to leave do it early, so the ones left are the loyal ones. Their newer model also lets each customer's odds of leaving change over time. A healthy curve drops early and then flattens, which means some group finds lasting value. A curve that keeps sliding toward zero means no group does, and more acquisition just pours water into a leaky bucket. Play video: How to keep your users How to keep your users David Lieb, Y Combinator Startup School, Aug 2024 · 29 min · starts at 0:43 · YouTube Growth beyond the funnel The funnel describes one product and one market. Companies also grow by selling to a new segment, entering a new country or adding a product their current customers will buy. Those bets are bigger and slower, and they work best once the core funnel holds water. Common traps Buying growth before fixing retention. New users leak out as fast as you pay for them. Celebrating sign-ups. Acquisition numbers look great right up until you check who stayed. Optimizing every stage at once. Pick the one that limits the rest. Averaging away the story. A total user count can grow while every new cohort retains worse than the last. In an interview For \"Our growth has stalled. What would you do?\", don't jump to a campaign. Walk the funnel stage by stage, ask for the conversion at each step and how recent cohorts retain, and name the one stage you'd fix first and why. Self-check: A meditation app gets plenty of downloads, but only 8% of new users open it a second time. The team wants an influencer campaign. What would you say? Hold the campaign. The first session isn't delivering enough value: 92% of new users never come back, so more downloads mostly buy more one-time visits. Find out what the 8% who return did in their first session, design the first session to get everyone there, and revisit acquisition once more new users stick."}, {"id": "pmm/growth/growth-loops-and-channels-that-compound", "text": "Wise, the money-transfer company, counted 19 million active customers in its 2026 fiscal year, up more than 21%. Its shareholder letter credits word-of-mouth recommendations for about 70% of new customers. Wise still raised its marketing spending 62% that year, adding brand campaigns on top of its existing channels. When seven in ten new customers arrive because somebody told them to, growth works differently. Customers aren't just the output of marketing. They're the engine. Funnels end, loops feed themselves A funnel runs one way: you spend money, people flow through, and when you stop spending, the flow stops. A loop feeds its own output back in, so each round sets up the next. Andrew Chen, author of The Cold Start Problem , argues there are only a few ways to scale user growth: paid acquisition, virality, search traffic from user-created content, and sales. They all work for the same reason. They're feedback loops: revenue from customers buys more customers, or a group of new users invites even more users. The common loops look like this: Loop How it feeds itself Fits when Viral or referral Users invite users The product is better with other people, or worth recommending Content User activity creates pages people find through search Users make public content others search for Paid Revenue funds ads that bring more paying customers A customer is worth more than it costs to acquire one Sales Revenue funds reps who close more revenue Deals are big enough to pay for a salesperson Play video: The Cold Start Problem The Cold Start Problem Andrew Chen, Talks at Google, Dec 2021 · 60 min · starts at 3:43 · YouTube What the research says about viral loops Sinan Aral and Dylan Walker ran a randomized experiment on Facebook with the 1.4 million friends of 9,687 users. Automatic \"passive broadcast\" notifications raised peer influence by 246%, while adding personal invites raised it only another 98%. Personal invites worked better per message and came with more engagement, but broadcasts went out far more often, so they drove more total adoption. That's a design choice, not just a marketing one. Small product decisions about what gets shared, and how, decide how strong a viral loop can be. Referrals bring better customers Paying for referrals can sound like bribery. A study by Philipp Schmitt, Bernd Skiera and Christophe Van den Bulte suggests it's often a good deal. Looking at a German bank that paid customers 25 euros per referral, they found referred customers were about 18% more likely to stay, and worth 16% to 25% more over the long run. That worked out to roughly a 60% return over six years on the 25 euros. A community loop: LEGO Ideas LEGO runs one of the clearest community loops in consumer products. Fans submit their own set designs, and any idea that wins 10,000 supporters is considered for production as an official set, with its creator earning 1% of net sales. One designer's promo video went viral and helped push a cat-themed set past 10,000 votes. Every hopeful designer becomes a recruiter, bringing new fans to LEGO Ideas to vote. A paid loop, and its limits Booking Holdings, the company behind Booking.com, spent $8.2 billion on marketing in 2025, up 12.5%. Most of it was performance marketing, led by search ads on Google. Booking judges each channel partly by the incremental bookings it brings and how often those customers come back, and it notes that its marketing gets more efficient as more customers book directly. In 2025, people coming straight to its platforms accounted for a mid-fifties percentage of room nights. A paid loop rents attention. It keeps working only while each customer is worth more than they cost, and it gets cheaper as more of those customers come back on their own. Common traps Forcing a viral loop onto a solo product. If nobody benefits from inviting a friend, an invite button won't create a loop. Counting the first round. A loop only matters if each round brings in enough people for the next. Renting forever. Paid growth that never turns into direct, repeat visits often gets more expensive over time. Copying another company's loop. LEGO's works because fans love designing sets. Yours has to fit what your users already want to do. In an interview For \"How would you grow this product?\", ask what users do that could bring in the next user, and sketch that loop step by step. Then name the step that's weakest and how you'd measure it, such as invites per new user or the share of content that ranks in search. Self-check: A project-management tool is mostly used by solo freelancers. The team wants a referral program like a social app's. What would you check first? Whether users have a reason to bring anyone in. Solo freelancers rarely need collaborators inside the tool, so invites may go nowhere. Look for a natural sharing moment instead, such as sending a client a project link, and build the loop around that: every client who sees a polished project page is a potential new user."}, {"id": "pmm/growth/planning-a-campaign-the-brief-the-idea-the-chann", "text": "eBay used to buy search ads on its own name, so people searching for eBay saw a paid eBay ad as well as the normal result. Then economists Thomas Blake, Chris Nosko and Steven Tadelis ran large field experiments at the company to see what those ads actually did. The brand-keyword ads had no measurable short-term benefit. For other keywords, ads helped mainly new and infrequent shoppers, but most of the spending went to frequent shoppers who'd have bought anyway, so average returns were negative. The ads had looked great in the dashboards, because people who click on search ads are often already planning to buy. A campaign has four parts, the brief, the idea, the channels and the measure, and it's the last one that tells you whether the other three worked. The brief: one page someone can act on Briefs are where most campaigns go wrong first. In a 2021 IPA survey of more than 1,700 marketers and agency staff in over 70 countries, 80% of marketers said they write good briefs, but only 10% of agencies agreed. Respondents estimated that a third of marketing budgets is wasted on poor briefs and misdirected work. A good brief fits on a page and answers six questions: Question Weak answer Strong answer What's the business problem? \"Raise awareness\" \"Trial sign-ups from small agencies fell 15% this year\" Who's it for? \"Everyone\" \"Owners of 5- to 20-person agencies\" What do they think now? Left blank \"They think we're built for big companies\" What's the one thing to say? Five messages One line from the positioning What must it include? Every logo and feature Only real requirements How will we know it worked? \"Engagement\" One number, with a date and a baseline The idea: clever isn't the same as right An idea can be brilliant to the people who made it and still land badly with the people it's about. In May 2024, Apple released an iPad Pro ad showing a piano, a camera, paint cans and other creative tools being flattened by an industrial crusher. Creatives hated it. Apple's vice president of marketing, Tor Myhren, apologized: \"We missed the mark with this video, and we're sorry\". The point of the ad, that one thin device can do the work of a studio, was a fine product message. The image chosen to express it told artists their tools, and maybe their craft, didn't matter. Test the idea with the audience it depicts, not just the team that loves it. The channels: long-term and short-term Les Binet and Peter Field, in research published by the IPA, found that the best results come from balancing two kinds of marketing: brand building, which shapes what people think of you over time, and sales activation, which prompts people to act now. In their landmark study, the best split on average was 60:40 in favor of brand building, and a later update found 62:38. B2B is different, but not as different as many B2B marketers assume. Their B2B research recommends roughly a 50/50 split, and finds that strategies for winning new customers tend to be much more effective than loyalty strategies. That's a finding about where marketing budget pays off. It doesn't change the product rule: a product still has to keep the users it wins. The reason acquisition matters so much in B2B is timing. John Dawes of the Ehrenberg-Bass Institute points out that companies switch providers like their bank or law firm about once every five years, so only around 5% of business buyers are in the market in any quarter. Brand building reaches the other 95% before they start shopping. Play video: The Short of It The Short of It Les Binet, Nov 2020 · 5 min · YouTube The measure: what did the campaign cause? Start with the standard scorecard. OpenStax's Principles of Marketing lists the usual ways to measure an ad campaign: brand recognition and awareness, impressions, conversion rate (the share of an audience that takes the action you wanted) and return on ad spend, which divides the revenue from a period by the ad dollars spent in it. Those numbers tell you what happened while the campaign ran. They don't tell you what the campaign caused, and that's the harder question. The pandemic handed Airbnb a natural experiment. When it filed to go public in 2020, it said about 23% of its traffic in 2019 had come from paid performance marketing, including search ads on the word \"Airbnb.\" During the pandemic it suspended nearly all performance marketing, and in the first nine months of 2020 about 91% of its traffic came through direct or unpaid channels. Its rivals were also bidding on its brand name in search. Its stated strategy afterward: more brand marketing, less performance marketing. The paid share of traffic was bound to fall once the ads stopped, so that number alone doesn't prove the ads added nothing, but it gave Airbnb reason to rethink the mix. A 2026 Kellogg case puts you in its marketing VP's seat in December 2020, deciding whether $5 million should go to trackable performance ads or to brand building on YouTube. The fix is to measure what a campaign added, not just what happened after it. The cleanest way is a holdout: show the campaign to a random group, hold it back from a similar group, and compare. Shortcuts don't hold up well. In a study of 15 advertising experiments at Facebook, covering 1.6 billion ad impressions, Brett Gordon and colleagues found that the usual observational methods often failed to match what the randomized tests showed. Common traps A brief with five messages. The work will say none of them clearly. Testing the idea only on fans. Ask the people the ad is about. All activation, no brand. It looks efficient until the pool of people who already know you runs dry. Reading credit as cause. Last-click attribution rewards ads shown to people who'd have bought anyway. In an interview For \"Plan a campaign for X,\" move through the four parts in order: the business problem and the one number you'll move, the audience and the single message, the idea and why it fits them, the channels and how you'd split long-term and short-t"}, {"id": "pmm/growth/brainstorm-a-campaign-for-a-launch", "text": "The facts. On September 30, 2025, OpenAI released Sora 2, its most advanced video-generation model, along with a new iOS app also called Sora, aimed at bringing AI video creation to everyday users. The app let people create, remix and \"cameo\" in AI-generated videos. Real people couldn't be generated unless they'd submitted a verified cameo of themselves. It launched invite-only in the US and Canada, with a Pro version, web access and a developer API to follow. Your task: You're the PMM for the Sora app at launch. Plan the first 90 days as a campaign: the brief (audience, one message, one goal), the idea, the channels and how you'll measure success. Be specific about the number that would tell you, by week four, whether the launch is really working. +++ reveal Sample plan Audience: people who already make and share short videos, starting with groups of friends rather than solo users. One message: \"Put yourself and your friends in any scene.\" The idea: make the cameo itself the campaign. Every new user records a cameo and gets invites to share, so each invite comes with a reason to make something together. Channels: seed invites with creators whose audiences love remixing, let finished videos travel to other social platforms with a clear Sora mark, and use the waitlist to build demand. Measure: not downloads. Watch the share of each week's new users who come back in week four, the share who make a video with a friend's cameo, and videos shared outside the app. What happened The launch itself was a hit. The app passed 100,000 installs on day one despite being invite-only, hit No. 1 on the US App Store and reached 1 million downloads faster than ChatGPT had. Then it faded. According to Appfigures data, downloads fell 32% month over month in December, a month when apps usually do well, and another 45% in January. By then the app had about 9.6 million downloads and $1.4 million in consumer spending in total. On March 24, 2026, six months after launch, OpenAI said it was shutting Sora down. CNBC reported that the initial excitement had faded and that OpenAI was cutting costs ahead of a possible IPO. What you can learn from it A launch spike isn't growth. The invite mechanic and the novelty drove acquisition. Falling downloads and spending in the months after suggest that keeping users was the harder problem. Pick a week-four number before launch. Downloads and chart rankings said the launch worked. A retention goal would have shown whether people were actually sticking around. Cost belongs in the brief. AI video is expensive to generate, so every active user costs real money. The economics of keeping users matter as much as attracting them. What interviewers push on \"Wasn't the invite system a smart loop?\" It was a strong acquisition loop. A loop also needs people to stay long enough to invite the next round. \"What would you have changed?\" Tie the launch to repeat use, like weekly prompts to make something with friends, and judge success by week-four retention, not chart position. Practice twist You're launching an AI photo-editing feature inside an app people already use daily. How does your campaign change when you don't need downloads at all?"}, {"id": "pmm/growth/restart-growth-for-a-stalled-product", "text": "The facts. Pinterest ended 2021 with 431 million monthly active users, down 6% from a year earlier. In the US, users fell 12%, from 98 million to 86 million. Pinterest blamed the end of the pandemic boom, lower search traffic after a Google algorithm change, and growing competition for people's time from video-centric apps. Revenue still grew 52% for the year, to $2.58 billion. By mid-2022, users had leveled off at 433 million, still down 5% from a year earlier. Pinterest had struggled to hold on to the users it gained early in the pandemic. Co-founder Ben Silbermann stepped down as CEO in June and was replaced by Bill Ready, whose experience at Google and PayPal was expected to help Pinterest grow its online shopping business. The activist investor Elliott Management said it had become Pinterest's largest investor and backed Ready. Your task: It's mid-2022 and you're a PMM at Pinterest. Diagnose which stage of growth stalled, then plan how to restart it. Say which users you'd focus on, what would bring them back or keep them, and which number you'd watch. +++ reveal Sample answer Diagnosis: this is mostly a retention and engagement problem, plus an acquisition hit from search. Pandemic users came for a specific season of life and didn't find a reason to stay, while video apps competed for the same time. Focus: younger users, where video apps are winning, and the US, where the drop was steepest. What brings them back: make Pinterest the place you go to act on an idea, not just save it. If a saved outfit or room idea leads straight to products you can buy, there's a reason to return every time you're planning something. Number to watch: the share of users who return in a given month, broken out by age group, plus how many sessions end with someone clicking through to a product. What Pinterest actually did Pinterest leaned into shopping. In April 2023 it signed a multiyear deal making Amazon its first partner for third-party ads, bringing more brands and products to the platform at a time when its image pinboard had started to feel dated next to TikTok and Reels. In October 2025 it launched Pinterest Assistant, an AI shopping helper that gives recommendations based on your taste. Global users grew again. Pinterest ended 2025 with 619 million monthly active users worldwide, and Gen Z made up more than half of its user base. What you can learn from it Name the stage before the fix. Pinterest named two causes: fewer people returning after the pandemic, and less traffic from Google search. A reason to come back addresses the first, while search traffic needs its own plan. Fit the product to the job. Pinterest leaned on what people already used it for, planning things, and made the next step easier. Watch who's growing. By 2025, Gen Z made up more than half of Pinterest's users, so younger users ended up central to the comeback, even though the numbers can't say which moves brought them in. Be careful about credit. Many moves happened at once, so no single one can claim the turnaround. What interviewers push on \"Why not just chase video like everyone else?\" Pinterest tried creator video, but matching a rival's format puts you on their ground. Its edge was intent: people arrive planning to do something. \"How would you know the plan was working before user numbers moved?\" Watch return visits and click-throughs in the target group month by month. Practice twist A meal-kit company lost a third of its subscribers after the pandemic ended. What stage stalled, and what's the first thing you'd test?"}, {"id": "pmm/growth/pick-the-one-growth-lever-to-pull", "text": "The facts. In the third quarter of 2026, Tinder's monthly active users were down 5.5% from a year earlier, an improvement from the quarter before. Among women, the decline narrowed from 8% to 6%. Daily active users were down just 1%, and turned to year-over-year growth in September. Sparks, which counts users in multi-message conversations, rose 1%. Paying users fell 4% to 8.9 million, while revenue per payer rose 2% to $18.02, leaving Tinder's direct revenue down 2% at $480 million. Match Group said it was spending more on marketing at Tinder and Hinge, and that its focus was \"reconsideration,\" particularly among younger Gen Z users. Its sister app Hinge, meanwhile, grew paying users 23% to 2.3 million. Your task: You run growth marketing for Tinder next year and can fund one lever: acquisition, activation, retention, referral or revenue. Pick one, say who it targets and defend why it beats the others. +++ reveal Sample answer Pull acquisition, aimed at bringing lapsed and new Gen Z users back to take another look. Why not revenue: revenue per payer is already rising. Pushing harder on price with a shrinking user base can speed the decline. Why not retention or activation alone: daily users and Sparks are improving, so the product changes for existing users seem to be landing. The constraint is the number of people willing to try Tinder at all. Why acquisition: payers come from users. Paying users fell 4% while users fell 5.5%, so the pool is the problem, especially among the young users the company says it has the most ground to recover with. How: change how Gen Z sees the app, not just how many ads they see. Lead with lower-pressure, social ways to meet, and give users a reason to bring a friend, which turns acquisition into a referral loop. Number to watch: new and returning Gen Z users each month, and how many of them reach a real conversation in their first week. What Match Group said it's doing Match Group described its next step as driving broader reconsideration, giving more people a reason to see Tinder differently and take another look. For the fourth quarter, it said it was doubling down on a \"Better with Friends\" roadmap, with features like Double Date and Events designed to give Gen Z more social, lower-pressure ways to meet, often with friends and in real life. Why this choice holds up It follows the numbers. Engagement is improving and monetization is fine, so the shrinking top of the funnel is what's left. It pairs a lever with a loop. Meeting with friends gives every user a reason to recruit the next one. Hinge shows people still pay for a dating app that feels different. Its paying users grew 23% while Tinder's fell. What interviewers push on \"Isn't 'reconsideration' just a brand campaign?\" Partly. The product has to change too, or the campaign brings people back to the same experience that made them leave. \"Why not invest in Hinge instead?\" It's a portfolio question worth raising, but the task was Tinder's growth, and Tinder still has far more paying users. Practice twist Six months later, Gen Z users are returning, but few of them pay. Which lever do you pull now?"}, {"id": "bm/getting-started/what-a-brand-manager-does", "text": "In 1931, a young ad man at Procter & Gamble got fed up. Neil McElroy was working on the advertising for Camay soap, and Camay wasn't only up against soaps from Lever and Palmolive. It was also up against Ivory, P&G's own flagship. On May 13, 1931, he wrote a memo arguing that each brand should have one person in charge, backed by a team that worked on that brand and nothing else: a brand assistant plus a few specialists. The memo ran three pages, which broke President Deupree's well-known one-page-memo rule. Deupree endorsed it with enthusiasm anyway. The idea was that each brand would be marketed \"as if it were a separate business.\" It became the modern system of brand management, and it became P&G's route to the top. By 2000, every P&G chief executive after Deupree had brand-management experience. McElroy later ran the company himself, then became President Eisenhower's Secretary of Defense in 1957. P&G still tells it the same way. It says it invented brand management in 1931 to give each brand one point of accountability, and it describes the job today as being \"a total business owner.\" It draws the line clearly: brand management creates the brands and oversees every part of their success, while marketing focuses on individual campaigns. What you actually own \"Business owner\" sounds grand. Day to day, it means a set of numbers you answer for and a set of people you need. Job postings are the clearest description of the work, because companies have to spell it out: The numbers and the rhythm. Kraft Heinz's associate brand managers own key parts of what it calls the \"brand operating rhythm\": performance tracking, forecasting inputs, customer programs, commercialization work and agency coordination, all while partnering closely with Sales, Finance, Insights and Operations. They track share, volume and revenue, find what's driving them and help act on it. The data and the budget. Clorox's associate marketing managers analyze the health of the business with syndicated data from Circana plus custom research, and they manage the A&SP and packaging budgets for their business unit's marketing team. A&SP means advertising and sales promotion. Syndicated data is store sales data that a research firm collects from retailers and sells to the brands that subscribe, so you can see your share and your competitors' wherever the data covers. Notice who's missing from those lists: anyone who reports to you. You own the result. Sales, Finance, R&D and Operations own most of the levers that move it. Who you work with Keurig Dr Pepper tells its MBA brand interns to work with colleagues across Marketing, Consumer Insights, Finance, Innovation, Sales and Operations. Here's what each of those partners typically owns at a big CPG company, and what they need from you. This is a general guide, not one company's org chart: Partner What they own What they need from you Sales (customer teams) The relationship with each retailer: which items it carries, shelf space, promotions A story the retailer's buyer cares about, and promotions that pay back Revenue growth management (at many big companies) Pricing, pack sizes and promotion rules across the portfolio A clear view of what your shoppers will pay and which promotions actually work Finance Forecasts, budgets and the official numbers Numbers you can defend, and early warning when they move Consumer insights Research and syndicated data The decision a study will inform, before it's fielded R&D and innovation Formulas, products and packaging A clear consumer need and a target cost Operations and supply chain Making and shipping the product A forecast they can plan to, and time to react to changes Agencies Creative and media A tight brief and fast, clear feedback The skill underneath all of it is influence. Most weeks you're asking people who don't report to you to put your brand near the top of their list, so you win them over with clear goals, good data and follow-through. For a short tour of the role, this explainer covers what brand managers do, who they collaborate with and how careers progress. Play video: What is brand management? Explained in 5 minutes What is brand management? Explained in 5 minutes Rabeea Hasan, Jun 2026 · 6 min · YouTube Titles don't line up At P&G, the ladder runs Brand Manager, Senior Brand Manager, Brand Director and Senior Brand Director, and Senior Brand Manager is the level for people with an advanced degree and some work experience. Other companies use the same words differently: Company Title for the role Experience asked Posted pay P&G Senior Brand Manager (MBA hires) An advanced degree and some work experience Not on that page Clorox Associate Marketing Manager (MBA recent graduate) At least 3 years of work; an MBA or master's is a plus, not a must $117,000 to $130,000 a year Kraft Heinz Associate Brand Manager (experienced hire) A bachelor's degree and 3 to 5 years $104,200 to $130,200 Keurig Dr Pepper Associate Brand Manager (MBA summer intern) 3 to 6 years after college Not compared here So \"senior\" at one company can be the entry point for MBAs, while \"associate\" at another is a hire with years of experience. Ignore the title and read three things: the experience asked for, the pay band and what you'd own. Two jobs called \"brand manager\" Search job boards for \"brand manager\" and you'll find two different jobs under one title. At a consumer goods company, the brand manager runs a business: share, volume, forecasts and budgets, worked through Sales, Finance and Operations, like the Kraft Heinz and Clorox roles above. At many other companies, the brand manager looks after how the brand shows up. Drone maker Unusual Machines, for example, wants its brand manager to serve as the steward of its brand identity: positioning and messaging, integrated campaigns, consistency across launches and packaging, and its social media strategy and brand voice. The same posting calls the role both \"Brand Manager\" and \"Brand Marketing Manager\". Here"}, {"id": "bm/getting-started/how-a-cpg-brand-makes-money", "text": "In June 2025, Clorox was about to move its U.S. business onto a new ERP system, the software that takes orders and runs shipping. To be safe, it was adding 1.5 weeks of inventory at retailers, which typically hold four weeks' worth of goods. Retailers stocked up. In the April to June quarter, Clorox's net sales rose 4% to $2.0 billion, and the extra ERP shipments added about 13 to 14 percentage points to that growth rate. In other words, without the extra stock, sales would have fallen. Then the stock ran down. By the end of June, Clorox said, it had shipped about two weeks of inventory \"ahead of consumption,\" a bit more than the original cushion. As retailers worked through it in the July to September quarter, net sales fell 19% to $1.43 billion, mainly from lower shipments tied to the switch. Some of the damage was real, though: order-fulfillment problems earlier in the year had cost Clorox consumption and market share. Two numbers moved very differently there: what Clorox shipped, and what shoppers bought. Brand managers live between those two numbers. Your biggest customers are retailers When you buy Tide, you almost always buy it from a store, not from P&G. That makes a handful of retailers enormous customers: Company Walmart's share of its sales The wider picture P&G (year to June 2026) About 16% Its top ten customers are about 43% of net sales General Mills (year to May 2026) 22% Walmart is 31% of its North America Retail segment Kraft Heinz (2025) About 21% Both of its reportable segments sell to Walmart When one customer buys a fifth of what you make, its buyer's view of your plan carries real weight. That's why brand managers spend so much time with their Sales partners on plans for each big retailer: which items it carries, at what price, with which promotions and displays. Retailers also charge brands to be on the shelf. This explainer walks through the fees and discounts, from slotting fees for new items to penalties for late or incorrect deliveries. Play video: The hidden costs that kill CPG brands: trade spend explained The hidden costs that kill CPG brands: trade spend explained Jia Liao, Nov 2025 · 11 min · starts at 1:28 · YouTube Sell-in and sell-through Sell-in is what you ship to retailers and distributors. It's what your company books as sales, so it's the number in quarterly results. Sell-through (some companies say sell-out) is what shoppers buy at the register. Brands track it with scanner data from firms like Circana and NielsenIQ, and call it consumption or retail sales. Retailer inventory sits between the two. When sell-in runs ahead of sell-through, stock builds up in stores and warehouses. When it falls behind, stock drains. Big companies explain the gap to investors. In results released in March 2026, General Mills said organic net sales in its North America Retail business were down 4%, while Nielsen-measured retail sales were down 3%, with the 1-point gap reflecting changes in retailer inventory. Here's what usually pulls the two numbers apart: What happened Sell-in vs. sell-through What comes next A new item ships to every store Sell-in jumps far ahead Reorders depend on whether shoppers come back Retailers stock up before a price increase or a big promotion Sell-in runs ahead Orders dip once the extra stock sells Retailers cut inventory to free up cash Sell-in falls behind Orders recover once stock settles at its new level A supply problem, like a plant outage or a system switch Sell-in falls behind, and shelves can go empty Shoppers who switch brands may not come back Over a longer stretch, growth in shipments and growth in consumption tend to line up, because stores can't keep piling up stock. In any single quarter they can be far apart, so when someone tells you shipments jumped, your first question is what shoppers did. Where a shelf dollar goes An illustration with made-up numbers. Say a jar of salsa sells for $5.00 on the shelf. The retailer keeps $1.50, a 30% margin on the shelf price, so it pays the brand $3.50. Across the year, the brand spends an average of $0.40 a jar on promotions and displays, so it really nets $3.10. Making and shipping the jar costs $1.90, leaving $1.20 of gross profit. Advertising takes another $0.30. That leaves the brand $0.90 out of a $5.00 sale to cover salaries, R&D and offices, and to earn a profit. Every dime of extra discount comes out of that $0.90. The online shelf P&G's e-commerce sales grew 6% in its 2026 fiscal year and now make up 20% of its total sales. Much of that online shelf belongs to retailers like Amazon and Walmart, so the same logic applies: you sell in to a retailer, and the retailer sells through to shoppers. Self-check: A new salsa shipped 1.2 million jars in its first quarter. Scanner data shows shoppers bought 400,000. Is it a hit? Too early to say. Most of the 1.2 million is pipeline fill: stock sitting on shelves and in warehouses. Watch sell-through per store per week and how many first-time buyers come back for a second jar. Reorders will follow what shoppers do, not what shipped."}, {"id": "bm/getting-started/the-brand-p-and-l-on-one-page", "text": "P&G spent $10.2 billion on advertising in its 2026 fiscal year, on $87.0 billion of net sales. That number is easy to find. What it spends on retailer price cuts, displays and coupons is harder to spot, because it doesn't show up as a marketing cost. P&G records its sales net of trade promotion spending, so that money comes off the top before sales are reported. That's the first surprise in a brand P&L. One of its biggest numbers isn't booked as a marketing expense at all, even though brand and sales teams plan it together. Some CPG companies spend up to 20% of their gross revenue on promotions, which makes it one of the largest items on the P&L. The P&L, top to bottom An illustration with made-up numbers. Line Amount What it is Gross sales $100M Units shipped × list price to retailers − Trade spend −$15M Promotions, display fees, off-invoice discounts, coupons Net sales $85M What the company reports as sales − Cost of goods sold −$51M Ingredients, packaging, making and shipping Gross profit $34M 40% of net sales: the gross margin − Advertising and other marketing −$10M Media, creative, sampling, research Brand contribution $24M What the brand adds before shared costs − Overheads −$11M Salaries, R&D, offices, usually shared across brands Operating profit $13M About 15% of net sales Four habits will keep you out of trouble in a P&L review: Margins are a share of net sales, not gross sales. Gross margin is gross profit divided by net sales. Trade spend sits above net sales, and advertising sits below gross profit. Move a dollar from promotions into advertising, and if units don't change, your reported sales go up by a dollar while profit stays the same. Price-based promotions count as trade spend. Coupons and other money off the price usually come out of sales, so they sit above net sales even when the consumer gets the discount. Names vary. \"Brand contribution\" might be called direct margin or brand profit somewhere else. Ask what's included before you compare two brands. Trade spend comes off the top P&G isn't unusual. Clorox, General Mills and Kraft Heinz also book trade promotions as a cut to sales, not as a marketing cost: Clorox nets the cost of shelf price reductions, in-store merchandising and consumer coupons against sales. Its estimate of what it owes retailers is big enough that a 10% change would move net sales by about $21 million. General Mills reports revenue net of trade promotion and consumer coupon redemption. Kraft Heinz records trade promotion as a reduction to revenues, while its advertising sits in selling, general and administrative expenses. McKinsey calls trade incentives one of the largest and least disciplined investments most organizations make: many put 8 to 11% of their revenue into them, and some up to 20%. Here's why it matters to you. A promotion lowers the price on every unit sold on deal, including the ones loyal shoppers would have bought at full price anyway. Shoppers also stock up on deal and then buy less for a few weeks afterward. So a promotion can double your volume for two weeks and still lose money. In this session from Kantar's Ignite conference, Les Binet explains how price promotions work, why brands get hooked on them and how to wean a brand off. Play video: Les Binet: Kicking the promotion habit Les Binet: Kicking the promotion habit Kantar, Oct 2026 · 30 min · starts at 2:33 · YouTube How four real companies compare Company (fiscal year) Gross margin Advertising P&G (2026) 50.2% of net sales $10.2 billion Clorox (2026) 42.3%, down from 45.2% $749 million, 11.1% of net sales General Mills (2026) 33.6% of net sales $873.6 million, including production and communication Kraft Heinz (2025) About 33%: $8,309 million of gross profit on $24,942 million of net sales $1,073 million P&G keeps about half of each sales dollar after making the product, and Kraft Heinz keeps about a third. Relative to its sales, P&G also spends far more on advertising. Their categories and business models differ, so compare a brand with its own history and its closest competitors, not with a company in a different aisle. Margin vs. markup Retail buyers and brand teams can use different math for what sounds like the same idea. Markup divides the profit by what the seller paid. OpenStax's example: buy shoes for $50, sell them for $75, and the markup is 50%. Margin divides the same profit by the selling price. On those shoes, for example, the margin is $25 ÷ $75, or about 33%. Same $25, two very different percentages. When a buyer quotes a number, ask which one they mean. Unit contribution: the number behind most brand math Contribution margin is how much a product's selling price exceeds its variable cost per unit. It's called a contribution because it's what each unit adds toward covering fixed costs. Brand decisions turn on it. Whether a display fee, a TV flight or a new flavor that steals sales from the old one pays off comes down to how many extra units you sell and what each one contributes. What you control You don't control every line, but you influence most of them, usually through a partner: P&L line Your lever Who you need Gross sales Price, pack sizes, which retailers carry you Sales, Finance Trade spend How deep, how often and with which retailers you promote Sales Cost of goods Formula, packaging, pack size R&D, Operations Advertising Budget, media mix, creative Agencies, Insights Overheads Mostly not yours Leadership When a case hands you a promotion or a price change, walk down the P&L out loud: what happens to units, net price, gross profit and contribution. Don't stop at volume. Self-check: Your director wants more profit next year with no extra budget. Name three places on the P&L to look. Trade spend first: cut promotions that don't pay back, since that money comes straight off net sales. Then cost of goods: a packaging or formula saving that consumers won't notice. Then price and mix: a price or pack-size change shoppers will accept, and more of the siz"}, {"id": "bm/getting-started/brand-management-interviews-and-the-marketing-case", "text": "The biggest interview in brand management lasts all summer. Large CPG companies rely heavily on summer internships as a pipeline to full-time offers. At Keurig Dr Pepper, MBA brand interns end the summer with a 25- to 30-minute strategy presentation to senior marketing leaders, followed by questions. So you're really competing twice: in the interviews that win you the internship, then over the summer that turns it into an offer. The interviews come first. The steps Companies publish their hiring steps, and they look alike: Company The steps it lists Clorox Online application, an introductory phone interview, possibly a short online assessment, interviews (virtual panels, one-on-ones or in person), then a decision and offer PepsiCo Application review, a recruiter conversation, one or more interviews that ask for real examples using its GREAT5 framework, then a decision and offer Keurig Dr Pepper Uses HireVue, an automated interview tool, in its campus recruiting P&G's process is application, assessment, interviews and offer, and for most roles the assessment is a hard gate: the results alone decide whether your application moves forward. The Peak Performance Assessment measures your experience and work-related attitudes against P&G's competencies, and some roles add an interactive test of cognitive ability. Fail one and you can try again after 12 months. Interviews are behavioral and most last around 45 to 60 minutes. P&G also expects you to do the assessments and interviews on your own, with no real-time help from other people or from AI. P&G's own talent team walks through both assessments in this video, including the games in the interactive one and the retake rules. Play video: How do P&G online assessments work? How do P&G online assessments work? P&G Careers, Oct 2026 · 14 min · starts at 2:56 · YouTube What each round tests Yale's career office says CPG interviews typically combine behavioral and case interviews, reflecting the industry's emphasis on structured thinking and practical business judgment. Assessments screen at scale. Treat them like an exam: learn the format in advance so nothing surprises you. Behavioral interviews check whether you've already done the job somewhere else: led without authority, delivered a result, recovered from a setback. Bring specific stories with numbers in them. Case interviews check whether you think like the owner of a business. P&G tells MBA candidates that its brand internship \"isn't a marketing internship,\" but a general management one. The marketing case isn't a consulting case A Chicago Booth student who came in ready for behavioral questions was surprised by marketing cases, which were nothing like consulting cases: much shorter and more to the point. At a Booth prep workshop, a Booth alum working in marketing at Kraft Heinz explained that companies love cases because they mirror challenges brand managers face every day, and told students to start with the customer. A year later, at another Booth prep session, alumni agreed on one top tip: consider the consumer first, and use a consumer insight as the thread that ties your analysis and recommendation together. Formats vary. A Rice MBA student's interviews for a Campbell's brand internship were a phone screen with one associate brand manager, then a final round with two senior associate managers that included a case. A structure that ends in a recommendation There's no official framework, but strong answers tend to move in this order: Clarify the goal. Growth, share, profit or defense? Over what time? What number would mean success? Start with the consumer. Who buys, why, and what's changing in what they need or choose. Look at the company and the competition. Your brand's strengths, costs and capacity; what rivals and store brands are doing; what the retailer wants. Choose who and how. With the STP model, you segment the market, select a target and position your product for it. Build the plan with the 4Ps: product, price, place and promotion. Run the number that decides it. Breakeven volume, cannibalization or payback. Recommend, with a test. Say what you'd do, what result would change your mind and how you'd test it before going national. Interviewers listen for a clear recommendation, math you show out loud and a consumer insight that holds the answer together. \"It depends\" is where a strong answer starts, never where it ends. Self-check: An interviewer says, \"Our frozen pizza brand lost two share points at one retailer this year. What would you do?\" Write your first three questions. Something like: Is the loss only at this retailer, or everywhere? Did anything change on shelf there: price, promotions, displays, distribution or out-of-stocks? Who are we losing shoppers to, a rival brand or the store brand, and why? Those three questions split the problem into the retailer, the shelf and the consumer before you reach for a fix."}, {"id": "bm/getting-started/the-budget-line-case", "text": "Hypothetical scenario. The brand and all numbers are made up to show the work. The facts. Your interviewer slides a page across the table: Casa Verde is a premium pasta sauce. Its 24-ounce jar sells for $4.49 on shelf, and the brand earns $1.40 on each jar it ships. Over the past year it lost two share points. Most of the lost buyers switched to store brands at two big grocery chains, where the store-brand jar sells for $2.29. The team wants to launch Casa Verde Everyday: a simpler recipe in the same size jar, priced at $2.99. The brand would earn $0.63 on each one. A concept test suggests that about half of the Everyday jars sold would replace a regular Casa Verde jar the shopper would otherwise have bought. Your task: \"Should we launch Everyday?\" You have five minutes. Walk through your clarifying questions, your structure, the one number that decides the case and your recommendation. +++ hints - Start with the goal, not the math. What is the team trying to win back, from whom, and at which retailers? Write one clarifying question first. - Every Everyday jar earns less than a regular jar. What happens to profit when someone who would have bought a regular jar buys Everyday instead? - Work out how many Everyday jars can come from regular-jar buyers before the launch loses money: compare Everyday's margin with the regular jar's. Then hold that number up against the concept test. +++ outline Clarify: The goal is . The time frame is . The share loss is mostly at . Shopper: The people switching are , because . The number: Breakeven cannibalization = ___ ÷ ___ = %. The concept test says %. So the launch . Competition and retailer: The retailer will care about . The 4 Ps, if we go ahead: Product . Price . Place . Promotion . Recommendation: I would , because . I'd test it by . +++ reveal A strong five-minute answer 1. Clarify (30 seconds). \"What's the goal here: winning back share, protecting profit, or protecting shelf space at those two chains? Over what time frame? Is the share loss everywhere, or mostly at those two retailers?\" 2. Start with the shopper (1 minute). Who's switching, and why? If they're leaving because money is tight, a cheaper Casa Verde could win them back. If they're leaving because they can't taste a difference, a cheaper version of the same promise won't fix it, and the real problem is that the premium jar isn't earning its price. 3. Run the number that decides it (1 minute). The general formula: breakeven cannibalization = the new item's margin ÷ the old item's margin. Here, $0.63 ÷ $1.40 = 45% . Why it works: each Everyday jar earns $0.63, but every one that replaces a regular jar also costs you that jar's $1.40. You break even when $0.63 = (share of jars that replace a regular one) × $1.40. If more than 45% of Everyday jars sold replace a regular jar, the launch loses money. The concept test says about 50%. For every 100 Everyday jars sold, the brand earns $63, but gives up 50 regular jars worth $70. That's $7 lost per 100 jars, before paying for the launch itself. 4. Check the competition and the retailer (1 minute). The retailer makes money on its own store brand, so a buyer may not want a third pasta sauce sitting between the store brand and the regular Casa Verde. Expect the buyer to ask what Everyday does for the category, not just for you. 5. Sketch the 4Ps if you'd go ahead (30 seconds). A recipe and label clearly different from the regular jar, a price near the store brand, shelf placement next to the store brand rather than next to the regular jar, and light support aimed at shoppers in store. 6. Recommend, with a number (1 minute). \"As designed, I wouldn't launch it nationally. At 50% cannibalization against a 45% breakeven, it loses money on every jar. I'd look at two fixes: raise Everyday's margin, or make it different enough from the regular jar that fewer regular buyers trade down. Then I'd test it at the two chains where we're losing the most, and launch more widely only if fewer than 45% of its sales come out of the regular jar.\" Why this answer works It starts with the shopper, not a framework. The \"why are they switching?\" question changes everything after it. It brings one number that decides the case. Breakeven cannibalization turns \"it might cannibalize\" into a clear yes-or-no test. It ends with a recommendation and a test, not \"it depends.\" What interviewers push on \"What if those shoppers were leaving for the store brand anyway?\" Then those sales aren't really cannibalized. Cannibalization means sales the regular jar would have kept without Everyday. If 25 of those 50 regular jars would have gone to the store brand regardless, only 25 are truly lost, and Everyday makes money. Always compare against what happens if you do nothing, not against today. \"Why not just cut the price of the regular jar?\" Run the math. A price cut shrinks the margin on every jar you already sell, including to loyal buyers who'd pay full price. Everyday only gives up margin on the buyers who switch. \"How much would you trust the concept test?\" Not fully. People often say they'll buy things they don't. Treat the 50% as a starting point, then measure the real number in a market test. Practice twist The operations team finds a cheaper jar, and Everyday's margin rises to $0.84. What's the new breakeven, and does your recommendation change? Answer: $0.84 ÷ $1.40 = 60%. With the concept test at 50%, Everyday now earns $84 per 100 jars and gives up $70, so it makes money. You'd still test first, because the 50% is only an estimate."}, {"id": "bm/getting-started/marketing-math-sprint", "text": "Hypothetical numbers, made up for practice. The facts. Eight questions, all from a made-up snack brand. Set a 10-minute timer and write one line of math for each. A box of crackers sells to the retailer for $5.00 and costs $3.50 to make. What's the margin %? What's the markup %? A bag of chips sells on shelf for $3.99. The retailer keeps 25% of the shelf price, and the brand earns 35% of what the retailer pays. How much does the brand earn per bag? A retailer offers an end-cap display for $600,000. Each extra unit sold earns $1.20. How many extra units do you need to break even? If the display adds 40,000 units a week, how many weeks does that take? A product earns $1.50 a unit. Packaging costs rise $0.25 a unit, and you don't raise the price. How much more volume do you need to earn the same total margin? A new flavor earns $0.72 a unit. The original flavor it may steal from earns $1.20. What's the breakeven cannibalization rate? Brand sales grew from $50 million to $62 million over three years. What's the compound annual growth rate? The brand sells 40,000 small bags at $2.49 and 10,000 large bags at $4.99. What's the average price per bag? Price elasticity is −1.5, and you raise the price 6%. What happens to units? What happens to revenue? Your task: Answer all eight, then check which tool each question needed. +++ hints - Before you calculate anything, name the tool each question needs: margin or markup, the channel chain, breakeven, CAGR, a weighted average or elasticity. - Watch what you divide by. Margin divides by price and markup divides by cost, and each partner's margin comes off that partner's own selling price. - For #4 and #5, write total margin = units × margin per unit and set the old total equal to the new one. For #6, growth compounds, so you need a root, not a division. +++ outline 1. Margin = ___ ÷ ___ = . Markup = ___ ÷ ___ = . 2. The retailer pays ___ × (1 − ) = . The brand earns ___ × ___ = . 3. Breakeven units = ___ ÷ ___ = . Weeks to pay back = ___ ÷ ___ = . 4. Extra volume needed = ___ ÷ ___ − 1 = . 5. Breakeven cannibalization = ___ ÷ ___ = . 6. CAGR = ( ÷ )^(1 ÷ ) − 1 = . 7. Average price = total revenue ___ ÷ total units ___ = . 8. Units: ___ × ___ = . Revenue: (1 + ) × (1 + ) − 1 = . +++ reveal Answers # Math Answer 1 $1.50 ÷ $5.00; $1.50 ÷ $3.50 30% margin; 43% markup 2 $3.99 × 75% = $2.99 to the brand; × 35% About $1.05 a bag 3 $600,000 ÷ $1.20; 500,000 ÷ 40,000 500,000 extra units; 12.5 weeks 4 $1.50 ÷ $1.25 − 1 20% more volume 5 $0.72 ÷ $1.20 60% 6 (62 ÷ 50)^(1/3) − 1 About 7.4% a year 7 ($99,600 + $49,900) ÷ 50,000 $2.99 8 −1.5 × 6%; 1.06 × 0.91 − 1 Units fall about 9%; revenue falls about 3.5% How each one works 1. Margin vs. markup The formulas: margin % = (price − cost) ÷ price. Markup % = (price − cost) ÷ cost. The math: $5.00 − $3.50 = $1.50 of profit. $1.50 ÷ $5.00 = 30% margin, and $1.50 ÷ $3.50 = 43% markup. Why: it's the same $1.50, measured against two different bases. Margin asks what share of the selling price is profit, which is how a P&L works, since every line is a share of net sales. Markup asks how much was added on top of cost, which is how many retailers think about pricing. To convert: margin = markup ÷ (1 + markup), so 43% ÷ 1.43 = 30%. Markup = margin ÷ (1 − margin), so 30% ÷ 0.70 = 43%. The trap: a buyer quotes one while you're thinking in the other. Ask which they mean. 2. The channel chain The formula: what each partner pays = the next partner's selling price × (1 − that partner's margin). Your profit = your selling price × your margin. The math: the retailer keeps 25% of $3.99, so it pays the brand $3.99 × 0.75 = $2.99. The brand keeps 35% of its own $2.99: $2.99 × 0.35 = about $1.05. Why: each partner's margin is a share of that partner's own selling price, so you work backward from the shelf one step at a time. The trap: taking the brand's 35% off the shelf price ($3.99 × 0.35 = $1.40) overstates what you earn by 35 cents a bag. 3. Breakeven volume The formula: breakeven units = fixed cost ÷ contribution per unit. Weeks to pay back = breakeven units ÷ extra units per week. The math: $600,000 ÷ $1.20 = 500,000 extra units. 500,000 ÷ 40,000 a week = 12.5 weeks. Why: a fixed cost doesn't change with volume. Each extra unit chips away at it by its contribution, the price minus what it costs to make and ship one more. The trap: counting every unit sold during the display. If the store sells 100,000 units a week while the display is up, but only 40,000 of them are extra, using 100,000 says you pay back in 5 weeks instead of 12.5. Then check the answer against how long the display runs: if it's up for four weeks, it can't earn back a cost that needs 12.5 weeks of extra sales. 4. Volume needed after a margin cut The formula: extra volume needed = old unit margin ÷ new unit margin − 1. The math: the new margin is $1.50 − $0.25 = $1.25. $1.50 ÷ $1.25 = 1.20, so you need 20% more volume. Why: total margin = units × margin per unit. To keep the total the same, old units × $1.50 must equal new units × $1.25, so new units ÷ old units = $1.50 ÷ $1.25. Where else it works: any time you give up margin per unit, including a price cut. For example, cutting price by $0.30 drops a $1.50 margin to $1.20, so you'd need $1.50 ÷ $1.20 − 1 = 25% more volume just to stand still. 5. Breakeven cannibalization The formula: breakeven cannibalization rate = new item's unit margin ÷ old item's unit margin. The math: $0.72 ÷ $1.20 = 60%. Why: each new unit earns $0.72. If a share c of those units would otherwise have been the original flavor, each one also costs c × $1.20 in lost original sales. You break even when $0.72 = c × $1.20, so c = 60%. Check with 100 new units: you earn $72, and losing 60 original units costs 60 × $1.20 = $72. The trap: it looks like #4, but there's no \"− 1\" and the margins are the other way up. Say which formula you're using before you divide. 6. Compound annual growth rate (CAGR) The formula: CAGR = (ending value ÷ starting value)"}, {"id": "bm/getting-started/which-brand-manager-job-is-this", "text": "Hypothetical postings. The companies are made up, and the wording is written to show the patterns you'll see in real ones. The facts. Three postings, all titled \"Brand Manager\": A. Harvest Lane Foods, frozen meals. \"Own the brand's annual plan and P&L. Read Circana and shipment data weekly and lead the monthly business review. Partner with Sales on customer plans, with Finance on the forecast and with R&D on product improvements. Manage the A&P budget and our agencies.\" B. Fieldnote, project-management software. \"Be the guardian of our brand identity across web, product and events. Keep our voice and visual guidelines current, brief design and agency partners, run brand campaigns, and track awareness and social engagement.\" C. Ridgeback, a fast-growing pet food startup sold online and at a national pet chain. \"Run the brand's growth with our founders: manage our Amazon and retail accounts, plan promotions, own our social channels and creator program, and report weekly sales and margin.\" Your task: For each posting, say which kind of brand manager job it is and name the two phrases that told you. Then say how you'd prep differently for an interview at A versus B. +++ hints - Ignore the titles. In each posting, underline what you would own and what you would be judged on. - Sort the words you underlined: business words (P&L, share, forecast, shipments) versus brand words (identity, voice, guidelines, awareness). - A posting can be both. For each one, ask which interview question it would most likely open with: a case with numbers, or a walk through your creative work? +++ outline A: This is . The phrases that told me: \" \" and \" \". B: This is . The phrases that told me: \" \" and \" \". C: This is . The phrases that told me: \" \" and \" \". To prep for A, I'd practice . To prep for B, I'd practice ___. +++ reveal The answers Posting Kind of job Phrases that give it away A CPG brand management \"P&L\", \"Circana and shipment data\", \"monthly business review\", \"Finance on the forecast\" B Brand identity and marketing \"guardian of our brand identity\", \"voice and visual guidelines\", \"awareness and social engagement\" C A blend of both, common at smaller companies \"Amazon and retail accounts\" and \"weekly sales and margin\" (business), plus \"social channels and creator program\" (brand) How you'd prep differently For A, expect a marketing case with math: breakeven, a P&L, a promotion that may not pay back, or a business to diagnose from share and distribution data. Bring behavioral stories about getting Sales or Finance to back your plan. For B, expect to walk through campaigns: one you'd run, one you'd critique, and how you'd keep a brand consistent across many teams. A portfolio or work samples may matter more than math. For C, prep both, plus channel economics: what Amazon fees and retailer promotions do to margin, and how you'd split a small budget between selling and storytelling. Why it matters The title doesn't tell you what the interview will test. The verbs in the posting do. Match your \"why brand management?\" answer to the job you're actually interviewing for, too: \"I want to run a business\" lands at A and falls flat at B. Practice twist A posting is titled \"Talent Brand Manager.\" Which kind is it? Answer: neither. Talent brand (or employer brand) roles market the company to job candidates, usually inside recruiting or HR. One more reason to read past the title."}, {"id": "bm/reading-the-business/share-distribution-and-velocity", "text": "In the quarter that ended in September 2026, two brands both added a lot of shelf space. BellRing's Premier Protein shakes grew their total distribution points, a measure of how much shelf presence a brand has, 36% over the year before. Their consumption grew 9%, by a measure that includes BellRing's own estimates for channels the data doesn't track. Freshpet grew its distribution points 14% and its Nielsen-measured sales 12.7%. Same move, very different payoff, at least on the headline numbers. Freshpet's extra shelf space turned into sales almost point for point, while Premier Protein's turned into about a quarter as much. To see what's going on, you need three numbers, and the third one does most of the explaining. First, where the numbers come from Every time a product is scanned at a store checkout, the sale is recorded. Firms like Circana and NielsenIQ collect those scans from retailers, combine them and sell the result to brands, so you can see your own sales and your competitors' week by week. People call it scanner data, or syndicated data, because the same dataset is sold to many companies. Not every retailer shares its scans, which is why companies sometimes add their own estimates for \"untracked channels\". Here are the words you'll hear most, in plain English: Term What it means A quick example Category The set of products shoppers treat as alternatives, as the data provider defines it All ready-to-drink protein shakes Item (or SKU) One specific product: one flavor in one size Chocolate, 11-ounce, 4-pack Dollar sales What shoppers spent on you at the register $2 million last quarter Unit sales How many packs shoppers bought 500,000 packs Dollar share Your dollar sales ÷ the category's dollar sales $2M of a $20M category = 10% Unit share Your unit sales ÷ the category's unit sales 500,000 of 4 million packs = 12.5% ACV (all commodity volume) A store's total sales of everything it sells, used to measure how big the store is A supercenter's ACV is many times a corner store's %ACV distribution How much of the market's total store sales happen in stores that carry your item 80% ACV: the stores carrying you do 80% of all sales TDPs (total distribution points) %ACV added up across all your items 3 items at 50% ACV each = 150 TDPs Velocity How fast you sell where you're stocked: sales per point of distribution, or per store per week $1,500 per point of ACV a week Consumption (or takeaway) What shoppers bought, from scanner data, as opposed to what you shipped to retailers Consumption up 9%, shipments up 15% Share: your slice of the category Share is your sales as a percentage of the whole category's sales. Run it on dollars and you get dollar share. Run it on units and you get unit share. A premium brand's dollar share sits above its unit share, because each unit costs more, and a value brand's sits below. When the two move in opposite directions, price or mix is doing the work: a higher price, or more sales of pricier items. Share also answers a different question from sales. In the third quarter of 2026, Vital Farms reached a record dollar and volume share of shell eggs, while its net revenue fell 8.4%, mostly from lower price and mix. Share is relative: if the category's dollars fall faster than yours, you gain share while selling less. The reverse happens too, so never report one without the other. Distribution: how many shelves you're on Store count treats a corner store the same as a supercenter. Percent ACV distribution fixes that by weighting each store by its total sales. If the stores that carry your product account for $800 million of a $1 billion market's sales, you're at 80% ACV. Here's why the weighting matters. For example, say a small town has three stores: a supercenter that sells $8 million a year of everything, a grocery store that sells $1.5 million and a corner store that sells $0.5 million. If only the supercenter carries you, you're in one store out of three, but you're at 80% ACV, because that store does $8 million of the town's $10 million in sales. Shoppers can find you where most of the shopping happens. Total distribution points (TDPs) add up the %ACV of every item in your line. For example, five items each at 60% ACV give you 300 TDPs. TDPs grow when more stores carry you, or when the stores that already carry you stock more of your items. For example, say two brands are both at 70% ACV. One has six items in every one of those stores (6 × 70 = 420 TDPs), and the other has two (140 TDPs). Same stores, but the first has three times the presence on shelf, and usually far more facings for shoppers to notice. Freshpet's numbers show why the second part matters. Its store count rose 5% to more than 31,000 stores, but its distribution points rose 14%, because more stores put in larger and double fridges. Three-quarters of those stores still have only one fridge, and when one club retailer installed very large fridges, the company said its velocities jumped too. Velocity: how fast each shelf sells Velocity is sales divided by distribution: per store, per TDP or per million dollars of ACV. It lets you compare items fairly, whatever their footprint. For example, say your granola sells $90,000 a week at 60% ACV. That's $90,000 ÷ 60 = $1,500 per point of distribution. A rival sells $60,000 a week at only 30% ACV, which is $2,000 per point. The rival sells less in total, but where shoppers can see both brands, they pick the rival faster. With your distribution, and if its velocity held, the rival would outsell you. Put the three together and you get the line every brand manager carries around: sales = distribution × velocity. Check it on the granola, for example: 60 points × $1,500 = $90,000 a week. Back to the opening puzzle. On the headline numbers, Freshpet's sales per distribution point held roughly steady. Most of its new points were bigger fridges in stores that already sold it, since its store count rose only 5%. Premier Protein's sales per point fell by roughl"}, {"id": "bm/reading-the-business/diagnosing-a-business", "text": "In the same summer quarter of 2026, two snack and nutrition companies reported sales bridges that were mirror images. Hershey's organic sales rose 0.8%: net price added about 11 points, while volume fell about 10 points, which Hershey put down to elasticity, shipment timing and planned portfolio changes. BellRing's net sales rose 6.8% on volume up 14.9% and price/mix down 8.1%. For its Premier Protein brand, BellRing said the volume came from extra promotion and new distribution, and the extra promotional spending weighed on price/mix. Hershey raised prices and sold less. BellRing cut its effective prices and sold more. Neither headline number, 0.8% or 6.8%, tells you whether the business is healthy. The bridge underneath does. Words to know A sales bridge (some teams call it a walk) splits a change in sales into pieces that add up to the total. These are the pieces you'll see: Term What it means A quick example Net sales What the company books: its shipments to retailers, after trade discounts The revenue line in an earnings release Organic sales Sales growth after removing currency swings and businesses bought or sold Reported +5%, minus 3 points from an acquisition = +2% organic FX Foreign exchange: currency moves that change what overseas sales are worth in dollars A stronger euro makes European sales worth more M&A Mergers and acquisitions: businesses bought or sold Buying a coconut water producer Volume How many units, cases or pounds moved 1 million cases Price/mix Sales change from price, plus shifts toward pricier or cheaper items Selling more large bags raises your average price Net price realization How much of a price change actually reached net sales, after discounts List price up 10%, deeper deals give back 2 points Elasticity How much volume changes when price changes −1.5 means a 10% price rise cuts units about 15% Base volume What you'd sell at regular price, with no promotion running 1,000 units a week Incremental volume The extra units a promotion adds on top of base 800 more units during the deal Trade promotion Temporary price cuts, displays and features at a retailer, paid for by the brand \"2 for $5\" Retail takeaway What shoppers bought, from scanner data The same thing as consumption Lapping Comparing against the same period a year earlier Lapping a big launch makes this year look weak Step 1: Take out what isn't the brand Start by removing currency and acquisitions, because neither tells you anything about shoppers. Conagra's net sales fell 1.4% in the quarter ended August 2026: currency added 0.2%, acquisitions and divestitures took away 0.5%, and organic sales fell 1.1%. Inside that organic number, price/mix added 1.0% and volume fell 2.1%, yet Conagra gained dollar share in categories from frozen vegetables to hot dogs. Volume down with share up usually means the category is shrinking faster than you are. That's a category problem first and a brand problem second. Step 2: Split volume from price and mix Price/mix bundles two things: changes in what you charge, and shifts in what people buy, such as more of a cheaper size. Volume is how many units moved. When price is up and volume is down, the question is elasticity: did you lose more units than the price increase was worth? When volume is up and price is down, the question is what you paid for it. Here's a bridge in miniature. For example, say last year you sold 100 units at $2.00, which is $200. This year you sold 95 units at $2.20, which is $209. Sales grew 4.5%, but volume fell 5% and price rose 10%. The pieces multiply: 0.95 × 1.10 = 1.045. In quarterly results, companies show them as points that roughly add up, the way Hershey showed about 11 points of price against about 10 points of lost volume. Step 3: Split base from promoted volume Base volume is what you'd sell at your regular price. Incremental volume is what a promotion adds on top. The catch is that every promotion also discounts sales you'd have made anyway. Circana calls that subsidization: the share of promoted volume you likely would have sold at base price. For example, say you normally sell 1,000 units a week at full price. During a two-week promotion, you sell 1,800 a week. About 1,000 of those you'd have sold anyway, so they're subsidized, and only about 800 are incremental. You gave the discount on all 1,800 units to win roughly 800 extra. Whether that pays depends on the margin you gave up on the units you'd have sold anyway, which is exactly the math in a promotion breakeven. Watch how much of your category sells on deal. In Premier Protein's ready-to-drink shake category, 25% of volume sold on a price reduction in the quarter, up 5 points from a year earlier. When that number climbs, shoppers learn to wait for the deal, and your base gets harder to read. Step 4: Check shipments against what shoppers bought Your net sales are shipments to retailers. Scanner data is what shoppers bought. Simply Good Foods' OWYN shakes saw net sales fall 3.3% in the quarter it reported in January 2026, while retail takeaway rose 17.8%. Retailers had started the quarter holding too much OWYN inventory, a lingering effect of an earlier product quality issue. Shoppers were buying more. Retailers were ordering less. Step 5: Check what you're lapping Every comparison has a base. Hershey's candy retail takeaway grew 0.8% in its third quarter, but because it was lapping a big Reese launch from the year before, its share fell. A launch, a stock-up or an extra week last year can make an ordinary month look like a slide. Sales moved because of... Check Currency or acquisitions Organic sales Price or mix Net price per unit, and which sizes and items grew Volume Distribution × velocity, and base vs. promoted Retailer ordering Scanner sales vs. net sales, and retailer inventory The comparison What happened in the same period last year In this Startup CPG webinar, presenters who introduce themselves as NielsenIQ staff break a frozen pizza's year-over-year sales change"}, {"id": "bm/reading-the-business/panel-data-penetration-and-loyalty", "text": "Freshpet's sales grew 10% in the third quarter of 2026, yet it added few new buyers. Its third-quarter report showed household penetration up 1%, while the total buy rate, what each buying household spends, was up 9%. A group of households it calls Most Valuable Pet Parents made up 72% of its sales, and that group grew 8%. Freshpet is the exception rather than the rule. Across the brands Worldpanel by Numerator tracks, 68% of the brands that grew in 2025 added households. Both things are true, and panel data is how you find out which story your brand is in. Words to know Term What it means A quick example Household panel A large sample of households that record what they buy, so you can follow the same people over time Homes that scan their receipts or barcodes Buyers Households that bought you at least once in the period 1,200 households Penetration Buyers as a share of all households 1,200 of 10,000 households = 12% Buy rate What each buying household spends on you in the period $40 a year Purchase frequency How many times a buyer bought you in the period 4 trips a year Spend per trip What a buyer spends on you each time $10 a trip Repeat rate The share of buyers who come back for another purchase Half of first-time buyers buy again Light and heavy buyers Buyers who buy you rarely vs. often Once a year vs. every week Share of requirements Your share of a buyer's total spending in the category, a common loyalty measure $40 of their $100 on coffee = 40% Repertoire The handful of brands a shopper rotates between Three yogurt brands, bought in turn These definitions are standard industry usage; providers word them slightly differently. What a panel measures Scanner data tells you what sold. A household panel tells you who bought it: a large sample of homes that record what they buy, so you can follow the same people over time. Penetration is the number of buying households divided by all households, times 100. From there, the math is simple: sales = households in the market × penetration × buy rate, and buy rate = trips per buyer × spend per trip. For example, say a town has 10,000 households, and 2,000 of them bought your salsa this year: 20% penetration. Each buyer bought it 4 times at $3 a trip, so the buy rate is $12, and sales are 10,000 × 20% × $12 = $24,000. To grow 10%, you could add 200 buying households, or get each buyer to spend $1.20 more. The panel tells you which of those actually happened. Number What it tells you What usually moves it Penetration How many households bought you at least once Distribution, visibility, advertising that reaches light buyers, trial sizes Purchase frequency How often buyers come back Habit, being easy to find, being remembered Spend per trip How much they buy each time Pack sizes, price, multi-buy offers Why most growth comes from more buyers Researchers at the Ehrenberg-Bass Institute call it double jeopardy: small brands have fewer buyers, and those buyers are slightly less loyal. The pattern was first spotted in the 1960s and shows up in many categories, from packaged goods to retail banking. Writing about business markets in 2021, the institute's conclusion is blunt: you can't grow sustainably by focusing on loyalty alone, because a bigger brand has many more buyers who are only slightly more loyal. For example, say Brand A reaches 24% of households and its buyers buy it 4 times a year, while Brand B reaches 8% and its buyers buy it 3 times. A sells four times as much as B, and almost all of that gap is the number of buyers, not how loyal they are. That's the typical shape of double jeopardy. Light buyers also matter more than the old 80/20 rule suggests. Byron Sharp and Charles Graham found the real split is closer to 60/20 over five years, about 50/20 over a year and about 40/20 in a quarter. So the light 80% of buyers deliver almost half of a brand's sales. That doesn't mean loyalty is irrelevant. Sharp himself writes that some growth comes from existing heavier buyers becoming a little more frequent and loyal. He also notes that in categories where people rotate between brands, penetration grows two ways: recruiting new buyers, and getting the many very light buyers who don't buy you every period to buy a little more often. Recruiting isn't enough The same Brand Footprint report has a second finding. Of the brands that recruited new households in 2024, only 22% kept them and were chosen more often a year later. What set those brands apart was a habit forming early: two in three were being bought more often inside their first year. So the panel question is never just \"did we add buyers?\" It's \"did we add buyers, and did they come back?\" Freshpet sits at the other end for now: a small brand in a fresh pet food category it's still converting shoppers into, with a heavy-buyer group that already drives most of its sales. Even there, part of the buy-rate rise came from having more of those heavy-buying households, the mirror image of the mix trap. How to read a panel report Read penetration and buy rate together. Either one alone can mislead. Watch for the mix trap. When light buyers drift away, the average buy rate rises even if nobody changed their habits. Check the time window. Over a longer window, more light buyers show up, so penetration rises and buy rate grows less than the window does. Only compare a 52-week number with another 52-week number. Ask where lapsed buyers went. A rival brand, the store brand or out of the category each calls for a different fix. Byron Sharp revisits the laws of brand growth in this conversation, including penetration, double jeopardy and why heavy buyers of a brand often buy it less than you'd think. Play video: Revisiting 'How Brands Grow' with Byron Sharp Revisiting 'How Brands Grow' with Byron Sharp The WARC Podcast, Aug 2026 · 45 min · starts at 4:00 · YouTube Self-check: Your brand's sales are flat. Penetration fell from 20% to 18%, and buy rate rose 11%. Your director says loyalty is up. What do you "}, {"id": "bm/reading-the-business/the-monthly-business-review", "text": "Read two earnings headlines from the same season. Vita Coco's third-quarter 2026 release led with net sales up 34%, driven by coconut water growth of 15%, with organic growth of 18% right there in the same line. Read the tables and you find why both numbers are needed: about 16 points of the 34% came from buying Copra, a coconut water producer. Vital Farms' third-quarter headline led with revenue up 9.7% from the second quarter and a record share of shell eggs. Its revenue was down 8.4% from the year before, a number that appears further down the release. Both headlines are accurate, and each one chose its numbers. In your monthly review, your director will read past your headline the way an analyst reads past these, so write one that survives the second read. Words to know Term What it means Monthly business review A regular meeting where the brand team reports how the business did and what should happen next. Some teams call it a business update or performance review Scorecard The same set of measures shown every month, so changes stand out Headline One sentence that says what happened, why, and what you want Driver The cause behind a number moving, like distribution, price or a competitor's promotion The ask The decision you want leadership to make Appendix Backup detail you don't present but can pull up if asked Names and formats vary by company; the parts above are common to most. Lead with the answer Barbara Minto's Pyramid Principle puts it simply: your thinking is easy for a reader to grasp when you present your ideas organized as a pyramid under a single point. McKinsey's alumni profile of Minto describes the problem she kept finding in reports: findings and conclusions jumbled together, with neither leading clearly to a recommendation. In her words, \"The problem was the thinking, not the language\". A list isn't a point. Minto's own course warns that saying there are three reasons for a change is not an insight in itself. The point at the top has to say what the reasons add up to. From numbers to a headline in three steps List what moved, and by how much. For example: sales down 3.5%, units down 9%, our price up 6% since March, distribution flat, and our main competitor held its price. Group the facts by cause. Units fell right after our price rose, distribution didn't change, and the competitor didn't follow. That's one story, not five numbers. Say what they add up to, then what you want. \"Sales fell 3.5% because shoppers bought 9% fewer pizzas after our March price increase, which our main competitor didn't match. We recommend testing a smaller price gap at our two largest retailers before deciding whether to keep it.\" Check the math before you present it: 1.06 × 0.91 = 0.965, so a 6% price rise and 9% fewer units do give about 3.5% lower sales. The headline should survive that check. What, so what, now what Stanford's Matt Abrahams calls \"What? So what? Now what?\" the most widely useful structure he knows: what's happening, why it matters to this audience, and what should happen next. Here's how it maps onto a monthly review: Part In your review For example What The month in one line, with the driver \"Share slipped as a rival's new item took display space at our biggest retailer\" So what What it means for the plan \"Our base velocity elsewhere is holding, so this is one account, not the brand\" Now what The decision you need \"Approve a shelf-and-display plan with that retailer for next quarter\" When the numbers go off track OpenStax describes the loop every review runs on. If the numbers stay within the forecast range, move on. When they fall outside it, find the root cause and adjust. Your review is where that root cause gets named, with a number behind it. A one-page review that works The headline: what happened, why, and what you want. The same scorecard every month: sales, share, distribution, velocity, price, the share of volume sold on promotion, and consumption against shipments. Two or three drivers, each tied to a number from the scorecard. Timing vs. real change: what you're lapping, retailer inventory moves, calendar shifts. Decisions, each with an owner and a date. An appendix for everything that doesn't support the headline. That structure is a working habit rather than a rule, so adapt it to your team. Common traps Walking the scorecard in order. Leadership has to do the synthesis for you. \"A mixed month.\" That says you haven't decided what the month meant. Explaining timing as performance. A shipment shift or a lapped launch isn't a trend. Burying the bad number. It will come up anyway, so put it where you can frame it. Cole Nussbaumer Knaflic turns a cluttered data presentation into a single \"big idea\" sentence and a specific recommendation, with a full before-and-after makeover. Play video: transform: a storytelling with data mini-workshop transform: a storytelling with data mini-workshop Cole Nussbaumer Knaflic, storytelling with data, Jun 2025 · 52 min · starts at 2:10 · YouTube Self-check: Dollar sales +3%, category +6%, share down 0.4 points, %ACV up 5%, velocity down 2%. Write the headline. Something like: \"We grew 3% only because we're in more stores. Each store is selling 2% less of us while the category grows 6%, so we're losing share, and we need to find out why shoppers are choosing us less before we add more distribution.\" It names the result, the driver and the next step."}, {"id": "bm/reading-the-business/driver-based-forecasting", "text": "In May 2025, Freshpet cut its sales growth outlook for the year from 21–24% to 15–18%. Its CEO said growth hadn't been as strong as the company expected, so the company would plan as if first-quarter conditions continued for the rest of the year. It cut twice more, to 13–16% and then, in November, to about 13%. Three cuts in one year, all in the same direction, is what forecast bias looks like from the outside. Every brand misses forecasts. What matters is whether you can say which assumption broke. A forecast built on last year's growth rate just misses. A forecast built on drivers tells you where it missed, and what to change. Words to know Term What it means A quick example Forecast Your best estimate of what will sell, not what you hope will sell 1.2 million units next quarter Plan or target What the business is aiming for, which can differ from the forecast The annual budget Baseline The forecast without promotions or one-time events What you'd sell in a normal week Driver Something that changes sales in a way you can estimate Distribution, velocity, price, promotions Lift The extra sales an event adds over the baseline A promotion that lifts units 40% Post-promotion dip Lower sales after a promotion, while shoppers or retailers work through stock Two slow weeks after a big deal Forward buying A retailer buying extra during a deal to sell after it ends Orders spike, then fall Pantry loading Shoppers stocking up during a deal Buying four jars instead of one Sell-in and sell-through What you ship to retailers vs. what shoppers buy Shipments vs. consumption Forecast error The gap between actual sales and the forecast Forecast 110, actual 100: an error of 10 MAPE Mean absolute percentage error: the average size of your errors, in percent 8% Bias A tendency to miss in the same direction Always too high S&OP Sales and operations planning: the monthly process that turns forecasts into one plan for demand, supply and finance The demand review is its first step Unconstrained forecast What shoppers would buy if supply weren't a limit The number you start from These are standard planning terms; companies define a few of them slightly differently, especially bias. Build it from drivers Simply Good Foods' 2026 outlook reads like a driver-based forecast in one sentence: growth from innovation and new distribution for Quest and OWYN, offset by Atkins, with the timing of price elasticity and lapping year-ago promotions weighing on the first half. Vital Farms shows what happens when drivers slip. In November 2026, it cut its 2026 revenue outlook to around $730 million from $775 million to $800 million. Its CFO pointed to new distribution that reached shelves later than expected, a more conservative assumption for how fast that distribution would sell, and an outlook that now assumes no seasonal lift in demand or in conventional egg prices. The building blocks look like this: Driver What you forecast Base Current distribution × base velocity × weeks New distribution Which stores and items, from which week, and how fast new points ramp up Events Promotion lift, minus any dip afterward Price Planned price changes × expected elasticity Calendar Selling days, holidays, launch timing For example, say you're in 1,000 stores, selling 10 units per store per week. Over a 13-week quarter, the base is 1,000 × 10 × 13 = 130,000 units. Add 200 new stores from week 7 at half speed (200 × 5 × 7 weeks = 7,000), plus one promotion expected to add 6,000 units, minus a 1,500-unit dip after it, and the forecast is 141,500. If the quarter misses, you can check each line to see which assumption broke. Keep the base and the events apart A promotion's spike is the easy part. What happens after is harder. In a 1995 note, Duke's John McCann showed that shipments typically spike during a trade promotion and dip afterward as retailers work through the stock they bought on deal. A dip in shoppers' own purchases, by contrast, was often hard to find in weekly store data. Circana today treats a large post-promotion dip as a sign of pantry loading, or of loyalty that only shows up on deal. Measure the dip for your own brand rather than assuming it. Forecast what shoppers buy, then what retailers order Monster's net sales fell 2.3% in the first quarter of 2025, even as Nielsen showed its retail sales strong and accelerating in most geographies. It named distributor ordering patterns, currency, weather and one fewer selling day among the causes. It also noted that billings over a short period swing with selling days, holiday timing, launches, price-increase timing, promotions and distributor incentives. So forecast consumption first, then layer on what retailers will order and when. Accuracy vs. bias A forecast error isn't a mistake, as the forecasting textbook by Rob Hyndman and George Athanasopoulos puts it. It's the unpredictable part of what happened. Percentage measures like MAPE make errors comparable across items, but they break down when sales are near zero, which matters for slow-moving items. Bias is different: a consistent gap in one direction. It often comes from incentives. If salespeople are judged on hitting quota, for example, they lean toward forecasting low. For example, say actual sales are 100 units every month. Forecasts of 110, 90, 110 and 90 have a 10% average error and no bias. Forecasts of 110, 110, 110 and 110 also have a 10% average error, but they're biased high every month. Same accuracy, very different problem. Accuracy Bias The question How far off are we? Are we always off the same way? A common measure Average absolute percentage error (MAPE) Average signed error The usual fix Better drivers and data Different incentives, and clear ownership of the number Agree on a sign convention before you report bias, since teams define error in both directions. Where your forecast goes: S&OP Sales and operations planning (S&OP) is the process that combines demand, supply and financial planning into one business pl"}, {"id": "bm/reading-the-business/flat-sales-growing-share", "text": "Hypothetical scenario. The brand and all numbers are made up to show the work. The facts. You're the associate brand manager on Brightleaf, a frozen breakfast sandwich. Here's the latest 52 weeks of scanner data against the year before: Year ago This year Change Category dollar sales $860M $800M −7% Brightleaf dollar sales $120M $120M Flat Brightleaf dollar share 14.0% 15.0% +1.0 point Brightleaf unit share 13.2% 13.0% −0.2 points Brightleaf average price per unit $4.95 $5.20 +5% Category average price per unit $4.68 $4.51 −4% Brightleaf %ACV distribution 88 88 Flat Your director is thrilled: \"Flat sales in a category down 7%, and we gained a full share point. Great year.\" Your task: In three sentences, say what's really going on, what worries you, and the one thing you'd check next. +++ hints - Start with units: dollar sales ÷ average price, for this year and last year. - Compare how your price moved with how the category's average price moved. Which way did each go? - Your director is looking at dollar share. What does unit share say, and what does the gap between the two tell you about price? +++ outline Units this year = ___ ÷ ___ = . Units last year = ___ ÷ ___ = . Change: %. Our price moved %. The category's average price moved %. Dollar share went . Unit share went . That tells me . Sentence 1, what's really going on: ___ Sentence 2, what worries me: ___ Sentence 3, what I'd check next: ___ +++ reveal The formulas Dollar share = your dollar sales ÷ category dollar sales. Unit share = your units ÷ category units. Units = dollar sales ÷ average price, so if dollars hold flat while price rises 5%, units fall about 5% (1 ÷ 1.05 = 0.952). Price premium = your average price ÷ the category's average price − 1. Why read them together: dollar share = unit share × (your price ÷ the category's average price). Dollar share can rise on price alone while unit share falls. What's really going on The dollar share gain is real, but it came from price, not shoppers. Brightleaf's sales held at $120 million while the category shrank, so dollar share rose from about 14.0% to 15.0%. Units tell a different story. Brightleaf raised its price about 5%, so the same dollars bought about 5% fewer sandwiches, and unit share slipped from 13.2% to 13.0%. The category is getting cheaper while Brightleaf gets pricier. The category's average price fell about 4%, which usually means shoppers are moving to cheaper items, store brands or deeper deals. Brightleaf's premium over the category average widened from about 6% ($4.95 vs. $4.68) to about 15% ($5.20 vs. $4.51). A strong answer, in three sentences: \"We held dollars and gained a dollar share point, but we sold about 5% fewer sandwiches and lost unit share, so the gain came from our price increase while shoppers moved toward cheaper options. Our price premium over the category has more than doubled, which is a risk if more shoppers keep trading down. Next, I'd check our price gap against the store brand and our top competitor, and whether our base velocity per store is holding.\" Why it matters Always read dollar and unit share together. Dollar share up with unit share down means you're winning on price, not on buyers. That can be a fine strategy, but say it out loud. Read share against the category. Flat sales in a shrinking category is a gain. Flat sales in a growing category is a loss. Watch the price gap. A premium that keeps widening works until it doesn't. The panel will show it first: fewer households buying you. Practice twist Next year Brightleaf holds its price at $5.20, the category falls another 5% in dollars, and Brightleaf's sales fall 2%. What's its new dollar share, and is that good news? Answer: $117.6 million ÷ $760 million ≈ 15.5%, up about half a point. Better than the category, but sales are now falling. Check whether units are falling faster than the category's, which would mean the price gap is starting to cost you shoppers."}, {"id": "bm/reading-the-business/fewer-buyers-buying-more", "text": "Hypothetical scenario. The brand and all numbers are made up to show the work. The facts. Coastal Crisp is a kettle chip brand. Household panel data for the past 52 weeks: Year ago This year Household penetration (share of U.S. households buying at least once) 12.0% 10.5% Purchase trips per buyer 4.0 4.4 Bags per trip 1.5 1.6 Average price per bag $4.00 $4.10 The brand team is pleased: \"Sales are up and loyalty is rising. Let's launch a rewards program for our heavy buyers.\" Your task: Work out what drove sales, then say whether you'd back the rewards program and what you'd do instead or as well. +++ hints - Split sales into two parts: how many households buy, and what each one spends. Work out both for each year. - Which part grew and which shrank, and by how much? - Before you accept \"loyalty is rising,\" ask who left. If the households that left were light buyers, what happens to the average spend of the ones who stayed? +++ outline Spend per buyer last year = ___ × ___ × ___ = . This year = . Penetration went from ___ to , a change of %. Sales index = penetration × spend per buyer: ___ last year vs. ___ this year ( %). Who probably left, and what that does to the average: ___ Would I back the rewards program? , because ___. What I'd do instead, or as well: ___ +++ reveal Do the math first Sales = households buying × what each buyer spends. Year ago This year Change Spend per buyer (trips × bags × price) 4.0 × 1.5 × $4.00 = $24.00 4.4 × 1.6 × $4.10 = $28.86 +20% Penetration 12.0% 10.5% −12.5% Sales index (penetration × spend) 288 303 +5% Sales grew 5%, but the brand lost one in eight of its buyers. A shrinking group spending 20% more covered the gap. Why \"loyalty is rising\" may be an illusion Some of that higher spend per buyer can be pure arithmetic. Light buyers are the easiest to lose, and when they leave, the average across the buyers who remain goes up even if nobody changed their habits. Say Coastal Crisp had 1,200 buyers in a sample: 400 heavy buyers spending $48 a year and 800 light buyers spending $12. That's an average of $24. If 150 light buyers drift away and nobody else changes, the remaining 1,050 average about $25.71, up 7%, with no one buying a single extra bag. A strong answer \"Sales are up 5%, but we lost 12.5% of our buyers, and part of the jump in spend per buyer is just light buyers leaving. A rewards program would pay the people already buying the most. I'd first pull buyer flows from the panel: how many buyers we lost and gained, and which brands the lapsed buyers went to. Then I'd put money into reaching more households: distribution where we're thin, visibility in store, and advertising that reaches light category buyers, not only our fans.\" What interviewers push on \"Isn't a loyal core a good thing?\" Yes, but a brand that only grows with fewer buyers is narrowing. Eventually the heavy buyers can't buy more chips, and there's no new base to grow from. \"How would you know whether heavy buyers really are buying more?\" Track the same households over time. If the people who bought last year are each buying more, it's real. If the average only rose because light buyers left, it isn't. Practice twist Next year, penetration recovers to 11.5% and spend per buyer falls back to $26.00. Are sales up or down from this year? Answer: 11.5 × $26.00 = 299, down about 1% from 303. Even with 10% more buyers, sales slip, because spend per buyer fell almost 10%. Recovery of buyers is the right direction, but you'd want to know why each buyer is spending less."}, {"id": "bm/reading-the-business/build-the-quarter", "text": "Hypothetical scenario. The brand and all numbers are made up to show the work. The facts. You own the second-quarter forecast (13 weeks) for Summit Oats granola bars, in units sold to shoppers: Today: Summit Oats is in 10,000 stores and sells a steady 12 units per store per week. New distribution: A grocery chain adds the brand to 2,000 more stores starting in week 5. New stores usually sell at half the normal rate for their first four weeks, then 80% after that. One promotion: A two-week price cut in weeks 7 and 8 at a different chain, where Summit Oats is already sold in 4,000 stores. Past events at that chain lifted units 60% in those stores during the promotion, followed by a 15% dip for the next two weeks while shoppers worked through their pantries. Your task: Build the quarter's forecast by component. Then name the two biggest risks and what you'd hand to the demand planning team. +++ hints - Don't start with a total. Build it line by line: the base first, then each change on top of it. - For the new stores, count the weeks at half speed and the weeks at 80% separately. - The promotion needs two lines: the lift during the event and the dip after it. Then ask how shipments will differ from what shoppers buy. +++ outline Base: ___ stores × ___ units × ___ weeks = ___ New stores, ramp weeks: ___ stores × ___ units × ___ weeks = ___ New stores, after the ramp: ___ stores × ___ units × ___ weeks = ___ Promotion lift: ___ stores × ___ extra units × ___ weeks = ___ Post-promotion dip: ___ stores × ___ fewer units × ___ weeks = ___ Total: ___ Biggest risk 1: ___ Biggest risk 2: ___ What I'd hand to demand planning: ___ +++ reveal The forecast, one driver at a time Component Math Units Base 10,000 stores × 12 × 13 weeks 1,560,000 New stores, weeks 5 to 8 2,000 × 6 (half rate) × 4 weeks 48,000 New stores, weeks 9 to 13 2,000 × 9.6 (80% rate) × 5 weeks 96,000 Promotion lift, weeks 7 and 8 4,000 × 7.2 extra (60% of 12) × 2 weeks 57,600 Post-promotion dip, weeks 9 and 10 4,000 × −1.8 (15% of 12) × 2 weeks −14,400 Total 1,747,200 Why build it this way Each line has an owner and a reason. If the quarter misses, you can see which assumption broke. A single \"up 12%\" number can't tell you that. The base and the events stay separate. Promotions and new distribution come and go. The base is what the brand does on its own, so keep it clean. Include the dip when your history shows one. Past events at this chain show a 15% dip, which gives back a quarter of the lift (14,400 of 57,600 units). Leave it out and you overstate the event's net gain by a third. The two biggest risks The new stores ramp more slowly or start later. It's the biggest addition after the base, 144,000 units, and the timing is the retailer's call, not yours. The promotion lift is borrowed from past events. If a competitor promotes the same weeks, or the display doesn't go up, the 60% lift could be half that. What you hand to demand planning The components, not just the total: the base rate, the new-store timing and ramp, the event dates, lift and dip. Also flag that shipments will run ahead of this number in weeks 4 and 5, because the chain needs to stock 2,000 new stores before a single shopper buys a bar. Expect the promoting chain to buy ahead of weeks 7 and 8, then order less for a few weeks after. Practice twist The chain pushes the new stores back to week 9. What's the new forecast? Answer: new stores now sell at half rate in weeks 9 to 12 (2,000 × 6 × 4 = 48,000) and at 80% in week 13 (2,000 × 9.6 = 19,200). Total = 1,560,000 + 67,200 + 57,600 − 14,400 = 1,670,400 units, about 76,800 fewer. Tell Operations and Finance the week the date moves, not at quarter end."}, {"id": "bm/reading-the-business/write-the-headline", "text": "Hypothetical scenario. The brand and all numbers are made up to show the work. The facts. It's your first monthly business review on Lumen laundry pods. Here's the latest four weeks against the same four weeks last year: Metric vs. year ago Lumen dollar sales +2% Lumen units −3% Category dollar sales +5% Lumen dollar share 11.2%, down 0.3 points %ACV distribution 92, up 1 point Base (non-promoted) units +8% Promoted units −29% Share of units sold on promotion 22%, down from 30% You also know two things: your team cut promotions this year on purpose to protect margin, and a competitor launched a new pod with heavy promotion two months ago. Your teammate drafted three possible headlines: \"Lumen sales grew 2% this month.\" \"Lumen lost 0.3 share points due to a number of factors in a competitive month.\" \"Lumen's share dipped 0.3 points as we cut promotions while a competitor launched with heavy deals, but our base business grew 8%, so we recommend holding the lower promotion plan.\" Your task: Pick the best headline or write a better one. Then write the two supporting lines that follow it, and the one decision you'd ask for. +++ hints - Find the number your director will ask about first. Is it good news or bad news? - Separate what you chose to do (cutting promotions) from what happened to you (a competitor's launch). Which explains the share loss? - A strong headline has four parts: what happened, why, what it means and what you want. Test each draft against all four. +++ outline What happened: ___ Why: ___ What it means: ___ What I want (the decision): ___ My headline: ___ Supporting line 1: ___ Supporting line 2: ___ The decision, with an owner and a date: ___ +++ reveal Why #3 wins #1 is true but useless. It hides the share loss and gives leadership nothing to decide. #2 names the problem and explains nothing. \"A number of factors\" tells your director you don't know yet. #3 does the job of a headline. It says what happened (share down), why (promotions cut while a rival promoted), what it means (the base looks healthy) and what to do (hold the plan). Someone who reads only that sentence still knows the month. The supporting lines The share loss is in promoted volume. Promoted units fell 29% as we cut promotions from 30% to 22% of units. Base units grew 8%, though part of that is shoppers who used to buy on deal now paying full price, so the cleaner check is base velocity in comparable weeks without promotion. The category is growing faster than we are. It grew 5% to our 2%. Our working hypothesis is that the competitor's heavily promoted launch picked up the deal shoppers we stopped chasing, and we'll confirm it with competitor share by retailer. The decision to ask for \"Hold the lower promotion plan for two more months, and approve one display event at our two biggest retailers next month to defend share while the competitor's launch is still promoting. I'll own it with our Sales lead, and we'll bring base velocity by retailer to the next review before deciding on a full quarter.\" The pattern Headline: what happened + why + so what + what you want. Then two or three lines of evidence, each tied to a driver. Then one decision with an owner and a date. If a number doesn't support the headline, it belongs in the appendix. Practice twist Same month, but base units fell 4% instead of growing 8%. Rewrite the headline. Answer: something like \"Lumen lost 0.3 share points and our base business fell 4%, so the problem isn't just lower promotion; we need to find out why fewer shoppers are buying at full price before we decide on spend.\" The headline changes because the diagnosis changes."}, {"id": "bm/trade-pricing/trade-and-promotion-economics", "text": "In the second quarter of 2026 (April to June), two Hefty businesses inside Reynolds Consumer Products went opposite ways on promotion. Hefty Home & Tableware ran fewer deals. Its retail volume fell 14% and its revenue fell $25 million, mostly because foam products were declining, yet its adjusted EBITDA (a common measure of operating profit) rose $8 million. Reynolds credited manufacturing efficiencies for the gain and said less promotion offset the profit lost on lower volume. Hefty Storage & Organization went the other way. Its retail volume rose 8% on strong food bag sales, but its adjusted EBITDA fell $3 million, mainly from the cost of ramping up new business and promoting behind new distribution. Fewer deals, less volume, more profit. More deals, more volume, less profit. Promotion wasn't the only thing moving either result, but it showed up in both. To see how both can make sense, follow the money a brand spends through retailers, then learn the one calculation that tells you whether a promotion paid for itself. Words to know Trade spend is the money a brand pays retailers to cut prices, feature and display its products, and stock new items. These are the terms you'll hear in any trade conversation: Term What it means A quick example Trade promotion A short-term discount or in-store event the brand pays for at a retailer Two weeks at a lower price, plus an end-of-aisle display TPR (temporary price reduction) A short-term cut in the shelf price $4.49 down to $3.49 for two weeks Feature The product in the retailer's weekly ad, app or email, usually with its price \"This week: $3.49\" in the circular Display Extra space outside the normal shelf An end-of-aisle stack or a pallet up front Slotting fee A one-time payment to get a new item onto a retailer's shelf or into its warehouse A fee to launch a new flavor Co-op advertising Brand money that helps pay for the retailer's ads featuring the brand The brand pays for part of a grocery chain's ad Off-invoice deal A discount on every unit the retailer buys during the deal period 40 cents off each unit ordered in March Scan-down (scan-based deal) A discount the brand pays on each unit that scans at the register during the deal 40 cents for each unit sold March 1 to 14 Baseline What you'd have sold with no promotion 1,000 units a week Incremental units The extra units a promotion adds above baseline 600 more units during the deal Subsidized units Units sold on deal that shoppers would have bought anyway at full price Your usual 1,000 a week, now discounted Post-promotion dip A drop after the deal while shoppers use up what they stocked Sales below baseline for two weeks Forward buying A retailer buying extra at the deal price to sell later at full price Ordering ten weeks of stock during a two-week deal Breakeven lift How much sales must rise during a deal just to match what you'd have earned without it \"We need sales to double to break even\" Where the money goes Trade spend doesn't show up where you might expect. Kimberly-Clark's annual report lists its trade promotion programs as slotting fees, cooperative marketing, temporary price reductions and other activities its retail customers run, and it books their cost as a reduction in sales revenue, not as a marketing expense. That accounting choice matters when you read results. Trade spend comes out before net sales, so when a brand cuts promotion, the saving shows up as better price/mix, and when it promotes harder, its net price falls even if its list price never moved. It's a lot of money. In 2019, McKinsey reported that consumer-goods companies worldwide put about 20% of their revenue into trade promotion each year and, citing 2016 Nielsen research, that 59% of promotions lost money, or 72% in the United States. Those figures are several years old, so treat them as a warning sign rather than a current benchmark. What a promotion bump is made of When a deal starts, sales jump. The question that matters is where the extra units came from. A classic study of store scanner data split the bump into three parts: shoppers switching from other brands, purchases borrowed from other weeks, and genuinely new buying in the category. Across four datasets, each part averaged about a third. Which third is which depends on whose money it is. The switching third is real growth for your brand, but not for the retailer, whose category didn't grow. The borrowed third is nobody's growth: those shoppers would have bought later at full price. The last third grows the category, which is what the retailer's buyer is judged on. Borrowing is easy to miss. Circana warns that a promotion showing a 12% bump during the deal can add nothing to the year once the slump afterward is counted. It depends on the category: promote a drink and people tend to drink more, but promote laundry detergent and shoppers stock up at home and wait longer to buy again. Retailers see the same leak from their side. A study of CVS's 2003 promotions found that about 45% of the gross sales lift was incremental for CVS. A field test then indicated that dropping promotions chainwide in 15 of the worst-performing categories would cut sales by about $7.8 million but raise profit by about $52.6 million. The breakeven math An illustration with made-up numbers. You sell a jar of pasta sauce to a grocery chain for $3.00. After making and shipping it, you earn $1.00 a jar. The chain normally sells 2,000 jars a week. It offers a one-week TPR, and you agree to fund $0.40 off every jar sold during the deal through a scan-down. Line Math Result What you earn per jar on deal $1.00 − $0.40 $0.60 What you'd earn without the deal 2,000 × $1.00 $2,000 Jars you must sell on deal to match it $2,000 ÷ $0.60 about 3,333 Breakeven lift 3,333 ÷ 2,000 about 1.67 times normal sales, or +67% The formula: breakeven units on deal = (normal units × normal earnings per unit + any fixed costs + profit lost in the dip afterward) ÷ earnings per unit on deal. With no fees and n"}, {"id": "bm/trade-pricing/pricing-power-and-price-pack-architecture", "text": "In 2023, Coca-Cola and Kellanova, the maker of Pringles and Cheez-It, both pushed prices up in North America. Coca-Cola's North American price and mix added 8 points of revenue growth, and its volume fell 1%. Kellanova's North American pricing and mix rose 10.1%, its volume fell 6.1%, and it pointed to rising price elasticity. Similar price moves, very different reactions. Coca-Cola's shoppers barely changed what they bought. Kellanova's bought noticeably less. The number that describes that difference is price elasticity, and every pricing decision turns on it. Treat the comparison as a puzzle, not a measurement. Price/mix includes selling more of a company's pricier packs and channels, not just higher list prices, and volume moves for other reasons too, such as retailers cutting inventory or a brand losing shelf space. Words to know Term What it means A quick example Price elasticity The % change in units sold divided by the % change in price −1.5: a 10% price rise cuts units about 15% Elastic vs. inelastic Elastic: units move by a bigger % than price (beyond −1). Inelastic: units move by a smaller % (between 0 and −1) A snack with many rivals vs. a must-have medicine Pricing power Raising your price and losing fewer buyers than competitors would Your loyal buyers stay while a rival's switch List price What the brand charges retailers before any discounts $30 a case Net price What the brand actually keeps after trade spend $26 a case after promotions Price gap How much more you cost than the alternative, often the store brand A dollar more per box Price point The shelf price a shopper sees, often a round or familiar number $0.99, $4.99 Opening price point The cheapest option in a category at that store The store brand's smallest box Price per unit Price divided by ounces, counts or loads, so packs can be compared 25 cents an ounce Price-pack architecture (PPA) The full ladder of sizes and prices a brand offers Single, 6-pack, 12-pack, club 24-pack Shrinkflation A smaller pack at the same price 16 ounces becomes 14.5, still $3.99 Skimpflation A cheaper recipe at the same price Less of the expensive ingredient Revenue growth management (RGM) Managing price, packs, mix, promotions and trade terms together A team that plans price and pack changes as one The elasticity math OpenStax defines demand elasticity as the percent change in quantity demanded divided by the percent change in price, and notes that when buyers can easily switch to a different product, demand is more elastic. An illustration with made-up numbers. Your coffee sells 100,000 bags a year. Retailers pay you $10 a bag, and you earn $3 a bag after costs. You raise your price 10%, to $11, and your elasticity is about −1.5. Line Before After Price per bag $10 $11 Bags sold 100,000 85,000 (10% × −1.5 = −15%) Revenue $1,000,000 $935,000 Earned per bag $3 $4 Profit $300,000 $340,000 The formula: units after a price change ≈ units before × (1 + elasticity × price change). Here, 100,000 × (1 − 1.5 × 0.10) = 85,000. Why profit rose while revenue fell: the extra $1 a bag goes straight to profit, so you earn a third more on every bag you still sell. You lost 15% of your bags, less than the third you gained on each one. The common mistake: judging a price change by revenue or units. Revenue fell 6.5% here, and the move still paid. Elasticity isn't fixed, either. It tends to rise when competitors hold their prices, when shoppers are squeezed and when a cheaper substitute sits right beside you. Brand elasticity vs. category elasticity Your brand's elasticity isn't the category's. In a price war Kantar analyzed for a consumer-goods leader in Mexico, category demand was inelastic, at −0.8, while the average brand's demand was elastic, at −1.37. Cutting prices mostly turned into a game of winning and losing share, which did little for the category or for profits. The reason is switching. Your brand elasticity is high because shoppers move between brands when one gets cheaper. When a rival matches your cut, those shoppers move back, and you're left with the category's much smaller response and a lower margin on everything you sell. Where pricing power comes from Here's the same puzzle inside one company. In the quarter ended October 31, 2025, higher net prices added 27 percentage points to J.M. Smucker's U.S. coffee sales, while lower volume and mix took away 6 points, mainly from Folgers and Dunkin'. Café Bustelo grew anyway. Smucker doesn't explain why Café Bustelo held up, so you'd ask the questions that predict pricing power. Do its buyers have close substitutes? Is it still adding new households? Did its price gap to rivals stay small after the increase? In general, pricing power comes from a few places: Few close substitutes, in shoppers' eyes, not just on the shelf. Being the brand people want, not just one they know, so a higher price feels worth it. Small dollar stakes, where a 10% increase is a few cents and habit carries the purchase. Price-pack architecture: change the pack, not just the price A list-price increase is the most visible move you can make. McKinsey notes that a 10% increase in the suggested retail price hits shoppers harder than raising net price the same amount through smarter promotions, more premium items or better trade terms with retailers, and that pack-size changes are also noticed somewhat less. That's where price-pack architecture comes in: a ladder of sizes and prices designed so each kind of shopper finds a rung that fits. In North America, Coca-Cola's mini-can volume grew high single digits in early 2026 after it launched single-serve mini-cans in convenience stores. Retailers build ladders too. Walmart's annual report lists focusing on opening price points in some food categories, and on private label brands and smaller pack sizes, among the ways it keeps prices low. The store brand often holds the bottom rung, so your packs have to earn their place against it. An illustration with made-up numbers. Pack "}, {"id": "bm/trade-pricing/routes-to-market-and-channels", "text": "Keurig Dr Pepper runs a U.S. fleet of about 8,100 vehicles. It sells its drinks by shipping to retailers' warehouses and through its own direct store delivery network, or DSD, which takes drinks straight to stores. KDP says DSD keeps its products available, cuts transport costs and gives it more control over the timing of new launches. In April 2026, it bought JDE Peet's, the owner of Peet's Coffee, and it plans to put its coffee business and JDE Peet's together in one of two separate public companies. Peet's had just gone the other way. In February 2026, it said it was moving its U.S. commercial deliveries off DSD to what it called \"an asset-light direct central distribution model,\" with the rest of the switch due by June. Kenvue, the Zyrtec maker that Kimberly-Clark bought in November 2026, describes no delivery fleet at all in its last annual report. It says most of its distribution centers are run by third-party operators, and that it delivers to distributors, customers and consumers. One owner, two routes: its own trucks for many of its drinks, central distribution for Peet's coffee. A drug brand with no trucks at all. The reason isn't the size of the company. It's the product, and the stores it sells through. Words to know Term What it means A quick example Route to market How a product gets from the factory to the shelf, and who sells it to the store Your own trucks, the retailer's warehouse or a distributor DSD (direct store delivery) The brand's own trucks, or distributors working for it, deliver to each store and often stock the shelf A soda driver filling the cooler Warehouse delivery The brand ships to the retailer's warehouse, and the retailer moves the product to its stores. Also called warehouse direct, or direct-to-warehouse (DTW) A truckload of allergy tablets to a chain's distribution center Distributor A middleman that buys from many brands and sells to many stores A wholesaler serving independent grocers Broker A sales agent that sells to retailers on a brand's behalf, for a commission A broker pitching your new flavor to a regional chain Drop size How much product a truck leaves at one store on one stop Two cases at a gas station vs. a pallet at a supercenter Shelf life How long a product stays sellable Days for fresh bread, weeks for packaged snack cakes Channel A type of store, or a way of buying Grocery, mass, club, drug, dollar, convenience, online Club store A membership store with few items, sold in big packs Costco, Sam's Club DTC (direct to consumer) The brand sells to shoppers itself A coffee subscription on the brand's website Quick commerce Delivery of everyday items in about an hour or less Allergy tablets during an allergy attack What DSD buys, and what it costs DSD is more than a truck. Flowers Foods, which delivers fresh bread through independent distributors, lists their jobs: ordering products, selling to stores, stocking shelves, keeping up special displays, looking after customer relationships, keeping enough inventory on hand and removing unsold goods. Utz, the snack maker, says its DSD system helps it win distribution and shelf space, run in-store merchandising, keep products fresh and test new items quickly in stores. That service has a price. Flowers paid its independent distributors fees equal to 11.8% of its total sales in its 2025 fiscal year. Some of its products go through warehouses instead, so the fees take an even bigger bite out of each DSD sale. When warehouse delivery wins Hostess built its business the other way. In its 2022 annual report, it said its direct-to-warehouse model, which delivers to customers' warehouses by common carrier, was made possible by technology that extends its products' shelf life. It also said DSD can be inefficient for convenience, drug and dollar stores, where each stop drops off a small amount. Big retailers often prefer it too. Utz says some of its national mass, club and grocery customers prefer warehouse delivery because they have the scale to run distribution and restocking for their own stores. Put it together and a pattern appears. These are tendencies, not laws: DSD tends to pay when... Warehouse delivery tends to pay when... The product goes stale fast and unsold stock has to be pulled The product keeps for weeks or months It's heavy or bulky for what it's worth, like cases of soda It's small and valuable for its size, like allergy tablets It sells fast and needs coolers, displays and frequent restocking The shelf stays full between restocks You need a presence in many small stores Most sales go through big chains with their own warehouses You launch and test new items often Launches are rarer Now run the three products through it. Soft drinks are heavy, sell fast and live in coolers and displays, so DSD earns its cost. Coffee and coffee pods keep for months and are worth a lot per case, so a retailer's warehouse can handle them cheaply, and Peet's gave \"asset-light\" as its reason. Zyrtec tablets are tiny, valuable and long-lasting, and they sell through chains that run their own distribution centers, so a fleet would add little. KDP's filings don't say how its own coffee reaches stores, so treat the coffee half of the answer as a reasoned guess. Borrowing someone else's trucks You don't have to own DSD to use it. Celsius moved its Alani Nu energy drinks into PepsiCo's DSD network and named wider distribution through that network as a driver of Alani Nu's growth. The cost showed up too. A bigger share of DSD sales brought higher trade spending and billbacks that cut reported revenue, and Celsius's third-quarter gross margin fell to 46.8% from 51.3%, which it put down to costlier aluminum and fuel as well as more DSD sales. A small, fast-growing brand gains reach it couldn't build alone, and pays for it in margin. Whether that's worth it depends on how much faster the brand grows with the extra stores. Channels: same product, different shopper jobs Route to market is how the product gets to the sto"}, {"id": "bm/trade-pricing/selling-in-to-retailers", "text": "Two retailers rearranged a shelf and got opposite results. At Carrefour, Colgate, the chain's oral-care category captain, recommended putting toothbrushes above toothpaste, and Carrefour's oral-care sales rose 6% to 16%, according to a 2009 Vanderbilt University study reported by ISB. At Kroger, the retailer moved big-pack cereal to eye level to drive big-pack sales. When Kellogg helped Kroger study the results, big packs were growing but the total cereal category was shrinking, partly because the new households it drew spent less than the households it lost. One change grew the whole aisle. The other grew the part that moved and shrank the rest. A retail buyer runs the whole aisle, so that's the test every pitch you make has to pass. Words to know Term What it means A quick example Key account One of your biggest retail customers, served by its own team A national grocery or mass chain Buyer (merchant) The retailer's decision-maker for a category: what's on the shelf, at what cost and with what support The snack bar buyer Category management Running a category like its own business: assortment, price, promotion and shelf space Deciding how much space bite-size bars get Category captain A supplier the retailer asks to advise on the whole category, usually a leader in it Colgate advising Carrefour on oral care Line review The retailer's scheduled review of which items stay, go or get added The yearly cereal review Reset Rebuilding the shelf after a line review New items in, slow ones out Planogram The map of exactly what goes where on the shelf Five facings of your item on the second shelf Sell-in Getting the retailer to stock and support your product Winning two new slots Sell story Your pitch showing the buyer how your item grows their category A one-page case with the math JBP (joint business plan) A yearly plan you and a retailer build together, with shared targets Growth goals, promotions and media for the year Retail media Ads a retailer sells on its site, app and stores, aimed with its shopper data Sponsored results in the retailer's app Who you're selling to The buyer is judged on the category, not on your brand. Kantar's 2026 PoweRanking study, based on interviews with hundreds of retailers and manufacturers, found that Delivering Growth & Profitability has been retailers' top measure of their suppliers for several years. The suppliers Kantar calls Total Value Creators are now also judged on growth across the retailer's whole business, from stores and online to retail media and loyalty. Category sales, in Kantar's words, are \"the ante to play\". Big retailers also hold the power. Walmart made up about 29% of WK Kellogg Co's net sales in 2024 and its top five customers about 52%, and the cereal maker warned that large retailers use their size to push for lower prices and more promotions paid for by suppliers. That's why brands give big customers their own teams. Reynolds Consumer Products, the maker of Hefty and Reynolds Wrap, gives each of its top customers a dedicated support team covering category management, production planning, demand planning and transportation planning, plus its own customer service representatives. Category captains: helpful, and not neutral Retailers often ask one supplier, called a category captain, to advise on the whole category. ISB describes the captain as generally the retailer's best-performing supplier in that category, one that shares consumer data and insight to grow it, and notes the catch: captains may naturally favor their own brands. U.S. Federal Trade Commission staff flagged category captains as a competition issue in 2001, recommending that any inquiries into them focus on cases that could shut out rivals or involve collusion. Where a supplier stands shapes its advice. Reynolds says it holds category-captain-level advisory roles with retailers, that its retail partners generally measure success by category growth, and that store brands made up 39% of its 2025 revenue, excluding sales to other businesses. A supplier that profits whichever label the shopper picks can push for category growth with less conflict than a brand-only supplier, whose rival on the shelf is often the retailer's own brand. The shelf has to be re-earned Winning a slot isn't the end. NIQ reports that many major retailers now refresh their assortments on 12-week cycles, while 67% of marketers wait 12 to 15 weeks to judge a launch, missing the retailer's decision window. WK Kellogg warned that even products that win shelf space can be pulled if they miss the sales expectations retailers set. The number that decides it is velocity, sales per store or per point of distribution, which Circana calls a core measure for assortment decisions and resets. What makes a buyer say yes A peer-reviewed study of 392 new-product decisions by buyers at a Dutch food retailer found that buyers were more likely to take on a new item when they expected it to grow the category, and when its relative gross margin was higher. Put that together with what buyers are judged on, and a sell story has one job: show the buyer how your item makes their category bigger and more profitable, in their numbers. A strong one fits on a page and follows this order: Headline: what the buyer gains, in the buyer's terms. The gap: where their category trails the market or misses a trend. Proof: how fast your item sells, the new households it brings and how much of its volume is new to the category. The math: what your item adds compared with what it replaces. The ask: which slots, in which reset, on what terms. Your support: sampling, retail media, a launch promotion. The risk: the objection you expect, and your answer. The math every buyer redoes An illustration with made-up numbers. You want one slot in a chain's salsa set for a new roasted-tomato salsa. At similar chains, it sells $150 per store per week. The item it would replace sells $60. The chain has 250 stores and earns a 30% margin on the aisle. Line Math"}, {"id": "bm/trade-pricing/the-25-off-math", "text": "Hypothetical scenario. The brand and all numbers are made up to show the work. The facts. You manage Sunny Grove orange juice. A grocery chain's buyer offers a promotion: Normal economics: the 52-ounce carton sells for $4.00 on shelf. The chain pays you $3.00, and it costs you $1.80 to make and ship, so you earn $1.20 a carton. The deal: 25% off for two weeks, so the shelf price drops to $3.00. You fund $0.75 of every carton sold on deal through a scan-down payment, and the chain gives up the other $0.25 from its own margin. A display: the chain also asks for a $15,000 fee to put Sunny Grove on an end-of-aisle display during the deal. Normal sales at this chain are 10,000 cartons a week. Past events like this one tripled sales during the deal weeks, to 30,000 a week, then sales dipped 15% for the two weeks after. Your task: Does this event make money? What lift would it need to break even? What would you negotiate before saying yes? +++ hints - Compare two worlds over the same weeks: one with the promotion and one without it. - Work out what you earn per carton on deal. Every carton sold on deal earns less, including the 10,000 a week you'd have sold anyway. - To find breakeven, add up everything the event has to cover (what you'd have earned anyway, the display fee and the dip), then divide by what you earn per carton on deal. +++ outline Earned per carton normally: . On deal: ___ − ___ = . Without the promotion, two weeks: ___ cartons × ___ = ___ With the promotion, two weeks: ___ cartons × ___ = ___ Display fee: ___ The dip after, two weeks: ___ fewer cartons × ___ = ___ Net effect of the event: ___ Breakeven cartons on deal: ( + ___ + ) ÷ ___ = ___, which is ___ times normal weekly sales What I'd negotiate: ___ +++ reveal The event, line by line Line Math Result Earned per carton on deal $1.20 − $0.75 $0.45 With the promotion, two weeks 60,000 × $0.45 $27,000 Without it, the same two weeks 20,000 × $1.20 −$24,000 (what you give up) Display fee −$15,000 The dip after, two weeks 2 × 1,500 fewer cartons × $1.20 −$3,600 Net effect of the event −$15,600 Tripling sales sounds like a hit. It still loses $15,600. The general formula, and why it works Breakeven units on deal = (what you'd have earned without the deal + fixed costs + the dip) ÷ what you earn per unit on deal. Here: ($24,000 + $15,000 + $3,600) ÷ $0.45 ≈ 94,700 cartons over two weeks, or about 47,300 a week. That's about 4.7 times normal sales, against the 3 times you expect. Without the fee and the dip, the bare breakeven lift is just the ratio of the two margins: $1.20 ÷ $0.45 ≈ 2.7 times normal sales. Why: on deal, every carton earns less, including the 10,000 a week shoppers would have bought at full price. Those cartons are subsidized. The extra cartons have to earn back that lost margin before they earn anything else, and then pay the fees too. The common mistake: judging the event on the lift (+200%) or on total sales during the deal, instead of on what you earn compared with doing nothing. What you'd negotiate Drop or cut the display fee, or ask for it to be tied to a second event. On its own, the fee is most of the loss. Ask for a shallower discount. At 15% off, you might give up less per carton than the extra cartons earn. Pick a better week, such as one when the category is busy anyway, or when a competitor isn't promoting. Ask for the chain's data on how many deal buyers are new to the brand. A promotion that brings in new households can be worth more than its two weeks of numbers. What interviewers push on \"But the buyer says the display will drive the lift.\" Then ask for the lift with and without a display from past events, and pay only for the difference it makes. \"Isn't a loss fine if it builds the brand?\" Sometimes, if it recruits new buyers who come back. Say what you'd measure to prove it, such as the repeat rate of new buyers from the event. Practice twist The chain waives the display fee in exchange for a second event later in the year. Does this event make money now? Answer: −$15,600 + $15,000 = −$600, so it's about breakeven. You'd need about 61,300 cartons over two weeks to break even (($24,000 + $3,600) ÷ $0.45), against the 60,000 you expect. The dip now decides it, so check whether past events really showed one."}, {"id": "bm/trade-pricing/take-the-price-increase-or-not", "text": "Hypothetical scenario. The brand and all numbers are made up to show the work. The facts. You manage Crunchwell crackers, an 8-ounce box: Today: retailers pay you $3.00 a box, and it costs you $1.80 to make and ship, so you earn $1.20 a box. You sell 1,000,000 boxes a year. The problem: wheat and packaging costs are rising $0.20 a box, which cuts your earnings to $1.00 a box. Option A, raise the price 8%, to $3.24 a box. Your past price increases suggest an elasticity of about −1.2 if your main competitor raises its price too, and about −2.5 if it doesn't. Option B, hold the price and absorb the cost. Option C, shrink the box to 7 ounces at the same $3.00. After the cost increase, the smaller box costs you $1.75 to make and ship. You expect about 3% fewer boxes sold, and some shoppers will notice. Your task: Recommend one option, with the numbers behind it, and say what would change your mind. +++ hints - Start with what holding the price costs you: work out profit per box before and after the cost increase. - For the price increase, work out how many boxes you could lose before you'd be worse off than holding the price. Then compare that with what elasticity predicts. - The smaller box earns more per box. Ask what shoppers and retailers will think, and what you'll do the next time costs rise. +++ outline Profit per box: before ; after the cost increase . Hold the price: ___ boxes × ___ = ___ Raise 8%: new price , profit per box , boxes , profit ___ Volume I can lose before the increase hurts: ___ ÷ ( + ) = % If the competitor doesn't follow: boxes , profit ___ Smaller box: profit per box , boxes , profit ___ My recommendation: , because . I'd change my mind if . +++ reveal The three options Option Profit per box Boxes Profit Before the cost increase $1.20 1,000,000 $1.20M B. Hold the price $1.00 1,000,000 $1.00M A. Raise 8%, competitor follows (−1.2) $1.24 904,000 about $1.12M A. Raise 8%, competitor holds (−2.5) $1.24 800,000 about $0.99M C. Smaller box $1.25 970,000 about $1.21M The formulas, and why they work Units after a price change ≈ units before × (1 + elasticity × price change). With −1.2 and +8%: 1,000,000 × (1 − 0.096) = about 904,000 boxes. Volume you can lose before a price increase hurts = the price increase ÷ (old profit per box + the price increase). Here: $0.24 ÷ ($1.00 + $0.24) ≈ 19%. Why: you break even when (1 − lost share) × new profit per box = old profit per box. Solve for the lost share and you get the increase divided by the new profit per box. Reading it: with the competitor following, you lose about 10% of boxes, well inside the 19% cushion, so the increase pays. If the competitor holds and you lose 20%, you're slightly worse off than holding the price. A strong recommendation \"Take the 8% increase, but only with a plan for the competitor. If they follow, we earn about $120,000 more than holding the price. If they don't, we land about where holding leaves us, so I'd watch weekly share for six to eight weeks and be ready to fund a promotion if our price gap widens too far. I wouldn't shrink the box: it looks best on paper, but it uses up shoppers' trust, retailers may push back, and it doesn't solve the next cost increase.\" Why the smaller box is a trap, even when it wins on paper It's a bigger price increase than it looks. Going from 8 ounces to 7 at the same price raises the price per ounce by about 14.3% (8 ÷ 7 ≈ 1.143), almost twice Option A's 8%. It wins on paper only because you assumed just 3% fewer boxes sold. Shoppers notice, and the story of a smaller box at the same price can do more damage than an honest price increase. It only works once. Next year you face the same problem with a box that's already smaller. There's a better use of pack size: add a new size at a new price point, such as a family box, so shoppers choose what they pay instead of discovering it later. Practice twist The competitor raises its price 5%, not 8%, so your price gap widens a little. You estimate your elasticity at −1.8 in that case. What happens? Answer: units fall about 14.4% (1.8 × 8%), to about 856,000 boxes, for profit of about $1.06M. That's still above holding the price at $1.00M, because 14% is inside the 19% cushion, but the margin of safety is thin."}, {"id": "bm/trade-pricing/which-channel-which-pack", "text": "Hypothetical scenario. The brand and all numbers are made up to show the work. The facts. You manage ClearDay, a 24-hour allergy tablet sold mostly in pharmacies. Three channels want something new: Pharmacy today: a 30-count box at $24.99. A warehouse club wants a 120-count pack at $39.99. It sells its own store-brand allergy tablets in the same aisle. Amazon wants a 70-count pack at $44.99, with a subscribe-and-save option. A grocery delivery app wants a 5-count \"rescue\" pack at $8.99, for shoppers who need relief within the hour. Your task: Decide which pack goes where and at what price. For each channel, name the shopper's job and the biggest risk. Then say how you'd keep the channels from undercutting each other. +++ hints - Start with the shopper. In each channel, are they stocking up, refilling on a schedule or fixing an allergy attack right now? - Work out the price per tablet for every pack. Where is the cheapest tablet, and who could switch to it? - For each channel, name one risk: a partner who loses sales, a store brand next to you, or a price that resets what shoppers think the brand is worth. +++ outline Price per tablet: 30-count ; 120-count ; 70-count ; 5-count ___ Club: pack , price . Shopper's job: . Risk: ___ Amazon: pack , price . Shopper's job: . Risk: ___ Delivery app: pack , price . Shopper's job: . Risk: ___ Pharmacy, what I protect: ___ How I keep the channels from undercutting each other: ___ +++ reveal Price per tablet first Channel Pack Price Per tablet Pharmacy 30-count $24.99 $0.83 Amazon 70-count $44.99 $0.64 Warehouse club 120-count $39.99 $0.33 Delivery app 5-count $8.99 $1.80 The formula: price per tablet = pack price ÷ tablets. Always compare packs this way. Shoppers do, and so do retail buyers. Channel by channel Warehouse club: stocking up. The 120-count is a different pack from anything in pharmacy, which helps, but at $0.33 a tablet it's less than half the pharmacy price. Risk: your heaviest pharmacy buyers move to the club, and the club's store brand sits right next to you at an even lower price. Consider a slightly smaller pack or a higher price, so the gap to pharmacy is clear but not dramatic. Amazon: refilling on a schedule. A 70-count with subscribe-and-save suits people who take a tablet every day of allergy season. Risk: subscription discounts quietly push the price per tablet down further, and every other channel's buyer will see it. Delivery app: fixing it right now. A 5-count at $1.80 a tablet is expensive, but this shopper is paying for speed, not quantity. Risk: low, as long as it stays small. It carries a high price per tablet and brings in people having a bad allergy day. Pharmacy: what you protect. The 30-count is your anchor price. Keep it out of deep promotions, and give pharmacy partners something the others don't get, such as a pharmacist-recommended display or first access to a new formula. How to keep channels from undercutting each other Retailers set their own shelf prices. You steer through the pack, what you charge each retailer and your suggested price. Make each channel's pack different, so shoppers can't compare like for like and retailers aren't fighting over the same item. Build a per-tablet price ladder on purpose: biggest pack cheapest per tablet, smallest most expensive, with steady gaps between them. Watch the most visible price. Online prices are the easiest for every buyer to check, so changes there travel fastest. What interviewers push on \"Why not just say no to the club?\" Because club shoppers may not be your pharmacy shoppers at all. If the club brings in buyers you can't reach otherwise, the pack and price just need to limit switching, not prevent the sale. \"How would you know if the club pack is stealing from pharmacy?\" Track pharmacy sales in areas near the club's stores against areas without one, and check whether pharmacy buyers in the household panel start showing up at the club. Practice twist The club offers to take a 90-count pack at $36.99 instead. What's the per-tablet price, and is it better for you? Answer: $36.99 ÷ 90 ≈ $0.41 a tablet. That's closer to the Amazon price and narrows the gap to pharmacy, so it limits switching. Whether it's better depends on whether club shoppers still see it as a good deal next to the store brand."}, {"id": "bm/trade-pricing/write-the-sell-story", "text": "Hypothetical scenario. The brand and all numbers are made up to show the work. The facts. You're pitching a grocery chain's buyer at the yearly review of the nutrition bar aisle. You want two slots for Summit Oats Protein Bites, a new item. Your data: The category grew 6% at this chain over the past year, against 9% nationally. Bite-size bars, a newer segment, grew 22% nationally, but this chain carries only two bite items. Your existing Summit Oats bars sell 15% faster per store than the average bar at this chain. At three other chains that launched the Bites, about 40% of buyers were new to the brand, and about 25% hadn't bought any nutrition bar at that chain in the year before. The two items you'd replace are in the bottom 10% of the aisle for sales per store. Together they sell about $90 per store per week. Based on the other chains, your two Bites should sell about $210 per store per week. The chain has 400 stores and earns a 35% margin on the aisle. Your support: $40,000 for in-store sampling and $25,000 of ads on the chain's own website and app. Your task: Write a one-page sell story: a headline, three proof points, the math for the buyer, your ask, and the risk the buyer will raise, with your answer to it. +++ hints - Write for the buyer, not for your brand. What is the buyer judged on? Category sales, margin and shopper trips. - Find the gap in the data: where is this chain behind the national trend, and how does your item help close it? - End with a specific ask and the support behind it: which slots, when, and what you'll spend to make it work. +++ outline Headline (what the buyer gains): ___ Proof point 1, the gap: ___ Proof point 2, our speed on shelf: ___ Proof point 3, new shoppers for the aisle: ___ The math for the buyer: ___ per store per week × ___ stores × 52 = ___ in sales, and ___ in margin at ___% The ask: ___ Our support: ___ The risk the buyer will raise, and my answer: ___ +++ reveal A strong one-pager Headline: \"Two bite-size slots can help close a 3-point growth gap in your bar aisle and add up to about $2.5 million in yearly sales across your 400 stores.\" Proof points: The gap: your bar aisle grew 6% last year against 9% nationally, and the fastest-growing segment nationally, bites at +22%, has just two items on your shelf. Speed on shelf: Summit Oats bars already sell 15% faster per store than your average bar, so shoppers here know and buy the brand. New shoppers for the aisle: at three chains that launched the Bites, about a quarter of buyers hadn't bought a bar there in the year before, so part of the gain is new to the aisle, not just switching between brands. About 40% were new to Summit Oats, so the Bites won't just move sales from one of our items to another. The math for the buyer: The formula: extra sales = (new items' sales − replaced items' sales) per store per week × stores × 52 weeks. Here: ($210 − $90) × 400 × 52 = about $2.5 million a year in sales, or about $874,000 of margin at your 35%. Why it works this way: the buyer's shelf is fixed, so they judge a new item by what it earns compared with what it replaces, not by its own total. The catch: this assumes the Bites' extra sales don't come out of other bars that stay on the shelf. Some will, so treat $2.5 million as the top of the range. The quarter of buyers who are new to the aisle is the part the buyer can count on most. The ask: two slots in the spring reset, replacing the two bottom-10% items. Our support: $40,000 of in-store sampling in the first eight weeks, and $25,000 of ads on your website and app aimed at bar buyers who don't buy bites yet. The risk you'll hear, and your answer: \"What if it just takes sales from your own bars?\" Answer: \"At the other chains, about 40% of Bites buyers were new to our brand and about a quarter were new to the bar aisle. We'll review your loyalty-card data with you at 12 weeks, and if the Bites aren't adding to the aisle, we'll take the slots back ourselves.\" Why this works It leads with the buyer's problem, the growth gap, not your product. Every proof point is a number the buyer can check. The ask is specific, and the support and the exit plan lower the buyer's risk. What interviewers push on \"Your $210 comes from other chains. Why would it hold here?\" Show that those chains look like this one in size, shoppers and price, and offer a test in a subset of stores if the buyer wants proof first. \"What if the buyer wants a promotion to launch it?\" Say what you'd fund and why: a launch price to drive trial makes sense; a deep, permanent discount doesn't. Practice twist The buyer offers one slot instead of two, replacing the weaker of the two items ($40 per store per week). Your stronger Bite should sell $120 per store per week. What's the yearly gain? Answer: ($120 − $40) × 400 × 52 = $1,664,000 in sales, about $582,000 of margin at 35%. One slot still delivers two-thirds of the total gain, so it's worth taking as a first step."}];
